946f23c5-2802-4934-81c5-488649b40023	2cd91fc5-05da-44a2-bbd4-251061cabbad	How can you shift the bits of a binary number to the left and right?	You can shift the bits of a binary number to the left using the left shift operator (<<) and to the right using the right shift operator (>>).	\N	\N	EASY	["bit-shifting", "bit-manipulation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:52:16.811935	0
51bc7442-a3f1-4bb9-8c41-77b27dfeceef	2cd91fc5-05da-44a2-bbd4-251061cabbad	What is the difference between signed and unsigned right shift operators in C++?	The signed right shift operator (>>) performs a logical shift, while the unsigned right shift operator (>>) performs an arithmetic shift. This means that the signed operator will fill the leftmost bits with the sign bit, while the unsigned operator will fill the leftmost bits with zeros.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["bit-shifting", "c++"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:52:16.812162	0
a92deb63-ca4d-4738-9293-6dc93f40a828	2cd91fc5-05da-44a2-bbd4-251061cabbad	How can you check if a bit is set in a binary number using bitwise operators?	You can check if a bit is set in a binary number using the bitwise AND operator (&) and the bitwise NOT operator (~). The expression (~x & (1 << i)) will be non-zero if the ith bit is set in x.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["bitwise-operators", "bit-manipulation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:52:16.812282	0
749f4a45-10b5-42bc-89a4-2df7f9c3caa7	2cd91fc5-05da-44a2-bbd4-251061cabbad	Design a function to swap two numbers without using a temporary variable, but instead using bitwise XOR operator.	The function can be implemented as follows: `def swap_numbers(a, b): return b ^ a, a ^ b`. This works because `a ^ b` will give the result of swapping the two numbers, and `a ^ a = 0` and `b ^ b = 0`, so `a ^ b` will give the original value of `b` and `b ^ a` will give the original value of `a`.	\N	\N	HARD	["bitwise-xor", "bit-manipulation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:52:16.812381	0
c2840405-90dc-4625-8482-a70ffff09b61	479f8f40-8201-4f92-afd9-564220b4fe35	How does a HashMap handle collisions when two keys hash to the same index?	HashMap uses a technique called chaining to handle collisions. When two keys hash to the same index, the values are stored in a linked list, and the next node in the list is accessed when a collision occurs.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["HashMap", "Chaining", "Collision"]	REVIEW	2.6	1	1	2026-07-27 01:51:59.220371	2026-07-26 00:51:54.494433	0
3bb7dc48-7f1c-4627-8ac0-583fd8705ddc	479f8f40-8201-4f92-afd9-564220b4fe35	What is the difference between HashMap and HashSet in Java?	HashMap stores key-value pairs, while HashSet stores only unique values. HashMap uses the key to store and retrieve values, while HashSet uses the hash of the value to store and retrieve.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["HashMap", "HashSet", "Java"]	REVIEW	2.36	1	1	2026-07-27 01:52:09.286214	2026-07-26 00:51:54.494328	0
5056a423-73e9-493f-a3bd-19a855502e2d	479f8f40-8201-4f92-afd9-564220b4fe35	What is the concept of load factor in a HashMap, and how does it affect performance?	The load factor is the ratio of the number of elements in the HashMap to its capacity. A high load factor can lead to poor performance due to increased collision rates and resizing operations.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["HashMap", "Load Factor", "Performance"]	REVIEW	2.36	1	1	2026-07-27 01:52:09.411734	2026-07-26 00:51:54.494507	0
346adfd4-c5f9-4ddd-80e0-45523f9a4945	a7000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Tell me about a time you had to learn a new technology quickly for a project.	STAR Framework:\n**Situation:** What technology? Why was it needed? What was the timeline?\n**Task:** What level of proficiency was required?\n**Action:** What was your learning strategy? (official docs, POC, pair with expert, courses)\n**Result:** How quickly did you become productive? What did you deliver with the new technology?\n\nKey points: Show structured learning approach and ability to become productive quickly.\nGerman context: Show Weiterbildung (continuous learning) attitude — it's valued in German tech culture.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["behavioral", "adaptability", "learning"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 01:26:49.982581	0
abf3258e-d384-4ff9-84f7-689080615d9d	a7000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Describe a time you had to adapt to a major change in project direction.	STAR Framework:\n**Situation:** What changed? (requirements pivot, reorg, technology change)\n**Task:** How did the change affect your work?\n**Action:** How did you adapt? Did you help others adapt? What did you preserve/discard?\n**Result:** Was the transition successful? How quickly did the team stabilize?\n\nKey points: Show resilience, pragmatism about sunk costs, and forward-looking mindset.\nGerman context: Show you adapted systematically — new plan, new milestones, clear communication of impact.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["behavioral", "adaptability", "change-management"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 01:26:50.000252	0
