89b5117e-8c5f-428a-bcb1-8b8df9d2ee82	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain the two-pointer technique and when to apply it.	Two pointers use two indices traversing an array (often from both ends or at different speeds) to solve problems in O(n) time without extra space. Apply when: (1) Searching pairs in sorted arrays. (2) Removing duplicates in-place. (3) Partitioning arrays (Dutch National Flag). (4) Detecting cycles (fast/slow pointers). Key insight: pointer movement eliminates portions of the search space.	// Two Sum in sorted array\nint left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;\nwhile (left < right) {\n    int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];\n    if (sum == target) return new int[]{left, right};\n    else if (sum < target) left++;\n    else right--;\n}\n	\N	EASY	["two-pointers", "arrays", "technique"]	REVIEW	2.36	1	1	2026-07-27 02:21:03.662431	2026-07-26 00:49:25.045293	1
cbb2053c-1bb4-4397-8d6c-63bd349db6d7	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Describe the DispatcherServlet request lifecycle in Spring MVC.	DispatcherServlet receives the request, consults HandlerMapping to find the controller\nmethod, invokes it via HandlerAdapter (applying interceptors pre/post), then resolves\nthe returned value through ViewResolver or HttpMessageConverter. Exception handling\noccurs via HandlerExceptionResolver if any phase throws.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["spring-mvc", "architecture", "servlet"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.400596	0
1a88c6ab-9455-48e5-b67c-69507face271	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does validation work with @Valid and BindingResult?	@Valid triggers JSR-380 Bean Validation on the annotated parameter. BindingResult\nmust immediately follow the validated parameter in the method signature. If validation\nfails and BindingResult is present, Spring populates it with errors instead of throwing\nMethodArgumentNotValidException, giving the controller manual error handling control.\n	@PostMapping("/users")\npublic ResponseEntity<?> create(\n        @Valid @RequestBody UserDto dto,\n        BindingResult result) {\n    if (result.hasErrors()) {\n        return ResponseEntity.badRequest()\n            .body(result.getFieldErrors());\n    }\n    return ResponseEntity.ok(userService.save(dto));\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["validation", "bean-validation", "error-handling"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.402736	0
41b7e75d-b1b2-4d22-9ae3-186c3ee6e5c5	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does @ControllerAdvice provide global exception handling?	@ControllerAdvice classes act as cross-cutting interceptors for all controllers.\nMethods annotated with @ExceptionHandler within them catch exceptions thrown by any\ncontroller. You can scope advice to specific packages or annotations via basePackages\nor assignableTypes attributes.\n	@RestControllerAdvice\npublic class GlobalExceptionHandler {\n    @ExceptionHandler(EntityNotFoundException.class)\n    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)\n    public ErrorResponse handleNotFound(EntityNotFoundException ex) {\n        return new ErrorResponse(ex.getMessage());\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["exception-handling", "controller-advice"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.404896	0
4c6215e5-c01a-4b2a-8ac9-0345bb2c912c	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Explain content negotiation in Spring MVC and how to configure it.	Content negotiation determines the response media type based on Accept header,\nURL suffix, or request parameter. Spring uses ContentNegotiationConfigurer to set\nstrategy priority and default type. HttpMessageConverters (Jackson for JSON,\nJAXB for XML) serialize the response to the negotiated format.\n	@Configuration\npublic class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {\n    @Override\n    public void configureContentNegotiation(\n            ContentNegotiationConfigurer configurer) {\n        configurer.defaultContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)\n            .mediaType("xml", MediaType.APPLICATION_XML);\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["content-negotiation", "configuration", "media-types"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.406823	0
6ac6cd2b-a586-421a-9b03-0a230a3037ae	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What are the ways to configure CORS in Spring Boot?	Three levels: @CrossOrigin on controller/method for granular control,\nWebMvcConfigurer.addCorsMappings() for global path-based rules, or a CorsFilter\nbean for servlet-level handling. Spring Boot also supports spring.web.cors properties.\nGlobal config is preferred for consistency across endpoints.\n	@Override\npublic void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {\n    registry.addMapping("/api/**")\n        .allowedOrigins("https://app.example.com")\n        .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE")\n        .allowCredentials(true);\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["cors", "security", "configuration"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.409067	0
1155ad41-99c5-4c9b-8dd5-35c3feca35cc	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How do @RequestMapping composed annotations work and what is their method resolution order?	@GetMapping, @PostMapping etc. are composed annotations combining @RequestMapping\nwith a specific HTTP method. Resolution narrows by path, method, params, headers,\nconsumes, and produces in that order. Ambiguous mappings throw IllegalStateException\nat startup. AntPathMatcher scores specificity when multiple patterns match at runtime.\n	@PutMapping(\n    value = "/users/{id}",\n    consumes = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE,\n    produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)\npublic User update(@PathVariable Long id,\n                   @Valid @RequestBody UserDto dto) {\n    return userService.update(id, dto);\n}\n	\N	HARD	["request-mapping", "routing", "annotations"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.410984	0
8831c9d8-1fc7-42db-8933-a145aa8dfb61	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What are the six constraints of REST architecture?	Client-Server, Stateless, Cacheable, Uniform Interface, Layered System, and Code on Demand (optional).\nStateless means each request contains all info needed; server stores no session state.\nUniform Interface is enforced via resource identification (URIs), manipulation through representations, self-descriptive messages, and HATEOAS.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["rest", "architecture"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.415313	0
1c487002-e8e9-43aa-98a8-c7ce55019f39	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Compare URI versioning, header versioning, and query param versioning for APIs. When would you choose each?	URI (/v1/users) - most visible, easy to cache, breaks when version changes. Best for public APIs.\nHeader (Accept: application/vnd.api.v2+json) - cleaner URIs, harder to test in browser. Best for internal APIs needing content negotiation.\nQuery param (/users?version=2) - easy to default, optional. Best for optional versioning on simple APIs.\nURI versioning is most common in practice due to simplicity and cacheability.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["versioning", "rest", "api-design"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.417211	0
1a8c2362-8bff-43ec-b430-bac6f816bb53	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Explain cursor-based vs offset-based pagination. Why is cursor-based preferred for large datasets?	Offset-based (LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100) is simple but breaks with inserts/deletes and degrades at high offsets (DB scans skipped rows).\nCursor-based uses an opaque token (typically encoded last-seen ID) so the query is WHERE id > :cursor LIMIT 20.\nCursor-based gives stable results under concurrent writes and constant-time DB performance regardless of page depth.\n	// Cursor-based response\n@GetMapping("/orders")\npublic Page<Order> list(@RequestParam(required = false) String cursor,\n                        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int limit) {\n    Long afterId = cursor != null ? decodeCursor(cursor) : 0L;\n    List<Order> items = repo.findByIdGreaterThanOrderById(afterId, Limit.of(limit + 1));\n    boolean hasNext = items.size() > limit;\n    if (hasNext) items = items.subList(0, limit);\n    return new Page<>(items, hasNext ? encodeCursor(items.getLast().getId()) : null);\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["pagination", "performance", "database"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.419486	0
221b2a0b-7973-4f41-988e-90a889500a86	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is a cache stampede (thundering herd) and how do you prevent it?	Cache stampede occurs when a popular cache key expires and many concurrent requests simultaneously hit the database to regenerate it. Prevention strategies: (1) Lock/mutex — only one request regenerates, others wait. (2) Early expiration — refresh before actual TTL expires (probabilistic early expiration). (3) Never expire — background thread refreshes cache, keys never truly expire. (4) Request coalescing — deduplicate identical in-flight requests.	// Probabilistic early expiration\ndouble expiry = cachedExpiry;\ndouble now = System.currentTimeMillis();\ndouble delta = computeTime; // time to regenerate\ndouble beta = 1.0; // tuning parameter\n// Recompute if: now - delta * beta * ln(random()) >= expiry\nif (now - delta * beta * Math.log(Math.random()) >= expiry) {\n    recompute();\n}\n	\N	HARD	["cache-stampede", "thundering-herd", "concurrency"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.444375	0
8e12495a-def3-4865-8946-b4624705327f	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is the Dependency Rule in Clean Architecture?	All source code dependencies must point inward, toward higher-level policies.\nInner layers cannot know anything about outer layers — no name, class, or function\nfrom an outer circle can be mentioned in an inner circle. Data crossing boundaries\nmust be in the form that is most convenient for the inner layer.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["clean-architecture", "dependency-rule"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.674941	0
c0d6dc2f-0698-4e09-8f07-123d2abfe5ad	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Name the four layers of Clean Architecture from innermost to outermost.	1) Entities — enterprise-wide business rules and objects.\n2) Use Cases — application-specific business rules orchestrating entity interactions.\n3) Interface Adapters — controllers, presenters, gateways that convert data between\n   use case format and external format (DB, web).\n4) Frameworks & Drivers — web frameworks, databases, UI, external tools.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["clean-architecture", "layers", "software-design"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.711328	0
2d4a9ee4-4d96-4ce8-a652-87c20934e42e	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does Dependency Inversion enable the Dependency Rule in practice?	Inner layers define interfaces (ports) that outer layers implement. The use case\nlayer declares a repository interface; the infrastructure layer provides the\nimplementation. At runtime, dependency injection wires the concrete class, so\nsource-code dependency points inward while control flow can go outward.\n	// Use Case layer defines the port\npublic interface OrderRepository {\n    Order findById(OrderId id);\n    void save(Order order);\n}\n\n// Infrastructure layer implements it\n@Repository\npublic class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {\n    private final SpringDataOrderRepo springRepo;\n    // delegates to JPA, depends on use case interface\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["dependency-inversion", "ports-and-adapters", "DIP"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.716455	0
9b5a4af6-4cd8-4a58-a70f-7d2b77b7368e	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is the difference between Ports and Adapters (Hexagonal) and Clean Architecture?	Hexagonal Architecture defines a core with ports (interfaces) and adapters\n(implementations) — primary/driving adapters invoke the core, secondary/driven\nadapters are invoked by the core. Clean Architecture adds explicit concentric\nlayers with stricter separation between entities and use cases. In practice\nthey are complementary: hexagonal focuses on the boundary shape, Clean Architecture\non the layering and dependency direction within the application.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["hexagonal-architecture", "ports-and-adapters", "clean-architecture"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.720362	0
0c89bc19-d67f-475f-b443-a478dfc69da9	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does Clean Architecture improve testability? Show a use case test.	Use cases depend only on interfaces, so tests substitute in-memory fakes with\nzero framework setup. No Spring context, no database, no HTTP — pure unit tests\nthat run in milliseconds. Each layer can be tested in isolation against its\nown boundary interfaces.\n	@Test\nvoid shouldPlaceOrder() {\n    // In-memory fake — no Spring, no DB\n    OrderRepository repo = new InMemoryOrderRepository();\n    PlaceOrderUseCase useCase = new PlaceOrderUseCase(repo);\n\n    OrderRequest request = new OrderRequest("SKU-1", 2);\n    OrderResponse response = useCase.execute(request);\n\n    assertThat(response.status()).isEqualTo("CONFIRMED");\n    assertThat(repo.findById(response.orderId())).isNotNull();\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["testing", "clean-architecture", "use-cases"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.72477	0
130ae4ea-0662-4587-a8ae-4b3be3b9a5c9	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How do you handle cross-cutting concerns (transactions, security) without violating the Dependency Rule?	Use decorators or aspect-oriented proxies at the interface adapter layer that\nwrap use case implementations. The use case remains unaware of transactions or\nsecurity annotations. Alternatively, define a UseCase interface and apply a\ntransactional decorator in the composition root — the domain stays pure while\ninfrastructure concerns compose around it.\n	// Composition root wires the decorator\npublic class TransactionalPlaceOrder implements PlaceOrderUseCase {\n    private final PlaceOrderUseCase delegate;\n    private final TransactionManager txManager;\n\n    @Override\n    public OrderResponse execute(OrderRequest req) {\n        return txManager.executeInTransaction(\n            () -> delegate.execute(req)\n        );\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["cross-cutting-concerns", "decorator-pattern", "clean-architecture"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.729399	0
0118c5b6-d3d0-490d-96bf-8a77db909b19	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What is Ubiquitous Language in DDD and why is it critical?	Ubiquitous Language is a shared vocabulary between developers and domain experts, used consistently in code, documentation, and conversation. It eliminates translation layers between business and technical teams. If the domain expert says 'Policy' but code says 'Contract', bugs and miscommunication follow. The language must be reflected in class names, method names, and module structure.	\N	\N	EASY	["ubiquitous-language", "communication", "ddd-fundamentals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.738444	0
e5bfed94-8bac-4f9c-a9fd-61b1919541c5	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How do you determine Bounded Context boundaries in a large domain?	Bounded Context boundaries align where the same term means different things or where teams have different models. Identify them by: (1) linguistic boundaries — same word, different meaning (e.g., 'Account' in billing vs identity), (2) team ownership boundaries, (3) differing rates of change, (4) distinct business capabilities. Each context has its own Ubiquitous Language, model, and data store.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["bounded-context", "context-boundaries", "strategic-ddd"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.742783	0
b72d33af-a89e-40a1-978a-e835613d038a	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What invariants does an Aggregate Root enforce, and what are the design rules?	An Aggregate Root is the single entry point that guards all business invariants for a cluster of related entities. Rules: (1) external objects reference only the root, never internal entities, (2) all state changes go through root methods that validate invariants, (3) one aggregate per transaction — eventual consistency between aggregates, (4) keep aggregates small to reduce contention.	public class Order { // Aggregate Root\n    private OrderId id;\n    private List<OrderLine> lines;\n    private Money totalLimit;\n\n    public void addLine(Product product, int qty) {\n        OrderLine line = new OrderLine(product, qty);\n        if (calculateTotal().add(line.cost()).exceeds(totalLimit)) {\n            throw new OrderLimitExceededException();\n        }\n        lines.add(line); // invariant enforced\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["aggregate-root", "invariants", "tactical-ddd"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.747349	0
fc25a328-7d7d-41b8-a36c-3003328d714b	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is CQRS and why is it often paired with event sourcing?	CQRS separates the write model (commands) from the read model (queries) into distinct services\nor data stores. Paired with event sourcing, events from the write side project into optimized\nread models (denormalized views), allowing each side to scale and evolve independently.\n	// Command side - handles business logic, emits events\npublic class OrderCommandHandler {\n    public void handle(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {\n        OrderAggregate order = repository.load(cmd.getOrderId());\n        order.place(cmd.getItems()); // validates and applies event\n        repository.save(order);      // persists OrderPlacedEvent\n    }\n}\n// Query side - subscribes to events, builds read model\npublic class OrderProjection {\n    @EventHandler\n    public void on(OrderPlacedEvent e) {\n        orderViewRepo.save(new OrderView(e.getOrderId(), e.getTotal(), "PLACED"));\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["cqrs", "read-write-separation", "scalability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.836598	0
d048ce82-1990-4a9d-a0e7-867e3cfc1783	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How do Value Objects differ from Entities, and when should you prefer them?	Entities have identity — two entities with identical attributes are still different if IDs differ. Value Objects have no identity — they are defined solely by their attributes and are immutable. Prefer Value Objects by default: they are simpler, thread-safe, and side-effect free. Use Entities only when you need to track lifecycle and identity (e.g., a User, an Order). Value Objects examples: Money, Address, DateRange.	// Value Object: equality by attributes, immutable\npublic record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n    public Money add(Money other) {\n        if (!currency.equals(other.currency))\n            throw new CurrencyMismatchException();\n        return new Money(amount.add(other.amount), currency);\n    }\n}\n\n// Entity: equality by identity\npublic class Customer {\n    private final CustomerId id;\n    private String name;\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Customer c && id.equals(c.id);\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["value-object", "entity", "tactical-ddd"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.752514	0
99dcd0c1-f01a-4f4c-bcc2-a85b4d71a60a	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What are Domain Events and how do they enable decoupling between Bounded Contexts?	Domain Events represent something meaningful that happened in the domain (e.g., OrderPlaced, PaymentReceived). They enable decoupling by allowing bounded contexts to react to changes without direct dependencies. The publishing context doesn't know or care who listens. This supports eventual consistency, audit trails, and enables CQRS. Events should be immutable, past-tense named, and carry enough data for consumers to act without calling back.	public record OrderPlacedEvent(\n    OrderId orderId,\n    CustomerId customerId,\n    Money total,\n    Instant occurredAt\n) implements DomainEvent {}\n\n// In aggregate root:\npublic void place() {\n    this.status = PLACED;\n    registerEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(id, customerId, total, Instant.now()));\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["domain-events", "eventual-consistency", "decoupling"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.759099	0
4eb6b380-2a1a-4ab6-91a0-87e5269ee4c0	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What is an Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) and when is it necessary?	An ACL is a translation layer that prevents a foreign or legacy model from leaking into your bounded context. It translates between external concepts and your domain model. Necessary when: (1) integrating with legacy systems whose model you can't change, (2) consuming third-party APIs with a different domain language, (3) protecting your model from upstream changes. Implemented as adapters, facades, or translators at context boundaries.	// ACL translates legacy CRM model to our domain\npublic class CrmCustomerTranslator {\n    private final LegacyCrmClient crmClient;\n\n    public Customer toDomainCustomer(String crmId) {\n        CrmRecord record = crmClient.fetchAccount(crmId);\n        return new Customer(\n            new CustomerId(record.getAccountNo()),\n            new FullName(record.getFirst(), record.getLast()),\n            Address.from(record.getAddr1(), record.getCity(), record.getZip())\n        );\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["anti-corruption-layer", "integration", "strategic-ddd"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.762393	0
94ac6734-c571-4623-a270-c4eedd9b7af0	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain the main Context Mapping strategies and when to use each.	Context Mapping defines relationships between bounded contexts. Key patterns: (1) Shared Kernel — two teams share a small common model (tight coupling, use sparingly), (2) Customer-Supplier — downstream team can negotiate changes with upstream, (3) Conformist — downstream accepts upstream's model as-is (no negotiation power), (4) Anti-Corruption Layer — downstream translates upstream model, (5) Open Host Service — upstream provides a well-defined protocol for all consumers, (6) Published Language — shared interchange format (e.g., JSON schema, protobuf).	\N	\N	HARD	["context-mapping", "strategic-ddd", "integration-patterns"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.765468	0
83d3db6a-0660-4245-9573-2c9acb67da7e	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the Singleton pattern and how do you implement it safely in Java?	Singleton ensures a class has exactly one instance with a global access point. Thread-safe implementations: (1) Enum singleton (preferred — handles serialization and reflection). (2) Bill Pugh (static inner class — lazy, thread-safe via class loading). (3) Double-checked locking with volatile. Avoid: eager initialization wastes resources if unused; synchronized getInstance() has performance overhead.	// Enum Singleton (preferred)\npublic enum DatabaseConnection {\n    INSTANCE;\n    public void query(String sql) { /* ... */ }\n}\n\n// Bill Pugh (lazy initialization holder)\npublic class Singleton {\n    private Singleton() {}\n    private static class Holder {\n        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();\n    }\n    public static Singleton getInstance() {\n        return Holder.INSTANCE;\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["singleton", "creational", "thread-safety"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.774844	0
a5ca069d-aef1-4a25-b323-30be5d5029a8	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain the Factory Method and Abstract Factory patterns.	Factory Method: defines an interface for creating objects but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate. Decouples client from concrete classes. Abstract Factory: provides an interface for creating families of related objects without specifying concrete classes. Use Factory Method when you have one product hierarchy; Abstract Factory when you have multiple related product families that must be used together.	// Factory Method\npublic abstract class NotificationFactory {\n    public abstract Notification createNotification();\n    public void send(String message) {\n        Notification n = createNotification();\n        n.notify(message);\n    }\n}\npublic class EmailFactory extends NotificationFactory {\n    public Notification createNotification() {\n        return new EmailNotification();\n    }\n}\n\n// Abstract Factory\npublic interface UIFactory {\n    Button createButton();\n    TextField createTextField();\n}\npublic class MaterialUIFactory implements UIFactory { /* ... */ }\n	\N	MEDIUM	["factory", "abstract-factory", "creational"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.778049	0
117add68-466d-4144-b40c-6fea4ae7d816	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the Strategy pattern and when should you use it?	Strategy defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. The client selects the algorithm at runtime. Use when: (1) Multiple algorithms exist for a task and you want to switch between them. (2) You want to eliminate conditional statements for algorithm selection. (3) Algorithms should be independently testable. Follows Open/Closed Principle — add new strategies without modifying existing code.	public interface CompressionStrategy {\n    byte[] compress(byte[] data);\n}\npublic class GzipCompression implements CompressionStrategy {\n    public byte[] compress(byte[] data) { /* gzip logic */ }\n}\npublic class ZipCompression implements CompressionStrategy {\n    public byte[] compress(byte[] data) { /* zip logic */ }\n}\n// Client\npublic class FileCompressor {\n    private CompressionStrategy strategy;\n    public FileCompressor(CompressionStrategy strategy) {\n        this.strategy = strategy;\n    }\n    public byte[] compress(byte[] data) {\n        return strategy.compress(data);\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["strategy", "behavioral", "polymorphism"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.781189	0
7bd6f96f-19d8-41ce-a4fb-49447da6fc02	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain the Observer pattern and its relationship to event-driven systems.	Observer defines a one-to-many dependency where when one object (subject) changes state, all dependents (observers) are notified automatically. Promotes loose coupling — subject doesn't know concrete observer types. In Java: PropertyChangeListener, Spring ApplicationEvent. Pitfalls: memory leaks (forgotten subscriptions), ordering dependencies, cascade updates. Modern alternatives: reactive streams (RxJava, Project Reactor), event buses.	public interface EventListener {\n    void onEvent(Event event);\n}\npublic class EventBus {\n    private final Map<String, List<EventListener>> listeners = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public void subscribe(String eventType, EventListener listener) {\n        listeners.computeIfAbsent(eventType, k -> new ArrayList<>())\n                 .add(listener);\n    }\n    public void publish(String eventType, Event event) {\n        listeners.getOrDefault(eventType, List.of())\n                 .forEach(l -> l.onEvent(event));\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["observer", "behavioral", "event-driven", "pub-sub"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.785363	0
6f0a5f6d-5daf-4130-a0c3-2220a7b693a0	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the Builder pattern and when is it preferred over constructors?	Builder separates construction of a complex object from its representation. Preferred when: (1) Constructor has many parameters (telescoping constructor anti-pattern). (2) Object has required and optional fields. (3) Object should be immutable after construction. (4) Construction involves multiple steps. In Java, often implemented as a static inner class. Lombok's @Builder generates this automatically.	public class HttpRequest {\n    private final String url;\n    private final String method;\n    private final Map<String, String> headers;\n    private final String body;\n\n    private HttpRequest(Builder builder) {\n        this.url = builder.url;\n        this.method = builder.method;\n        this.headers = Map.copyOf(builder.headers);\n        this.body = builder.body;\n    }\n    public static class Builder {\n        private final String url; // required\n        private String method = "GET";\n        private Map<String, String> headers = new HashMap<>();\n        private String body;\n\n        public Builder(String url) { this.url = url; }\n        public Builder method(String m) { this.method = m; return this; }\n        public Builder header(String k, String v) { headers.put(k, v); return this; }\n        public Builder body(String b) { this.body = b; return this; }\n        public HttpRequest build() { return new HttpRequest(this); }\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["builder", "creational", "immutable", "fluent-api"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.789574	0
91b344a0-fa50-4f66-9a84-9cdff02369f2	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain the Decorator pattern and how it differs from inheritance.	Decorator dynamically adds responsibilities to objects by wrapping them. Both decorator and wrapped object implement the same interface. Unlike inheritance (static, compile-time), decorators compose behavior at runtime and can be stacked. Use when: extending functionality without subclass explosion, adding cross-cutting concerns (logging, caching, validation). Java examples: BufferedInputStream wrapping FileInputStream, Collections.unmodifiableList().	public interface DataSource {\n    String readData();\n    void writeData(String data);\n}\npublic class FileDataSource implements DataSource { /* base */ }\n\npublic class EncryptionDecorator implements DataSource {\n    private final DataSource wrapped;\n    public EncryptionDecorator(DataSource source) { this.wrapped = source; }\n    public String readData() { return decrypt(wrapped.readData()); }\n    public void writeData(String data) { wrapped.writeData(encrypt(data)); }\n}\n// Usage: new EncryptionDecorator(new CompressionDecorator(new FileDataSource()))\n	\N	MEDIUM	["decorator", "structural", "composition", "wrapping"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.797121	0
fc6adbf0-eeb2-45a9-a6be-b34c4ec12c7f	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the Proxy pattern and what are its common variants?	Proxy provides a surrogate or placeholder for another object to control access. Variants: (1) Virtual Proxy — lazy initialization of expensive objects. (2) Protection Proxy — access control based on permissions. (3) Remote Proxy — represents an object in a different address space (RMI). (4) Caching Proxy — caches results of expensive operations. (5) Logging Proxy — adds logging around method calls. In Java: java.lang.reflect.Proxy for dynamic proxies, Spring AOP proxies.	public class CachingProxy implements DataService {\n    private final DataService realService;\n    private final Map<String, Object> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    public CachingProxy(DataService realService) {\n        this.realService = realService;\n    }\n    public Object getData(String key) {\n        return cache.computeIfAbsent(key, realService::getData);\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["proxy", "structural", "lazy-loading", "access-control"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.806492	0
4f37ee50-733a-4fcc-bdc8-c0462826efd6	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain the Command pattern and its use in undo/redo systems.	Command encapsulates a request as an object, allowing parameterization, queuing, logging, and undo/redo. Components: Command (interface with execute/undo), ConcreteCommand (binds receiver to action), Invoker (triggers commands), Receiver (performs actual work). For undo: maintain a history stack of executed commands; each command knows how to reverse itself. Enables macro commands (composite of multiple commands).	public interface Command {\n    void execute();\n    void undo();\n}\npublic class InsertTextCommand implements Command {\n    private final TextEditor editor;\n    private final String text;\n    private final int position;\n\n    public InsertTextCommand(TextEditor editor, String text, int pos) {\n        this.editor = editor; this.text = text; this.position = pos;\n    }\n    public void execute() { editor.insert(text, position); }\n    public void undo() { editor.delete(position, text.length()); }\n}\n// Invoker with undo stack\npublic class CommandHistory {\n    private final Deque<Command> history = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    public void execute(Command cmd) { cmd.execute(); history.push(cmd); }\n    public void undo() { if (!history.isEmpty()) history.pop().undo(); }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["command", "behavioral", "undo-redo", "encapsulation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.816554	0
d5557efb-4c93-43ba-813a-1879072da633	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is event sourcing and how does it differ from traditional state persistence?	Event sourcing persists every state change as an immutable event rather than storing current state.\nCurrent state is derived by replaying events from the event store. This provides a complete audit\ntrail, enables temporal queries, and allows rebuilding state at any point in time.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["event-sourcing", "persistence", "audit"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.831177	0
17410ca1-eef0-40a0-a55a-bbf41c6461fe	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you handle eventual consistency in an event-driven system?	Accept that read models lag behind writes. Use idempotent event handlers with deduplication\n(event ID tracking). Provide UI patterns like optimistic updates or polling. For critical\nconsistency, use process managers or return confirmation only after projection catches up.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["eventual-consistency", "idempotency", "distributed-systems"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.839984	0
c86a8e70-9408-4425-8568-ba92e7f1fc4f	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Explain the Saga pattern and the difference between choreography and orchestration.	Sagas coordinate distributed transactions through a sequence of local transactions with\ncompensating actions on failure. Choreography uses events where each service reacts\nindependently (decentralized, harder to trace). Orchestration uses a central coordinator\nthat directs participants (easier to reason about, single point of failure risk).\n	// Orchestration-based saga\npublic class OrderSaga {\n    @SagaStart\n    public void handle(OrderPlacedEvent event) {\n        commandGateway.send(new ReserveInventoryCommand(event.getItems()));\n    }\n    @SagaEventHandler\n    public void on(InventoryReservedEvent event) {\n        commandGateway.send(new ProcessPaymentCommand(event.getOrderId()));\n    }\n    @SagaEventHandler\n    public void on(PaymentFailedEvent event) {\n        commandGateway.send(new ReleaseInventoryCommand(event.getOrderId()));\n        end(); // compensating action triggered\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["saga", "distributed-transactions", "compensation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.843895	0
6df59eee-e1cf-4489-9449-907009a85c6a	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you design an event store and what are the key considerations?	An event store is an append-only log partitioned by aggregate ID. Key considerations:\noptimistic concurrency via expected version numbers, snapshotting for aggregates with many\nevents, global ordering for projections, and retention/archival policies. Events are immutable\nonce written.\n	CREATE TABLE event_store (\n    event_id       UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    aggregate_id   UUID NOT NULL,\n    aggregate_type VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    version        BIGINT NOT NULL,\n    event_type     VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    payload        JSONB NOT NULL,\n    metadata       JSONB,\n    created_at     TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),\n    UNIQUE (aggregate_id, version)  -- optimistic concurrency\n);\n	\N	EASY	["event-store", "append-only", "concurrency"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.849693	0
844997e5-aed3-4c49-9299-e6356af74eee	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you handle event schema evolution and versioning in a production system?	Use an event registry with explicit version numbers per event type. Support upcasting\n(transforming old events to new schema on read) rather than migrating stored events.\nApply weak schema (add fields with defaults, never remove/rename). For breaking changes,\nintroduce a new event type and deprecate the old one. Schema registries (like Avro with\ncompatibility checks) prevent incompatible changes at publish time.\n	// Upcaster transforms v1 events to v2 on replay\npublic class OrderPlacedUpcaster implements EventUpcaster {\n    @Override\n    public boolean canUpcast(String eventType, int version) {\n        return "OrderPlaced".equals(eventType) && version == 1;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public JsonNode upcast(JsonNode event) {\n        ObjectNode node = (ObjectNode) event;\n        node.put("currency", "USD"); // field added in v2\n        node.put("schemaVersion", 2);\n        return node;\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["schema-evolution", "upcasting", "backward-compatibility"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.854699	0
0b6e2a1c-caba-487c-beb3-d0db5f73130d	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What are the four levels of the Richardson Maturity Model?	Level 0 — single URI, single HTTP method (RPC tunnel, e.g. SOAP over POST).\nLevel 1 — individual resources with distinct URIs but only POST.\nLevel 2 — proper use of HTTP verbs (GET, PUT, DELETE) and status codes.\nLevel 3 — HATEOAS: responses include hypermedia links guiding the client\nto available state transitions. Each level builds on the previous one.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["rest", "richardson-maturity-model", "api-design"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.863047	0
54eb05bf-9fb3-4711-b44e-79163dbfe359	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What is HATEOAS and why is it considered the highest REST maturity level?	HATEOAS (Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State) means the server\nembeds hyperlinks in responses that tell the client what actions are available\nnext. Clients need no out-of-band knowledge of the API structure — they\ndiscover transitions at runtime. This decouples client from server URL\nstructure and enables independent API evolution.\n	// Spring HATEOAS example\n@GetMapping("/orders/{id}")\npublic EntityModel<Order> getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    Order order = service.findById(id);\n    return EntityModel.of(order,\n        linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).getOrder(id)).withSelfRel(),\n        linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).cancel(id)).withRel("cancel"));\n}\n	\N	HARD	["hateoas", "hypermedia", "rest-maturity"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.868289	0
42b5d9b7-e6c4-4d0c-9449-3e73751c407a	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Why must a RESTful service be stateless?	Each request must contain all information needed to process it — the server\nstores no client session between requests. This enables horizontal scaling\n(any server can handle any request), simplifies failover, improves cacheability,\nand makes the system more reliable since no session state can be lost.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["rest", "statelessness", "scalability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.872381	0
12932c0d-484e-4fec-bcfc-42b05be519b3	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What are best practices for REST resource naming?	Use plural nouns for collections (/orders, /users), not verbs. Nest\nsub-resources to show hierarchy (/users/{id}/orders). Use hyphens for\nreadability in multi-word names (/line-items). Avoid file extensions —\nuse Accept headers for content negotiation. Keep URIs lowercase and\nnever expose implementation details (no /getOrder or /api/v1/OrderService).\n	// Good\nGET  /api/v1/customers/{id}/orders\nPOST /api/v1/orders\n\n// Bad\nGET  /api/v1/getCustomerOrders?customerId=5\nPOST /api/v1/orderService/createOrder\n	\N	EASY	["rest", "uri-design", "naming-conventions"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.875669	0
f44f71f7-1f43-4599-a703-ae3067c7b099	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Which HTTP methods are idempotent and what does idempotency mean for API design?	Idempotent means multiple identical requests produce the same server state\nas a single request. GET, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS are idempotent. POST\nand PATCH are not. PUT replaces the entire resource so repeating it is safe.\nDELETE on an already-deleted resource returns 404 but state remains the same.\nIdempotency enables safe retries on network failures without side effects.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["http-methods", "idempotency", "rest"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.878302	0
699d92d0-81cd-47dc-8b9f-a6bbeb8af7b3	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Explain HTTP content negotiation via the Accept header.	The client sends an Accept header specifying preferred response formats\n(e.g. application/json, application/xml) with optional quality factors.\nThe server inspects Accept and returns the best matching representation\nwith the appropriate Content-Type. If no match exists, the server responds\nwith 406 Not Acceptable.\n	// Spring MVC content negotiation\n@GetMapping(value = "/products/{id}",\n            produces = {MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE,\n                        MediaType.APPLICATION_XML_VALUE})\npublic Product getProduct(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return productService.findById(id);\n}\n// Client: Accept: application/xml;q=0.9, application/json;q=1.0\n// Server responds with JSON (higher quality factor)\n	\N	MEDIUM	["content-negotiation", "http-headers", "rest"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.88098	0
39018e58-b56f-45f3-9f3b-2fe6b1f3408b	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Explain round robin vs weighted round robin load balancing. When would you choose weighted?	Round robin distributes requests sequentially across servers in equal turns. Weighted round robin assigns a weight to each server, sending proportionally more traffic to higher-weight nodes. Choose weighted when servers have unequal capacity (e.g., 16-core vs 4-core machines) or during canary deployments where new versions should receive less traffic initially.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["load-balancing", "round-robin", "traffic-distribution"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.45155	0
99b6377d-d806-4881-bd9d-7907c44713da	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) and how do you identify violations?	SRP: a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one responsibility or job. Identify violations by asking 'who would request changes to this class?' If multiple actors (e.g., HR and Accounting) would cause changes, the class has multiple responsibilities. Signs: class name includes 'And' or 'Manager', class has methods serving different stakeholders, changes to one feature break another.	// Violation: Employee handles persistence AND business logic\npublic class Employee {\n    public double calculatePay() { /* ... */ }\n    public void save() { /* DB logic */ }\n    public String generateReport() { /* formatting */ }\n}\n\n// Fixed: separate responsibilities\npublic class Employee { public double calculatePay() { /* ... */ } }\npublic class EmployeeRepository { public void save(Employee e) { /* ... */ } }\npublic class EmployeeReportGenerator { public String generate(Employee e) { /* ... */ } }\n	\N	EASY	["srp", "solid", "single-responsibility", "cohesion"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.886775	0
5addd16b-5dcd-4fa5-80b3-8b1610f324e0	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Explain the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) with a practical example.	OCP: software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification. Add new behavior by adding new code (new classes/implementations), not changing existing code. Achieved through: abstraction (interfaces), polymorphism (strategy pattern), composition. Violation sign: adding a new type requires modifying existing switch/if-else chains.	// Violation: adding new shape requires modifying this method\npublic double area(Shape shape) {\n    if (shape instanceof Circle c) return Math.PI * c.radius * c.radius;\n    if (shape instanceof Rectangle r) return r.width * r.height;\n    // Must modify for every new shape!\n}\n\n// Fixed: polymorphism\npublic interface Shape {\n    double area();\n}\npublic class Circle implements Shape {\n    private final double radius;\n    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }\n}\npublic class Triangle implements Shape {\n    public double area() { return 0.5 * base * height; }\n}\n// New shapes added without modifying existing code\n	\N	MEDIUM	["ocp", "solid", "open-closed", "polymorphism"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.889228	0
6f2bfe87-c0c1-483d-9c54-908ab8bd06d6	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) and what are common violations?	LSP: objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without altering program correctness. Subtypes must honor the contract of the base type. Common violations: (1) Square extending Rectangle (setting width changes height — breaks Rectangle's contract). (2) Throwing UnsupportedOperationException in overridden methods. (3) Strengthening preconditions or weakening postconditions in subclass.	// Violation: Square breaks Rectangle's contract\npublic class Rectangle {\n    protected int width, height;\n    public void setWidth(int w) { this.width = w; }\n    public void setHeight(int h) { this.height = h; }\n    public int area() { return width * height; }\n}\npublic class Square extends Rectangle {\n    public void setWidth(int w) { this.width = w; this.height = w; } // breaks LSP!\n}\n\n// Fixed: separate abstractions\npublic interface Shape { int area(); }\npublic record Rectangle(int width, int height) implements Shape {\n    public int area() { return width * height; }\n}\npublic record Square(int side) implements Shape {\n    public int area() { return side * side; }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["lsp", "solid", "liskov", "substitution", "contracts"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.891641	0
58644d14-030a-4ae3-bca5-515a89476c1c	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Explain the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) and its benefits.	ISP: clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they don't use. Split fat interfaces into smaller, focused ones. Benefits: (1) Reduces coupling — classes only depend on methods they actually call. (2) Easier to implement — no need for empty/throwing method stubs. (3) Better testability — mock only relevant interface. Signs of violation: interfaces with many methods where most implementors throw UnsupportedOperationException.	// Violation: fat interface\npublic interface Worker {\n    void work();\n    void eat();\n    void sleep();\n}\npublic class Robot implements Worker {\n    public void work() { /* ok */ }\n    public void eat() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); } // ISP violation!\n    public void sleep() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }\n}\n\n// Fixed: segregated interfaces\npublic interface Workable { void work(); }\npublic interface Feedable { void eat(); }\npublic interface Restable { void sleep(); }\npublic class Robot implements Workable { public void work() { /* ok */ } }\npublic class Human implements Workable, Feedable, Restable { /* all methods */ }\n	\N	MEDIUM	["isp", "solid", "interface-segregation", "decoupling"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.894169	0
178b97d5-1d8f-4074-9d60-a65a4dd88ee3	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) and how does Spring implement it?	DIP: (1) High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. (2) Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions. Spring implements DIP via IoC container: beans depend on interfaces, Spring injects concrete implementations at runtime. This inverts the traditional control flow where high-level code directly instantiates dependencies.	// Violation: high-level depends on low-level\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final MySQLOrderRepository repo = new MySQLOrderRepository(); // tight coupling!\n}\n\n// Fixed: depend on abstraction\npublic interface OrderRepository {\n    void save(Order order);\n}\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository repo; // depends on interface\n    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }\n}\n@Repository\npublic class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {\n    public void save(Order order) { /* JPA logic */ }\n}\n// Spring injects JpaOrderRepository into OrderService\n	\N	MEDIUM	["dip", "solid", "dependency-inversion", "ioc", "spring"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.896488	0
70e38d99-9f9b-4ba6-b597-5646e3ab4439	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the ACID properties and what does each guarantee?	Atomicity: entire transaction succeeds or fails completely (no partial writes). Consistency: transaction moves DB from one valid state to another (constraints/triggers enforced). Isolation: concurrent transactions don't interfere with each other (as if sequential). Durability: once committed, data survives crashes (written to non-volatile storage). Trade-offs: strict ACID reduces throughput; many systems relax isolation for performance.	\N	\N	EASY	["acid", "transactions", "fundamentals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.905264	0
a4047714-cd87-478b-8b44-3f1841a12f5a	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the SQL isolation levels and what anomalies does each prevent?	READ UNCOMMITTED: allows dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, phantoms. READ COMMITTED (PostgreSQL default): prevents dirty reads; allows non-repeatable reads and phantoms. REPEATABLE READ: prevents dirty and non-repeatable reads; PostgreSQL also prevents phantoms (snapshot isolation). SERIALIZABLE: prevents all anomalies including write skew; transactions appear to execute serially. Higher isolation = more blocking/aborts = lower throughput.	\N	Dirty read: reading uncommitted data from another transaction. Non-repeatable read: re-reading same row gives different result. Phantom: re-executing query returns new/removed rows.	MEDIUM	["isolation-levels", "transactions", "concurrency"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.907883	0
3b4e4a99-1eaf-473b-bc7b-619afc08c667	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How does a B-tree index work and why is it the default index type?	B-tree is a balanced tree where each node contains sorted keys and pointers to child nodes. Leaf nodes contain pointers to actual rows. Properties: O(log n) search, insert, delete. Supports equality (=) and range queries (<, >, BETWEEN). Keeps data sorted enabling ORDER BY without extra sort. Self-balancing: splits/merges maintain O(log n) height. Default because it handles most query patterns well (point lookups, ranges, sorting, prefix LIKE).	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["b-tree", "index", "data-structures"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.910244	0
52187dac-3637-44f8-bc09-9b246ce3e2fc	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the difference between clustered and non-clustered indexes?	Clustered index: defines physical row order on disk. Table data IS the index leaf level. Only ONE per table. In PostgreSQL, this is the table heap itself (CLUSTER command reorders once but doesn't maintain). In MySQL InnoDB, primary key IS the clustered index. Non-clustered (secondary) index: separate structure pointing to the row location. Multiple allowed. Lookups may need a 'bookmark lookup' to fetch non-indexed columns from the base table.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["clustered-index", "non-clustered", "physical-storage"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.912796	0
3ea3882f-6067-432b-a16b-19e8a22a0627	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How do you read an EXPLAIN ANALYZE output in PostgreSQL?	Key metrics: 1) actual time (first row..last row in ms). 2) rows: estimated vs actual (big difference = stale statistics). 3) loops: how many times node executed. 4) Plan nodes: Seq Scan (full table), Index Scan (index + heap), Index Only Scan (covers query), Bitmap Scan (two-phase: build bitmap, then fetch). 5) Nested Loop vs Hash Join vs Merge Join. Look for: Seq Scan on large tables, high row estimates vs actual, Sort with large external sorts.	EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;\n-- Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders\n--   (cost=0.43..8.45 rows=1 width=64)\n--   (actual time=0.023..0.025 rows=3 loops=1)\n-- Planning Time: 0.15 ms\n-- Execution Time: 0.08 ms\n	\N	HARD	["explain-analyze", "query-plan", "postgresql", "performance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.915106	0
e7605d23-d3e4-4fa4-84e3-31b386331095	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the N+1 query problem and how do you solve it?	N+1 problem: fetching a parent entity with N children results in 1 query for parents + N individual queries for each child's related data. Example: loading 100 orders then lazily fetching customer for each = 101 queries. Solutions: 1) JOIN FETCH / eager join in one query. 2) Batch fetching (@BatchSize in Hibernate). 3) Subselect fetching. 4) EntityGraph or @Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT). 5) DTO projection with explicit JOIN in JPQL. Detect with: Hibernate SQL logging or p6spy.	\N	\N	EASY	["n-plus-one", "orm", "performance", "hibernate"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.917631	0
19471305-e5ff-4902-9f20-e42fedc33913	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is connection pooling and why is it essential for database performance?	Connection pooling maintains a cache of reusable database connections. Opening a new DB connection involves TCP handshake, TLS negotiation, authentication, memory allocation - expensive (5-50ms). Pools (HikariCP, pgBouncer) keep connections open and lend them to requests. Key settings: maximumPoolSize (typically 2*CPU cores + effective_spindle_count for OLTP), minimumIdle, connectionTimeout, idleTimeout, maxLifetime. Too many connections cause contention on DB side (context switching, lock contention).	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["connection-pooling", "hikaricp", "performance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.919981	0
72a82751-5d66-428a-b8b7-586eaef38273	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the difference between optimistic and pessimistic locking?	Optimistic: assume conflicts are rare. Read data with a version/timestamp. On write, check version hasn't changed; if it has, retry or fail (StaleObjectStateException). No DB locks held during read. Good for: high-read, low-contention. Pessimistic: acquire explicit lock on read (SELECT FOR UPDATE). Blocks other transactions from modifying the row. Good for: high-contention, short transactions. Trade-off: pessimistic prevents conflicts but reduces concurrency; optimistic allows concurrency but may require retries.	// JPA Optimistic Locking\n@Entity\npublic class Account {\n    @Version\n    private long version; // auto-incremented on update\n}\n\n-- Pessimistic (SQL)\nSELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE;\n-- Row is locked until transaction commits/rolls back\n	\N	MEDIUM	["optimistic-locking", "pessimistic-locking", "concurrency"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.922313	0
459825ee-a820-4d58-9e58-ce105a368027	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the normal forms (1NF through 3NF) in database normalization?	1NF: each column holds atomic (indivisible) values; no repeating groups or arrays. 2NF: 1NF + every non-key column depends on the ENTIRE primary key (eliminates partial dependencies in composite keys). 3NF: 2NF + no transitive dependencies (non-key column depending on another non-key column). Example violation of 3NF: storing city AND zip_code where city depends on zip_code, not directly on the primary key. BCNF: every determinant is a candidate key.	\N	\N	EASY	["normalization", "1nf", "2nf", "3nf", "schema-design"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.92457	0
949e64ad-17cf-4893-aa81-6a2e5a3676ce	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the logical order of SQL query execution?	Logical processing order (not written order): 1) FROM + JOINs (determine working set). 2) WHERE (filter rows). 3) GROUP BY (aggregate). 4) HAVING (filter groups). 5) SELECT (pick columns, evaluate expressions). 6) DISTINCT. 7) ORDER BY. 8) LIMIT/OFFSET. This is why you can't use a SELECT alias in WHERE (not evaluated yet), but CAN use it in ORDER BY. HAVING filters after aggregation; WHERE filters before.	-- Written order:\nSELECT department, COUNT(*) as cnt  -- step 5\nFROM employees                      -- step 1\nWHERE salary > 50000               -- step 2\nGROUP BY department                 -- step 3\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 5                -- step 4\nORDER BY cnt DESC                  -- step 7\nLIMIT 10;                          -- step 8\n	\N	EASY	["sql-order", "query-execution", "fundamentals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.927102	0
670c71a5-1a10-4389-99cb-4717cee29449	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Describe B-tree index structure and why it provides O(log n) lookups.	B-tree is a self-balancing tree where internal nodes store keys and child pointers, and leaf nodes store keys with row pointers (or values in InnoDB). Each node holds multiple keys (high fan-out), keeping tree height low (typically 3-4 levels for millions of rows). Lookups traverse root to leaf comparing keys at each level. Supports equality, range, and prefix queries because leaves are linked in sorted order.	\N	\N	EASY	["b-tree", "index-structure", "fundamentals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.932798	0
b44655ce-8956-4c46-a923-0b214b6165ce	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	When would you use a hash index instead of a B-tree index?	Hash indexes provide O(1) exact-match lookups but cannot support range queries, sorting, or prefix matching. Use them for equality-only access patterns like session lookups by token, cache key resolution, or join columns that are never range-scanned. PostgreSQL supports hash indexes; MySQL Memory engine uses them. B-tree is preferred in most cases because it handles both equality and range while hash only wins marginally on pure equality.	\N	\N	EASY	["hash-index", "use-cases", "trade-offs"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.935088	0
2b11f52f-66aa-4964-bcf9-4f972db3939b	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Explain the composite index leftmost prefix rule with an example.	A composite index on (a, b, c) can satisfy queries filtering on (a), (a, b), or (a, b, c) but NOT (b), (c), or (b, c) alone. The index is sorted first by a, then by b within each a value, then by c. Skipping the leftmost column means the optimizer cannot binary-search into the tree. A query on (a, c) uses the index for column a only; column c requires a filter step after the index scan.	-- Given: CREATE INDEX idx_abc ON orders(status, customer_id, created_at);\n-- Uses full index:\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'SHIPPED' AND customer_id = 42 AND created_at > '2024-01-01';\n-- Uses prefix (status only):\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';\n-- Cannot use index (skips leftmost):\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;\n	\N	MEDIUM	["composite-index", "leftmost-prefix", "query-optimization"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.937264	0
8e13c690-5e58-4f79-aa60-75e9972bdb8f	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is a covering index and how does it eliminate table lookups?	A covering index includes all columns needed by a query, so the database can satisfy the query entirely from the index without accessing the table heap (index-only scan). This eliminates random I/O to fetch rows. In PostgreSQL, EXPLAIN shows 'Index Only Scan'. In MySQL, EXPLAIN shows 'Using index' in Extra. Design covering indexes for frequent queries by INCLUDEing selected columns beyond the search keys.	// JPA native query benefiting from covering index\n@Query(nativeQuery = true, value =\n  "SELECT customer_id, total FROM orders WHERE status = ?1 AND created_at > ?2")\nList<Object[]> findRecentByStatus(String status, LocalDate since);\n\n-- Covering index (PostgreSQL):\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_covering ON orders(status, created_at) INCLUDE (customer_id, total);\n-- EXPLAIN shows: Index Only Scan using idx_orders_covering\n	\N	MEDIUM	["covering-index", "index-only-scan", "performance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.940101	0
0e45426e-a745-4e26-b13d-b7c7a7906a47	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is a partial (filtered) index and when should you use one?	A partial index includes only rows matching a WHERE predicate, making it smaller and faster than a full index. Use when queries consistently filter on a subset: active records, non-null values, recent dates, or specific status values. Smaller index means less storage, faster inserts on excluded rows, and better cache utilization. The query's WHERE clause must match or imply the index predicate for the planner to use it.	-- Only index unprocessed orders (5% of table)\nCREATE INDEX idx_pending_orders ON orders(created_at)\n  WHERE status = 'PENDING';\n\n-- Query must include matching predicate:\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND created_at < now() - interval '1 hour';\n-- Planner uses idx_pending_orders (tiny index vs full table scan)\n	\N	MEDIUM	["partial-index", "filtered-index", "postgresql"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.942331	0
d407ffc7-dcd6-49c8-b781-0b91623107ab	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	When should you NOT add an index? List scenarios where indexes hurt performance.	Avoid indexing when: 1) Table is small (<1000 rows) - sequential scan is faster than index overhead. 2) Column has very low cardinality (boolean, status with 2-3 values) - index scan reads most of the table anyway. 3) Table is write-heavy with rare reads - each INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must maintain all indexes. 4) Column is frequently updated - causes index page splits and fragmentation. 5) Query returns >10-15% of rows - optimizer chooses seq scan regardless. Monitor with pg_stat_user_indexes: idx_scan = 0 means unused index wasting write cycles.	\N	\N	HARD	["anti-patterns", "write-amplification", "index-selection"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.944587	0
652d74b1-d233-4f31-a1cd-1042cdbe9581	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How do you interpret EXPLAIN ANALYZE to determine if an index is being used effectively?	Key indicators: 1) Index Scan vs Seq Scan - check if expected index is chosen. 2) Rows estimated vs actual - large mismatch means stale statistics (run ANALYZE). 3) Bitmap Index Scan appears for moderate selectivity (too many rows for plain index scan, too few for seq scan). 4) Filter: rows removed shows post-index filtering waste. 5) High 'loops' count in nested loop join signals missing index on inner relation. 6) Index Cond vs Filter - predicates in Index Cond are pushed to index; those in Filter are evaluated after fetch.	EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 AND total > 100;\n-- Good: Index Scan using idx_orders_customer (actual rows=5)\n--   Index Cond: (customer_id = 42)\n--   Filter: (total > 100)        -- <- not pushed to index\n--   Rows Removed by Filter: 45   -- <- 45 rows fetched but discarded\n--   Buffers: shared hit=3\n-- Fix: composite index on (customer_id, total) pushes both to Index Cond\n	\N	HARD	["explain-analyze", "query-plan", "performance-tuning"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.947107	0
6f75f776-a875-4aa7-8e50-f740f6c32da7	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF with a concrete example of a table that violates each.	1NF: all columns atomic, no repeating groups. Violation: storing 'Java,Python' in a single skills column. 2NF: 1NF + no partial dependencies on composite keys. Violation: (student_id, course_id) -> student_name depends only on student_id. 3NF: 2NF + no transitive dependencies. Violation: employee table with zip_code -> city; city depends on zip_code, not on employee_id. Each normal form eliminates a specific type of update anomaly.	\N	\N	EASY	["normalization", "1nf", "2nf", "3nf"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.952563	0
8734eb2c-6abc-4fb5-967d-70e4cdc2dcfc	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What is BCNF and how does it differ from 3NF? Give a scenario where a table is in 3NF but not BCNF.	BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form) requires that every determinant is a candidate key. 3NF allows a non-key attribute to determine part of a candidate key. Classic example: a table (student, subject, professor) where each professor teaches only one subject, and each subject has multiple professors. {student, subject} is the PK, but professor -> subject is a functional dependency where professor is not a superkey. This is 3NF (no transitive dependency on non-prime attributes) but violates BCNF.	\N	\N	HARD	["bcnf", "normalization", "functional-dependencies"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.95462	0
095fbebc-048b-4060-ba23-0aa95433bc77	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What is a functional dependency and how do you identify them when designing a schema?	A functional dependency X -> Y means that for every valid instance, if two tuples agree on X they must agree on Y. Identification: ask 'given a value of X, is Y uniquely determined?' For example, employee_id -> email (one email per employee) but department_id -/-> employee_name (many employees per department). Functional dependencies drive normalization: partial dependencies violate 2NF, transitive dependencies violate 3NF, non-candidate-key determinants violate BCNF.	// JPA entity showing transitive dependency violation (3NF)\n@Entity\npublic class Employee {\n    @Id private Long id;\n    private String zipCode;\n    private String city;  // city depends on zipCode, not on id directly\n}\n// Fix: extract Address or use zipCode lookup\n	\N	MEDIUM	["functional-dependencies", "schema-design", "normalization"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.956498	0
dd7c2f70-a8c6-457a-9fd7-6a6b6f8d1b85	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the difference between horizontal and vertical scaling?	Vertical scaling (scale up) adds more resources (CPU, RAM) to a single machine. Horizontal scaling (scale out) adds more machines to distribute load. Horizontal scaling provides better fault tolerance and theoretically unlimited capacity, while vertical scaling is simpler but has hardware limits.	\N	\N	EASY	["scaling", "horizontal", "vertical", "fundamentals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.506856	0
65a8ce5b-3e65-4971-8c6b-f13c68e7b90b	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	When should you denormalize a database, and what are the tradeoffs?	Denormalize when: read-heavy workloads where JOINs are the bottleneck, pre-computed aggregates needed for dashboards, microservice boundaries require data locality, or caching hot paths. Tradeoffs: faster reads but slower writes (must update multiple places), risk of data inconsistency (update anomalies), increased storage, and more complex application code to maintain consistency. Always measure first with EXPLAIN ANALYZE before denormalizing. Common patterns: materialized views, summary tables, embedding frequently-joined columns.	// Denormalized order with embedded customer name\n@Entity\npublic class Order {\n    @Id private Long id;\n    private Long customerId;\n    private String customerName;  // denormalized for read performance\n    private BigDecimal totalAmount;  // pre-computed aggregate\n}\n// Trade: must update customerName if customer renames\n	\N	MEDIUM	["denormalization", "performance", "tradeoffs"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.959077	0
bc757a49-30df-445d-ba33-bee1ac3bd23b	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What is a star schema and why is it preferred for analytics/data warehousing?	Star schema has a central fact table (measurable events like sales) surrounded by dimension tables (descriptive context like customer, product, time). Fact tables store foreign keys to dimensions plus numeric measures. Benefits: simple queries (few JOINs, always fact-to-dimension), excellent query performance (dimension tables are small, bitmap indexes effective), intuitive for business users and BI tools. Deliberately denormalized dimensions (no snowflaking) trade storage for query speed. Contrast with OLTP normalized schemas optimized for writes.	-- Star schema example\nCREATE TABLE fact_sales (\n    sale_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_key INT REFERENCES dim_customer(key),\n    product_key INT REFERENCES dim_product(key),\n    date_key INT REFERENCES dim_date(key),\n    quantity INT,\n    revenue DECIMAL(12,2)\n);\n-- Query: revenue by product category and quarter\nSELECT d.quarter, p.category, SUM(f.revenue)\nFROM fact_sales f\nJOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.key\nJOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.key\nGROUP BY d.quarter, p.category;\n	\N	MEDIUM	["star-schema", "data-warehouse", "analytics"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.961471	0
1996c1df-76ea-479a-912d-03f284a8720a	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How do you decide the right level of normalization for a Java/Spring application with both OLTP and reporting needs?	Use normalized schema (3NF/BCNF) for OLTP tables handling writes via JPA entities. For reporting, create materialized views or a separate read model (CQRS pattern). Spring approach: primary DataSource with normalized schema for transactional operations, secondary read-only DataSource pointing to denormalized views or a data warehouse. Use @Transactional(readOnly=true) on reporting queries to route to read replicas. Event-driven sync (Debezium CDC or domain events) keeps the read model eventually consistent with OLTP source.	@Configuration\npublic class DataSourceConfig {\n    @Bean @Primary\n    DataSource oltpDataSource() { /* normalized 3NF schema */ }\n\n    @Bean @Qualifier("reporting")\n    DataSource reportingDataSource() { /* denormalized star schema */ }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class SalesReportService {\n    @Transactional(readOnly = true)\n    public Report getQuarterlySales(int quarter) {\n        // queries denormalized materialized view\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["cqrs", "normalization", "spring", "architecture"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.963655	0
