22bd1134-85c2-41d3-a85b-4fd0f8a9c9c6	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What Redis data structures beyond simple strings would you use in a Java application, and for what?	Sorted Sets for leaderboards and rate limiting (ZADD/ZRANGEBYSCORE). Hashes for object caching without serialization overhead. Streams for event sourcing and message queues with consumer groups. HyperLogLog for approximate unique counts at constant memory. Pub/Sub for real-time notifications between services.\n	// Jedis - sliding window rate limiter with sorted set\nString key = "rate:" + userId;\nlong now = System.currentTimeMillis();\nPipeline p = jedis.pipelined();\np.zremrangeByScore(key, 0, now - windowMs);\np.zadd(key, now, UUID.randomUUID().toString());\np.zcard(key);\np.expire(key, (int)(windowMs / 1000));\nList<Object> results = p.syncAndReturnAll();\nlong count = (Long) results.get(2);\nif (count > maxRequests) throw new RateLimitException();\n	\N	HARD	["redis", "data-structures", "rate-limiting"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.989646	0
0d9ae7f3-d709-4990-b0d6-8be630c0d373	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How does Cassandra's write path achieve high throughput, and what are the failure implications?	Writes go to commit log (sequential disk append) then memtable (in-memory sorted structure). No read-before-write, no locking - all writes are appends. Memtables flush to immutable SSTables periodically. This gives O(1) write performance regardless of dataset size. Trade-off: reads may check multiple SSTables (mitigated by bloom filters and compaction), and deletes require tombstones that accumulate until compaction.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["cassandra", "write-path", "storage-engine"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:24.992672	0
92b135da-1bed-4437-9214-dd872aee8851	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What are the three main JOIN algorithms and when does the optimizer choose each?	Nested Loop Join: iterates outer table, probes inner for each row - best for small outer set with indexed inner table. Hash Join: builds hash table from smaller relation, probes with larger - best for large unsorted datasets with equality joins. Merge Join (Sort-Merge): sorts both inputs then merges - best when both sides are pre-sorted (index scan) or result is needed sorted. Optimizer chooses based on table sizes, available indexes, and sort order.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["join-algorithms", "query-planner", "performance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.003972	0
e6be6f3a-4993-4b28-b47e-ea2da19adff5	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you read a query execution plan to identify the most expensive operation?	Start from innermost (most indented) node and read outward. Each node shows estimated cost (startup..total), row estimate, and actual time in ANALYZE mode. The node with highest 'actual time * loops' is the bottleneck. Look for sequential scans on large tables, high 'Rows Removed by Filter' indicating missing indexes, and large differences between estimated and actual rows indicating stale statistics.	EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, FORMAT TEXT)\nSELECT o.id, c.name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.status = 'PENDING';\n-- Look for:\n--   actual time=0.015..245.332 rows=50000  <- expensive node\n--   Buffers: shared read=12000             <- heavy I/O\n--   Rows Removed by Filter: 980000         <- missing index\n	\N	EASY	["explain-analyze", "execution-plan", "diagnostics"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.006902	0
242a17e7-4239-484d-a0ab-aed7db5330db	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Why can a correlated subquery be orders of magnitude slower than an equivalent JOIN?	A correlated subquery executes once per row of the outer query (O(n*m) in worst case), while a JOIN allows the optimizer to choose hash or merge strategies that process both tables in a single pass (O(n+m)). Modern optimizers can sometimes decorrelate subqueries into joins, but complex predicates or LIMIT/EXISTS patterns may prevent this. Use EXPLAIN to verify if decorrelation occurred.	// Slow: correlated subquery executes per-row\n@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.salary > " +\n       "(SELECT AVG(e2.salary) FROM Employee e2 WHERE e2.department = e.department)")\nList<Employee> findAboveAvgSalary();\n\n// Fast: rewritten as JOIN against pre-aggregated subquery\n@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e JOIN " +\n       "(SELECT department, AVG(salary) as avgSal FROM Employee GROUP BY department) d " +\n       "ON e.department = d.department WHERE e.salary > d.avgSal")\nList<Employee> findAboveAvgSalaryOptimized();\n	\N	MEDIUM	["subquery", "join-rewrite", "query-optimization"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.009433	0
af14e5eb-8ec0-4ae0-b913-3755ebc61448	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is a cost-based optimizer and what statistics does it rely on?	A cost-based optimizer (CBO) evaluates multiple execution plans and picks the one with lowest estimated cost (I/O + CPU). It relies on table statistics: row count, column cardinality (n_distinct), histogram of value distribution, average row width, correlation (physical vs logical order), and null fraction. Stale statistics lead to bad plans. PostgreSQL updates via ANALYZE; MySQL via ANALYZE TABLE.	\N	\N	EASY	["cost-based-optimizer", "statistics", "query-planner"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.011642	0
7a05f11e-9d79-48a9-bb88-f98a70998089	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	When should you use a materialized view versus a regular view for query optimization?	Use materialized views when: the underlying query is expensive (aggregations, multi-table joins), data changes infrequently relative to reads, and slight staleness is acceptable. Regular views execute the query each time and add no performance benefit. Trade-offs: materialized views consume storage, require refresh strategy (periodic or on-demand), and can become stale. In PostgreSQL, REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY allows reads during refresh but requires a unique index.	-- PostgreSQL materialized view for dashboard metrics\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW order_daily_stats AS\nSELECT date_trunc('day', created_at) as day,\n       status, COUNT(*) as cnt, SUM(total) as revenue\nFROM orders GROUP BY 1, 2\nWITH DATA;\n\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_ods_day_status ON order_daily_stats(day, status);\n-- Concurrent refresh (no read lock):\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY order_daily_stats;\n	\N	MEDIUM	["materialized-view", "caching", "postgresql"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.013906	0
879c88c8-7b70-47ae-a832-43cfa4cd4784	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you use query hints in JPA/Hibernate and when are they appropriate?	Query hints override optimizer decisions when you have knowledge the optimizer lacks. In JPA, use @QueryHints annotation or entityManager.createQuery().setHint(). Common hints: org.hibernate.fetchSize (batch fetch rows), org.hibernate.readOnly (skip dirty checking), org.hibernate.timeout (query timeout), and native hints via nativeQuery with database-specific syntax. Use sparingly - they bypass CBO intelligence and break when data distribution changes.	@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.status = :status")\n@QueryHints({\n    @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.fetchSize", value = "500"),\n    @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.readOnly", value = "true"),\n    @QueryHint(name = "jakarta.persistence.query.timeout", value = "5000")\n})\nList<Order> findByStatus(@Param("status") String status);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["query-hints", "jpa", "hibernate"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.016116	0
8cf8231d-6c90-450f-ad16-686e9dbf7096	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you analyze a slow query log to identify and prioritize optimization targets?	Enable slow query log (MySQL: long_query_time, PostgreSQL: log_min_duration_statement). Prioritize by: total time impact (frequency * avg duration) not just single-query duration. A 50ms query running 100K times/day costs more than a 5s query running 10 times. Use pt-query-digest (MySQL) or pgBadger (PostgreSQL) to aggregate. Look for: full table scans, filesorts, temp table creation, lock waits, and queries not using indexes. After fixing, verify with EXPLAIN ANALYZE and compare before/after metrics.	-- MySQL slow query log config\nSET GLOBAL slow_query_log = 'ON';\nSET GLOBAL long_query_time = 0.5;  -- 500ms threshold\nSET GLOBAL log_queries_not_using_indexes = 'ON';\n\n-- PostgreSQL: log queries over 200ms\nALTER SYSTEM SET log_min_duration_statement = 200;\nSELECT pg_reload_conf();\n\n-- Aggregate analysis (pt-query-digest output):\n-- Rank  Response time  Calls  R/Call  Query\n-- 1     45.2%  892s   98432  9.1ms   SELECT orders WHERE status=?\n-- 2     22.1%  436s      18  24.2s   SELECT ... GROUP BY ... HAVING\n	\N	HARD	["slow-query-log", "performance-tuning", "monitoring"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.018305	0
bbaef6b5-124d-4f6e-99fc-5e4703ad3ffe	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What are savepoints and how do they differ from full transaction rollback?	A savepoint marks a point within a transaction you can roll back to without aborting the entire transaction. SAVEPOINT creates the marker, ROLLBACK TO undoes work back to that point, RELEASE discards it. Unlike full ROLLBACK, the transaction remains active and prior work before the savepoint is preserved. Use cases: batch processing where individual items may fail, nested logical operations, and retry logic within a single transaction.	Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();\nconn.setAutoCommit(false);\nSavepoint sp = conn.setSavepoint("before_update");\ntry {\n    stmt.executeUpdate("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1");\n    stmt.executeUpdate("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2");\n} catch (SQLException e) {\n    conn.rollback(sp);  // only undo since savepoint\n}\nconn.commit();\n	\N	EASY	["savepoints", "rollback", "jdbc"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.024175	0
20021063-45a0-4d20-ae0a-37b25e1dbb53	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Explain MVCC and how it enables readers to never block writers.	Multi-Version Concurrency Control maintains multiple versions of each row, tagged with transaction IDs. Readers see a consistent snapshot based on their transaction start time, ignoring uncommitted or newer changes. Writers create new row versions rather than overwriting in place. This eliminates read-write locks entirely — readers access their snapshot while writers append new versions. Trade-off: dead tuples accumulate and require VACUUM (PostgreSQL) or purge threads (InnoDB) to reclaim space.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["mvcc", "concurrency", "postgresql", "innodb"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.027386	0
9b56df69-2039-4441-9a66-368d125c510c	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is a phantom read and which isolation levels prevent it?	A phantom read occurs when a transaction re-executes a range query and finds new rows inserted by another committed transaction. Example: SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE salary > 100k returns 5, then another transaction inserts a matching row, and re-query returns 6. READ COMMITTED and REPEATABLE READ (standard SQL) allow phantoms. SERIALIZABLE prevents them via predicate locking. PostgreSQL's REPEATABLE READ also prevents phantoms because it uses snapshot isolation (SSI), unlike MySQL which requires gap locks.	\N	\N	EASY	["phantom-read", "isolation-levels", "anomalies"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.029841	0
5173d5a5-355b-40b4-a996-a74728bcf4ca	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does deadlock detection work in relational databases and how do you prevent deadlocks?	Databases maintain a wait-for graph where nodes are transactions and edges represent lock waits. A cycle in this graph indicates deadlock. Detection runs periodically (InnoDB: every few seconds) or on each wait. On detection, the DB aborts one transaction (victim chosen by cost/age). Prevention strategies: acquire locks in consistent order, keep transactions short, use lock timeouts (innodb_lock_wait_timeout), reduce isolation level, use SELECT FOR UPDATE NOWAIT to fail fast instead of waiting.	// Consistent lock ordering prevents deadlock\n// Always lock accounts in ID order regardless of transfer direction\npublic void transfer(long fromId, long toId, BigDecimal amount) {\n    long first = Math.min(fromId, toId);\n    long second = Math.max(fromId, toId);\n    em.find(Account.class, first, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);\n    em.find(Account.class, second, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);\n    // now safe to update both\n}\n	\N	HARD	["deadlock", "locking", "prevention"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.032076	0
9354ef56-ded5-41d5-8ef1-ca73ae50c09c	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is the difference between dirty read and non-repeatable read?	Dirty read: reading data written by an uncommitted transaction — if that transaction rolls back, you read data that never existed. Non-repeatable read: reading the same row twice within a transaction and getting different values because another transaction committed an UPDATE between the two reads. Key difference: dirty reads involve uncommitted data (potential rollback), non-repeatable reads involve committed changes. READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads; REPEATABLE READ prevents both.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["dirty-read", "non-repeatable-read", "isolation-levels"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.034531	0
16162fe5-8c02-447f-bc23-57bc523a46c5	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does Spring's @Transactional propagation affect nested transaction behavior?	REQUIRED (default): joins existing transaction or creates new one. REQUIRES_NEW: suspends current transaction and creates a fresh one — inner commit/rollback is independent. NESTED: uses savepoint within existing transaction — inner rollback only undoes to savepoint, but outer rollback undoes everything including nested work. MANDATORY: throws if no existing transaction. Key pitfall: self-invocation bypasses the proxy, so @Transactional on a method called from the same class is ignored.	@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)\npublic void auditLog(String event) {\n    // commits even if calling transaction rolls back\n    auditRepo.save(new AuditEntry(event));\n}\n\n@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)\npublic void processItem(Item item) {\n    // uses savepoint; caller can catch exception and continue\n    itemRepo.save(transform(item));\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["spring", "propagation", "nested-transactions"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.037206	0
0f90c6df-ec66-44ae-8048-aa0c87f28b94	a5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is write skew and why does REPEATABLE READ not prevent it in standard SQL?	Write skew occurs when two transactions read overlapping data, make disjoint writes based on what they read, and both commit — leaving the database in an invalid state neither intended. Classic example: two on-call doctors both read 'two doctors on call', each decides to remove themselves, resulting in zero doctors. Standard REPEATABLE READ only locks rows actually read/written, not the predicate condition. Only SERIALIZABLE (via SSI in PostgreSQL or gap locks in MySQL) detects and aborts one transaction to prevent the anomaly.	-- Write skew example (both succeed under REPEATABLE READ)\n-- T1: SELECT count(*) FROM oncall WHERE shift='night'; -- returns 2\n-- T2: SELECT count(*) FROM oncall WHERE shift='night'; -- returns 2\n-- T1: DELETE FROM oncall WHERE doctor='Alice' AND shift='night';\n-- T2: DELETE FROM oncall WHERE doctor='Bob' AND shift='night';\n-- Both commit -> 0 doctors on call (constraint violated)\n-- Fix: SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;\n	\N	HARD	["write-skew", "serializable", "ssi"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.039633	0
8bf59982-a322-466d-8dfa-593e58a023c7	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How does the sliding window technique work? What problems does it solve?	Sliding window maintains a window [left, right] over a contiguous subarray, expanding right to include elements and shrinking left to maintain constraints. Converts brute-force O(n²) to O(n). Solves: maximum/minimum subarray of size k, longest substring without repeating characters, minimum window substring, maximum sum subarray of size k. Two variants: fixed-size window and variable-size window.	// Longest substring without repeating characters\nSet<Character> window = new HashSet<>();\nint left = 0, maxLen = 0;\nfor (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {\n    while (window.contains(s.charAt(right))) {\n        window.remove(s.charAt(left++));\n    }\n    window.add(s.charAt(right));\n    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["sliding-window", "subarray", "technique"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.047479	0
f9244efd-b1ed-4129-9d52-6ffa398661bd	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the prefix sum technique and how is it used for range queries?	Prefix sum precomputes cumulative sums so any subarray sum can be answered in O(1). prefix[i] = sum of elements from index 0 to i-1. Sum of range [l, r] = prefix[r+1] - prefix[l]. Build in O(n), query in O(1). Extends to 2D (prefix sum matrix for submatrix queries). Used in: range sum queries, counting subarrays with given sum, equilibrium index problems.	// Build prefix sum\nint[] prefix = new int[arr.length + 1];\nfor (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n    prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + arr[i];\n}\n// Query sum of range [l, r]\nint rangeSum = prefix[r + 1] - prefix[l];\n	\N	EASY	["prefix-sum", "range-query", "technique"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.049844	0
29141467-a871-41dd-af29-6702a99b072a	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain Kadane's algorithm for maximum subarray sum.	Kadane's algorithm finds the maximum sum contiguous subarray in O(n) time, O(1) space. Key insight: at each position, the maximum subarray ending here is either the current element alone or the current element plus the maximum subarray ending at the previous position. If the running sum becomes negative, reset it — a negative prefix can never improve a future subarray.	int maxSum = arr[0], currentSum = arr[0];\nfor (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\n    currentSum = Math.max(arr[i], currentSum + arr[i]);\n    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, currentSum);\n}\nreturn maxSum;\n	Variant: to find the actual subarray, track start/end indices. Reset start when currentSum resets to arr[i]. Update end when maxSum updates.	MEDIUM	["kadane", "maximum-subarray", "dynamic-programming"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.05223	0
b695df52-eb0f-45e2-85c5-20bff8391bff	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Describe the Dutch National Flag algorithm and its applications.	Dutch National Flag (3-way partition) sorts an array of three distinct values in a single pass using three pointers: low, mid, high. Elements before low are first value, between low and mid are second value, after high are third value. O(n) time, O(1) space. Applications: sort colors (LeetCode 75), partition around pivot in quicksort, segregate 0s/1s/2s.	// Sort array of 0s, 1s, 2s\nint low = 0, mid = 0, high = arr.length - 1;\nwhile (mid <= high) {\n    if (arr[mid] == 0) swap(arr, low++, mid++);\n    else if (arr[mid] == 1) mid++;\n    else swap(arr, mid, high--);\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["dutch-national-flag", "partitioning", "three-way"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.055777	0
4ea8e810-1666-4e16-b7f2-d1333615f24e	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How do you apply binary search on a sorted array and what are common pitfalls?	Binary search halves the search space each iteration: O(log n). Pitfalls: (1) Integer overflow in mid calculation — use left + (right - left) / 2. (2) Off-by-one in boundaries — decide inclusive [left, right] vs exclusive [left, right). (3) Infinite loops — ensure search space shrinks every iteration. (4) Variants: lower_bound (first >= target), upper_bound (first > target), search in rotated array.	// Lower bound: first index where arr[i] >= target\nint left = 0, right = arr.length;\nwhile (left < right) {\n    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;\n    if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;\n    else right = mid;\n}\nreturn left;\n	\N	EASY	["binary-search", "sorted-array", "lower-bound"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.058055	0
0a042aaa-06d5-4008-9d67-166c8b30dc74	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain the merge intervals algorithm and its approach.	Merge overlapping intervals: (1) Sort intervals by start time. (2) Iterate, maintaining a current merged interval. (3) If next interval overlaps (start <= current.end), extend current.end = max(current.end, next.end). (4) Otherwise, add current to result and start new interval. Time: O(n log n) for sort. Applications: meeting room scheduling, free time calculation, interval coverage.	Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);\nList<int[]> merged = new ArrayList<>();\nint[] current = intervals[0];\nfor (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {\n    if (intervals[i][0] <= current[1]) {\n        current[1] = Math.max(current[1], intervals[i][1]);\n    } else {\n        merged.add(current);\n        current = intervals[i];\n    }\n}\nmerged.add(current);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["merge-intervals", "sorting", "greedy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.06008	0
a5d67ef9-6266-41fc-8e70-279ce56fc3bb	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What techniques are used for matrix traversal problems?	Common techniques: (1) Spiral traversal — maintain four boundaries (top, bottom, left, right), shrink after each direction. (2) Diagonal traversal — iterate by sum of indices (i+j = constant). (3) BFS/DFS for connected components (island problems). (4) In-place marking for visited cells. (5) Direction arrays: dx={0,0,1,-1}, dy={1,-1,0,0} for 4-directional movement. Key: check bounds before accessing neighbors.	// 4-directional BFS on matrix\nint[] dx = {0, 0, 1, -1};\nint[] dy = {1, -1, 0, 0};\nQueue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();\nqueue.offer(new int[]{startRow, startCol});\nwhile (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n    int[] cell = queue.poll();\n    for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n        int nx = cell[0] + dx[d], ny = cell[1] + dy[d];\n        if (nx >= 0 && nx < rows && ny >= 0 && ny < cols\n            && !visited[nx][ny]) {\n            visited[nx][ny] = true;\n            queue.offer(new int[]{nx, ny});\n        }\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["matrix", "traversal", "bfs", "spiral"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.062353	0
d125599d-bf15-43d6-a5a9-99962431afa2	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is the difference between memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up) DP?	Memoization: recursive approach that caches results of subproblems as they're computed. Starts from the original problem and breaks down. Tabulation: iterative approach that fills a table from smallest subproblems up. Memoization is easier to implement and only solves needed subproblems. Tabulation avoids recursion overhead, has better cache locality, and can optimize space by keeping only needed rows/states.	\N	\N	EASY	["memoization", "tabulation", "top-down", "bottom-up"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.068906	0
07c2828f-b679-420d-89ec-4603b3893aba	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What are the two key properties that indicate a problem can be solved with DP?	1) Optimal substructure: the optimal solution contains optimal solutions to subproblems. (e.g., shortest path — subpaths of shortest path are also shortest). 2) Overlapping subproblems: the same subproblems are solved multiple times. (e.g., Fibonacci — fib(5) computes fib(3) twice). If only optimal substructure exists without overlap, greedy or divide-and-conquer may be better.	\N	To identify DP problems: look for optimization (min/max/count), choices at each step, and recursive structure where subproblems repeat. Common patterns: linear DP, interval DP, tree DP, bitmask DP, digit DP.	EASY	["optimal-substructure", "overlapping-subproblems", "identification"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.071397	0
15fb66ef-0e8d-4583-854a-0c3dc4412a8e	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Explain the 0/1 Knapsack problem and its DP solution.	Given n items with weights and values, and a knapsack capacity W, maximize total value without exceeding capacity. Each item is taken or not (0/1). DP state: dp[i][w] = max value using first i items with capacity w. Transition: dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], dp[i-1][w-weight[i]] + value[i]). Time: O(nW), Space: O(nW) reducible to O(W) since each row only depends on the previous row.	// Space-optimized 0/1 Knapsack\nint[] dp = new int[W + 1];\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    for (int w = W; w >= weight[i]; w--) { // reverse to avoid reuse\n        dp[w] = Math.max(dp[w], dp[w - weight[i]] + value[i]);\n    }\n}\nreturn dp[W];\n	\N	MEDIUM	["knapsack", "optimization", "classic"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.07396	0
11a14cc0-9c83-4d56-bb55-94c8144e119b	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) algorithm work?	Find the longest subsequence common to two strings. DP state: dp[i][j] = LCS length of first i chars of s1 and first j chars of s2. Transition: if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1], dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1; else dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]). Time: O(mn), Space: O(mn) reducible to O(min(m,n)). To reconstruct the actual subsequence, backtrack from dp[m][n].	int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\nfor (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n        if (s1.charAt(i-1) == s2.charAt(j-1))\n            dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;\n        else\n            dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);\n    }\n}\nreturn dp[m][n];\n	\N	MEDIUM	["lcs", "string", "subsequence", "classic"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.076475	0
22894331-208c-4e52-ba72-01c640852d2c	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Explain the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) problem and its O(n log n) solution.	Find the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence. O(n²) DP: dp[i] = LIS ending at index i. O(n log n) patience sorting: maintain an array 'tails' where tails[i] = smallest tail element for increasing subsequence of length i+1. For each element, binary search for its position in tails. If larger than all, append; otherwise replace the first element >= it. Final answer is tails.length.	// O(n log n) LIS\nList<Integer> tails = new ArrayList<>();\nfor (int num : arr) {\n    int pos = Collections.binarySearch(tails, num);\n    if (pos < 0) pos = -(pos + 1);\n    if (pos == tails.size()) tails.add(num);\n    else tails.set(pos, num);\n}\nreturn tails.size();\n	\N	HARD	["lis", "binary-search", "patience-sorting"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.078945	0
8915d061-1c0f-403b-925f-b99ecab30db3	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Describe the Coin Change problem and its DP formulation.	Given coin denominations and a target amount, find the minimum number of coins needed (or number of ways to make change). Min coins: dp[amount] = min(dp[amount - coin] + 1) for each coin. Initialize dp[0] = 0, rest = infinity. Count ways: dp[amount] += dp[amount - coin] for each coin. For unbounded supply, iterate coins in outer loop (combinations) or inner loop (permutations).	// Minimum coins\nint[] dp = new int[amount + 1];\nArrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);\ndp[0] = 0;\nfor (int coin : coins) {\n    for (int a = coin; a <= amount; a++) {\n        dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - coin] + 1);\n    }\n}\nreturn dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];\n	\N	MEDIUM	["coin-change", "unbounded-knapsack", "classic"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.081924	0
83e2fb23-3383-4fd7-b6b5-7a40fb1437ff	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is the state transition pattern for interval DP problems?	Interval DP solves problems on contiguous subarrays/substrings. State: dp[i][j] = optimal answer for interval [i, j]. Transition: try all split points k in [i, j-1]: dp[i][j] = optimize(dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + cost(i,k,j)). Iterate by increasing interval length. Examples: matrix chain multiplication, burst balloons, palindrome partitioning, optimal BST. Time typically O(n³).	// Matrix Chain Multiplication\nfor (int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n - len; i++) {\n        int j = i + len - 1;\n        dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        for (int k = i; k < j; k++) {\n            int cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j]\n                     + dims[i] * dims[k+1] * dims[j+1];\n            dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], cost);\n        }\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["interval-dp", "matrix-chain", "split-point"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.084495	0
cbebd05f-fc01-4f6d-80b8-0311497533d9	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Compare BFS and DFS traversal - when would you choose one over the other?	BFS explores level by level using a queue, O(V+E) time, O(V) space. DFS explores as deep as possible using a stack/recursion, O(V+E) time, O(V) space. BFS guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs and is preferred for level-order problems. DFS is better for topological sorting, cycle detection, pathfinding in mazes, and problems requiring backtracking. BFS uses more memory on wide graphs; DFS uses more on deep graphs.	// BFS from source node\nQueue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();\nboolean[] visited = new boolean[V];\nqueue.offer(src);\nvisited[src] = true;\nwhile (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n    int node = queue.poll();\n    for (int neighbor : adj.get(node)) {\n        if (!visited[neighbor]) {\n            visited[neighbor] = true;\n            queue.offer(neighbor);\n        }\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["bfs", "dfs", "traversal"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.090528	0
1317a9b5-77de-40bc-ba3f-40944eab0762	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain Dijkstra's algorithm and its limitations.	Dijkstra finds shortest paths from a single source to all vertices in a weighted graph with non-negative edges. Uses a priority queue (min-heap) to greedily select the closest unvisited vertex. Time complexity: O((V+E) log V) with a binary heap. It fails with negative edge weights because it assumes a shorter path cannot be found once a node is finalized. For negative weights, use Bellman-Ford O(VE).	PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[1] - b[1]);\nint[] dist = new int[V];\nArrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\ndist[src] = 0;\npq.offer(new int[]{src, 0});\nwhile (!pq.isEmpty()) {\n    int[] curr = pq.poll();\n    int u = curr[0], d = curr[1];\n    if (d > dist[u]) continue; // stale entry\n    for (int[] edge : adj.get(u)) { // {neighbor, weight}\n        int v = edge[0], w = edge[1];\n        if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {\n            dist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n            pq.offer(new int[]{v, dist[v]});\n        }\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["dijkstra", "shortest-path", "greedy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.093575	0
e46f18a5-3887-46f2-9ba9-398e37aaddf7	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How does Kahn's algorithm perform topological sort?	Kahn's algorithm uses BFS with in-degree tracking. Initialize a queue with all nodes having in-degree 0. Repeatedly dequeue a node, add to result, and decrement in-degree of its neighbors. If a neighbor's in-degree becomes 0, enqueue it. If the result size < V, the graph has a cycle. Time: O(V+E). Unlike DFS-based topological sort, Kahn's naturally detects cycles and produces a lexicographically smallest order when using a min-heap.	int[] inDegree = new int[V];\nfor (int u = 0; u < V; u++)\n    for (int v : adj.get(u)) inDegree[v]++;\nQueue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();\nfor (int i = 0; i < V; i++)\n    if (inDegree[i] == 0) queue.offer(i);\nList<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>();\nwhile (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n    int u = queue.poll();\n    order.add(u);\n    for (int v : adj.get(u))\n        if (--inDegree[v] == 0) queue.offer(v);\n}\nif (order.size() != V) throw new RuntimeException("Cycle detected");\n	\N	MEDIUM	["topological-sort", "dag", "kahns-algorithm"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.09674	0
ffdf1355-b0e5-4549-af30-9f604c134940	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain Union-Find with path compression and union by rank.	Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union) supports find and union operations on disjoint sets. Path compression flattens the tree during find by pointing each visited node directly to the root. Union by rank attaches the shorter tree under the taller tree's root. Together they achieve near-constant amortized time: O(alpha(n)) per operation where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function. Used in Kruskal's MST, detecting cycles in undirected graphs, and connected components.	int[] parent, rank;\nint find(int x) {\n    if (parent[x] != x)\n        parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // path compression\n    return parent[x];\n}\nvoid union(int x, int y) {\n    int px = find(x), py = find(y);\n    if (px == py) return;\n    if (rank[px] < rank[py]) parent[px] = py;\n    else if (rank[px] > rank[py]) parent[py] = px;\n    else { parent[py] = px; rank[px]++; }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["union-find", "disjoint-set", "path-compression"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.099543	0
28a18882-351e-43f2-a2df-90b469073de9	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Compare Kruskal's and Prim's MST algorithms.	Both find Minimum Spanning Trees. Kruskal's sorts all edges by weight and greedily adds edges that don't form cycles (using Union-Find), O(E log E). Prim's grows the MST from a starting vertex using a priority queue to pick the minimum edge connecting a visited to unvisited vertex, O((V+E) log V). Kruskal's is better for sparse graphs (E ~ V) since it processes edges globally. Prim's is better for dense graphs (E ~ V^2) especially with adjacency matrix representation.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["mst", "kruskal", "prim", "greedy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.102202	0
30e8d5e4-7c1a-45c7-b090-4f31aae4b311	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How do you detect cycles in directed vs undirected graphs?	Directed graphs: use DFS with three states (unvisited, in-stack, done). A back edge to an in-stack node indicates a cycle. Alternatively, if topological sort doesn't include all vertices, a cycle exists. Undirected graphs: during DFS/BFS, if you encounter a visited node that isn't the immediate parent, a cycle exists. Union-Find also works: if two vertices of an edge share the same root, adding that edge creates a cycle. Both approaches are O(V+E).\n	// Directed cycle detection with DFS coloring\nint[] color = new int[V]; // 0=white, 1=gray, 2=black\nboolean hasCycle(int u) {\n    color[u] = 1; // mark in-stack\n    for (int v : adj.get(u)) {\n        if (color[v] == 1) return true;  // back edge\n        if (color[v] == 0 && hasCycle(v)) return true;\n    }\n    color[u] = 2; // done\n    return false;\n}\n	\N	HARD	["cycle-detection", "dfs", "directed-graph", "undirected-graph"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.104369	0
d1879cb0-1e83-4ce6-88b0-afb330cec291	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	When would you use an adjacency list vs an adjacency matrix?	Adjacency list: O(V+E) space, efficient for sparse graphs (most real-world graphs). Iterating neighbors is O(degree). Adjacency matrix: O(V^2) space, better for dense graphs or when O(1) edge lookup is needed. Matrix enables fast edge existence checks and works well with matrix-based algorithms (Floyd-Warshall, transitive closure). List is preferred for BFS/DFS traversal. Matrix wastes memory when E << V^2 but is cache-friendly for small dense graphs.	\N	\N	EASY	["adjacency-list", "adjacency-matrix", "graph-representation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.10693	0
cb277f6e-0b06-46e1-a466-8984f308ebb8	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Why is merge sort preferred over quicksort for linked lists and external sorting?	Merge sort is stable and guarantees O(n log n) worst-case. For linked lists, merge is O(1) extra space since nodes are re-linked without copying. For external sorting (data exceeding RAM), merge sort naturally splits data into sorted runs on disk and merges them sequentially, minimizing random I/O. Quicksort's random-access partitioning is impractical when data doesn't fit in memory.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["merge-sort", "external-sorting", "stability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.11196	0
73432cfb-8cf3-4561-bfd6-9a5fcab32af8	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What pivot selection strategies exist for quicksort, and how do they affect worst-case performance?	Common strategies: (1) First/last element — O(n²) on sorted input. (2) Random pivot — expected O(n log n), worst-case still O(n²) but extremely unlikely. (3) Median-of-three (first, middle, last) — avoids worst-case on sorted/reverse-sorted input. (4) Introselect/median-of-medians — guarantees O(n log n) but high constant factor. Java's dual-pivot quicksort uses two pivots with insertion sort for small subarrays, achieving excellent cache performance.	// Median-of-three pivot selection\nint mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\nif (arr[mid] < arr[low]) swap(arr, low, mid);\nif (arr[high] < arr[low]) swap(arr, low, high);\nif (arr[high] < arr[mid]) swap(arr, mid, high);\nint pivot = arr[mid]; // median is now at mid\n	\N	MEDIUM	["quicksort", "pivot-selection", "time-complexity"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.114201	0
b10721f8-d57c-42f4-acb2-738e661dfe9f	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What sorting algorithm does Java's Arrays.sort() use and why?	For primitives, Arrays.sort() uses dual-pivot quicksort (Yaroslavskiy's algorithm) — not stable but fast with excellent cache locality. For objects, it uses TimSort (a hybrid merge sort + insertion sort) which is stable, preserving equal-element ordering required by the Comparable/Comparator contract. TimSort exploits existing runs (partially sorted sequences) in real-world data, achieving O(n) best-case.	\N	\N	EASY	["java-internals", "timsort", "arrays-sort"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.116262	0
c9a489f0-e367-45e6-adbc-73d8160a8c3b	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Explain counting sort and when it outperforms comparison-based sorts.	Counting sort counts occurrences of each value, then computes prefix sums to place elements in output. Runs in O(n + k) time where k is the value range. Outperforms comparison-based O(n log n) sorts when k = O(n). Ideal for: sorting integers in a known small range, character frequency problems, and as a subroutine of radix sort. Limitation: impractical when k >> n (sparse value ranges waste memory).	int[] count = new int[k + 1];\nfor (int x : arr) count[x]++;\n// prefix sum for stable placement\nfor (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) count[i] += count[i - 1];\nint[] output = new int[arr.length];\nfor (int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    output[--count[arr[i]]] = arr[i];\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["counting-sort", "non-comparison", "linear-time"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.118225	0
5b09186e-75c9-429c-b53d-ed8908cd3a2c	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What does sorting stability mean and when does it matter in practice?	A stable sort preserves the relative order of elements with equal keys. It matters when: (1) sorting objects by one field while preserving prior sort order on another field (multi-key sorting). (2) Database result ordering where ties must remain in insertion order. (3) Contractual correctness — Java's Comparator contract expects stable sorting for objects. Stable sorts: merge sort, TimSort, counting sort, insertion sort. Unstable: quicksort, heapsort, selection sort.	\N	\N	EASY	["stability", "sorting-properties", "java-contracts"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.120146	0
508b1b1d-59ad-42e7-80ca-8940e4486a14	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Prove that Java is pass-by-value, not pass-by-reference.	Java always passes a copy of the value. For primitives, it copies the value itself. For objects, it copies the reference (pointer), not the object. You can mutate the object through the copied reference, but you cannot make the caller's variable point to a different object. Swapping two object references inside a method has no effect on the caller.	void swap(String a, String b) {\n    String temp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = temp;\n}\n\nString x = "hello", y = "world";\nswap(x, y);\n// x is still "hello", y is still "world"\n// Only the local copies of references were swapped\n	\N	MEDIUM	["pass-by-value", "references", "method-parameters"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.24476	0
eeea969e-7656-44b8-a1ec-61f00cb84b02	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How does heapsort work and what are its trade-offs compared to merge sort and quicksort?	Heapsort builds a max-heap in O(n), then repeatedly extracts the maximum and sifts down. Guarantees O(n log n) worst-case with O(1) auxiliary space — the only comparison sort with both optimal time and constant space. Trade-offs: poor cache locality (jumps across array during sift), unstable, and roughly 2-3x slower than quicksort in practice. Used when guaranteed worst-case matters and extra memory is unavailable.	// Heapify subtree rooted at index i\nvoid siftDown(int[] arr, int i, int n) {\n    int largest = i;\n    int left = 2 * i + 1, right = 2 * i + 2;\n    if (left < n && arr[left] > arr[largest]) largest = left;\n    if (right < n && arr[right] > arr[largest]) largest = right;\n    if (largest != i) {\n        swap(arr, i, largest);\n        siftDown(arr, largest, n);\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["heapsort", "heap", "space-complexity"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.122425	0
04be74df-a9ba-43a1-87f2-b7522b6e55ff	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How does radix sort achieve linear time, and what is the relationship between its digit width and performance?	Radix sort processes digits from least-significant to most-significant (LSD) or vice versa (MSD), using a stable sub-sort (counting sort) per digit. For n integers with d digits in base b, time is O(d(n + b)). Choosing b = n gives O(d * n); for w-bit integers with d = w/log(n), total is O(n * w/log(n)) — sublinear in n*w. Trade-off: larger base reduces passes but increases counting array size. Optimal for fixed-width keys (IP addresses, strings of uniform length).	\N	\N	HARD	["radix-sort", "non-comparison", "complexity-analysis"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.125682	0
e2c624fc-8047-4cd8-bccf-00e46d029ee2	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does the KMP (Knuth-Morris-Pratt) algorithm achieve O(n+m) pattern matching?	KMP preprocesses the pattern to build a failure function (LPS array) indicating the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix for each position. On mismatch, instead of resetting to the beginning, the algorithm shifts the pattern using the LPS value, avoiding redundant comparisons. This guarantees O(m) preprocessing and O(n) matching, where n is text length and m is pattern length.	// Build LPS (Longest Prefix Suffix) array\nint[] buildLPS(String pattern) {\n    int[] lps = new int[pattern.length()];\n    int len = 0, i = 1;\n    while (i < pattern.length()) {\n        if (pattern.charAt(i) == pattern.charAt(len)) {\n            lps[i++] = ++len;\n        } else if (len > 0) {\n            len = lps[len - 1]; // don't increment i\n        } else {\n            lps[i++] = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return lps;\n}\n	\N	HARD	["pattern-matching", "kmp", "string-search"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.130568	0
e3b59e63-b90e-45f1-b78b-770413573d56	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Explain the Rabin-Karp algorithm and when you would prefer it over KMP.	Rabin-Karp uses a rolling hash to compare the pattern hash against substring hashes in O(1) per position. On hash match, it verifies character-by-character to handle collisions. Average case is O(n+m) but worst case is O(nm) due to hash collisions. Prefer Rabin-Karp over KMP for multi-pattern search (checking multiple patterns simultaneously) since you can compare against multiple hashes in one pass.	// Rolling hash: remove leading char, add trailing char\nlong BASE = 31, MOD = 1_000_000_007;\n// hash("bcd") from hash("abc") when window shifts right:\nhash = (hash - s.charAt(left) * basePow) % MOD;\nhash = (hash * BASE + s.charAt(right)) % MOD;\n	\N	MEDIUM	["rolling-hash", "rabin-karp", "pattern-matching"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.132795	0
72e27810-54a5-4db8-9cf1-c0dc1560b86f	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does a Trie support efficient prefix matching? What are its time and space complexities?	A Trie stores characters along edges so that common prefixes share nodes. Searching for a prefix of length k takes O(k) time regardless of how many words are stored. Insertion is O(k) per word. Space is O(ALPHABET_SIZE * N * L) in the worst case where N is word count and L is average length. Tries excel at autocomplete, spell-checking, and IP routing (longest prefix match).	class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n    boolean isEnd;\n}\nvoid insert(String word) {\n    TrieNode node = root;\n    for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\n        int idx = c - 'a';\n        if (node.children[idx] == null)\n            node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();\n        node = node.children[idx];\n    }\n    node.isEnd = true;\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["trie", "prefix-search", "data-structure"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.13503	0
01000930-dc37-4705-9abd-07748ada8436	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What are the common approaches to detect if a string is a palindrome?	Three main approaches: (1) Two-pointer from both ends moving inward, comparing characters - O(n) time, O(1) space. (2) Reverse and compare - O(n) time and space. (3) For substring palindrome problems, expand around center for each of 2n-1 centers (each char + each gap) - O(n^2) total. For longest palindromic substring, Manacher's algorithm achieves O(n) using previously computed palindrome radii.	// Expand around center - handles both odd and even length\nString expandAroundCenter(String s, int left, int right) {\n    while (left >= 0 && right < s.length()\n            && s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)) {\n        left--;\n        right++;\n    }\n    return s.substring(left + 1, right);\n}\n	\N	EASY	["palindrome", "two-pointers", "expand-around-center"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.137145	0
75f1a4a0-9476-49e0-ac2c-bdd54635d5b8	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Compare sorting-based vs frequency-map approaches for checking anagrams.	Sorting: sort both strings and compare. O(n log n) time, O(n) space for the sorted copies. Simple but slower. Frequency map: count character frequencies in one string, decrement for the other, check all counts are zero. O(n) time, O(1) space (fixed 26-char alphabet). Frequency map is preferred for interviews. For follow-ups like 'find all anagram substrings', combine frequency map with sliding window.	// O(n) frequency count approach\nboolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {\n    if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;\n    int[] freq = new int[26];\n    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\n        freq[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;\n        freq[t.charAt(i) - 'a']--;\n    }\n    for (int count : freq)\n        if (count != 0) return false;\n    return true;\n}\n	\N	EASY	["anagram", "frequency-map", "sorting"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.139143	0
cbe0a50e-cc53-4afc-9953-dad82b020c18	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does polynomial string hashing work and what are its collision pitfalls?	Polynomial hashing maps a string to an integer: hash(s) = sum(s[i] * p^i) mod M, where p is a prime base (31 or 37 for lowercase) and M is a large prime modulus. Collisions occur when two distinct strings produce the same hash. Mitigation: use double hashing (two different base/mod pairs) reducing collision probability to ~1/M^2. Birthday paradox means collisions become likely after ~sqrt(M) strings, so M should be at least 10^9.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["hashing", "polynomial-hash", "collision"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.141229	0
