72839f7e-9f67-4f55-b07b-df32c2fb7da9	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Compare in-order, pre-order, post-order, and level-order tree traversals.	In-order (Left, Root, Right): produces sorted sequence for BST. Pre-order (Root, Left, Right): useful for tree serialization and copying. Post-order (Left, Right, Root): useful for deletion and evaluating expression trees. Level-order (BFS): visits nodes level by level using a queue. All DFS traversals are O(n) time, O(h) space (recursion stack). Level-order is O(n) time, O(w) space where w is max width.	// Iterative in-order using stack\nStack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();\nTreeNode curr = root;\nwhile (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {\n    while (curr != null) {\n        stack.push(curr);\n        curr = curr.left;\n    }\n    curr = stack.pop();\n    result.add(curr.val); // visit\n    curr = curr.right;\n}\n	\N	EASY	["traversal", "inorder", "preorder", "postorder", "bfs"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.151578	0
6b57405c-f76c-4207-8fff-a52a58c4b28f	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How do AVL trees and Red-Black trees maintain balance? Compare them.	AVL: strictly balanced (height difference between subtrees <= 1). Uses rotations (single/double) after insert/delete. Faster lookups due to stricter balance. Red-Black: relaxed balance via coloring rules (root is black, no two consecutive reds, equal black-height on all paths). Fewer rotations on insert/delete (max 2 rotations for insert, max 3 for delete). AVL better for read-heavy workloads; Red-Black better for write-heavy (used in Java TreeMap, Linux kernel).	\N	\N	HARD	["avl", "red-black-tree", "balanced-tree", "rotations"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.153659	0
e9825851-1bc0-4514-88ed-b8590dc65814	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Explain the Trie data structure and its applications.	A Trie (prefix tree) stores strings character-by-character in a tree where each edge represents a character. Each node may mark end-of-word. Operations (insert, search, prefix search) are O(m) where m is string length, independent of number of stored strings. Applications: autocomplete, spell checking, IP routing (longest prefix match), word games, phone directories.	class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n    boolean isEndOfWord;\n}\n// Insert word\nvoid insert(String word) {\n    TrieNode node = root;\n    for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\n        int idx = c - 'a';\n        if (node.children[idx] == null)\n            node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();\n        node = node.children[idx];\n    }\n    node.isEndOfWord = true;\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["trie", "prefix-tree", "string", "autocomplete"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.155606	0
27318804-e84d-4fca-a9f8-adee4986442e	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is a Segment Tree and what problems does it solve?	A Segment Tree is a binary tree where each node stores aggregate information (sum, min, max) for a range of array elements. Supports range queries and point/range updates in O(log n). Build: O(n). Applications: range sum/min/max queries with updates, counting inversions, interval scheduling. Lazy propagation enables O(log n) range updates by deferring updates to children until needed.	// Build segment tree for range sum\nvoid build(int[] arr, int node, int start, int end) {\n    if (start == end) {\n        tree[node] = arr[start];\n    } else {\n        int mid = (start + end) / 2;\n        build(arr, 2*node, start, mid);\n        build(arr, 2*node+1, mid+1, end);\n        tree[node] = tree[2*node] + tree[2*node+1];\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["segment-tree", "range-query", "lazy-propagation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.158002	0
8f4f95f3-113f-4a43-acc4-a27766af0273	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How do you find the Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) of two nodes?	For BST: traverse from root — if both nodes are smaller, go left; if both larger, go right; otherwise current node is LCA. O(h). For general binary tree: (1) Recursive — if current node is either target, return it; recurse left and right; if both return non-null, current is LCA. O(n). (2) Binary Lifting — precompute 2^k-th ancestors for O(log n) queries after O(n log n) preprocessing. (3) Euler tour + RMQ for O(1) queries.	// LCA in binary tree (recursive)\nTreeNode lca(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {\n    if (root == null || root == p || root == q) return root;\n    TreeNode left = lca(root.left, p, q);\n    TreeNode right = lca(root.right, p, q);\n    if (left != null && right != null) return root;\n    return left != null ? left : right;\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["lca", "binary-tree", "bst", "ancestor"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.160118	0
16e6e61c-a6d7-4047-a4ce-e0e703924c3f	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How do you serialize and deserialize a binary tree?	Serialization converts a tree to a string; deserialization reconstructs it. Common approaches: (1) Pre-order with null markers — serialize as 'val,left,right' with '#' for null. Deserialize by reading tokens sequentially. (2) Level-order (BFS) with null markers. (3) Pre-order + in-order (no null markers needed, but requires unique values). Pre-order with nulls is simplest: O(n) time and space for both operations.	// Serialize: pre-order with null markers\nString serialize(TreeNode root) {\n    if (root == null) return "#";\n    return root.val + "," + serialize(root.left) + "," + serialize(root.right);\n}\n// Deserialize\nTreeNode deserialize(Queue<String> tokens) {\n    String val = tokens.poll();\n    if ("#".equals(val)) return null;\n    TreeNode node = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(val));\n    node.left = deserialize(tokens);\n    node.right = deserialize(tokens);\n    return node;\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["serialization", "binary-tree", "preorder"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.162072	0
