8640a2fa-7c50-4758-bcef-95a1e1e2db49	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is the difference between Iterator's fail-fast and fail-safe behavior?	Fail-fast iterators (ArrayList, HashMap) throw ConcurrentModificationException if the collection is structurally modified during iteration (detected via modCount). Fail-safe iterators (ConcurrentHashMap, CopyOnWriteArrayList) work on a snapshot or tolerate concurrent modification without throwing exceptions. Fail-safe iterators may not reflect the latest state of the collection.	// Fail-fast: throws ConcurrentModificationException\nList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b", "c"));\nfor (String s : list) {\n    list.remove(s); // throws!\n}\n\n// Safe removal via Iterator\nIterator<String> it = list.iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    if (it.next().equals("b")) it.remove(); // OK\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["iterator", "fail-fast", "concurrency"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.17982	0
0b9d4867-9342-45bb-8e74-404f65893daf	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What does Collections.unmodifiableList() return and what are its limitations?	Returns an unmodifiable VIEW of the original list. Calling add/remove/set on the wrapper throws UnsupportedOperationException. However, if the backing list is modified directly, changes ARE visible through the unmodifiable view. For a truly immutable copy, use List.copyOf() (Java 10+) or new ArrayList<>(original) wrapped in unmodifiableList. The returned list also does not support structural operations via its listIterator.	List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b"));\nList<String> unmod = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);\n// unmod.add("c"); // throws UnsupportedOperationException\noriginal.add("c");\nSystem.out.println(unmod.size()); // prints 3!\n\n// Truly immutable:\nList<String> immutable = List.copyOf(original);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["immutability", "collections-utility", "unmodifiable"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.181894	0
c36d1228-2cae-4ea9-afc3-2cd208847131	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does PriorityQueue work internally and what is its time complexity?	PriorityQueue is backed by a binary min-heap (array-based). Elements are ordered by natural ordering or a provided Comparator. offer/add: O(log n) - sift up. poll/remove: O(log n) - sift down. peek: O(1). remove(Object): O(n) - linear search + sift. It is NOT thread-safe; use PriorityBlockingQueue for concurrent access. Does NOT guarantee order of iteration.	PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();\npq.offer(5); pq.offer(1); pq.offer(3);\npq.poll(); // returns 1 (smallest)\npq.poll(); // returns 3\n\n// Max-heap via Comparator\nPriorityQueue<Integer> maxPQ = new PriorityQueue<>(\n    Comparator.reverseOrder()\n);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["priorityqueue", "heap", "data-structures"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.18384	0
4a420de1-a425-4cd8-8a04-f98d6eafc4c6	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is LinkedHashMap and how does it maintain ordering?	LinkedHashMap extends HashMap and maintains a doubly-linked list connecting all entries in insertion order (default) or access order (with accessOrder=true in constructor). Access-order mode moves entries to the tail on get/put, making it suitable for LRU cache implementations. Override removeEldestEntry() to auto-evict the oldest entry when the map exceeds a certain size.	// LRU Cache using LinkedHashMap\nLinkedHashMap<String, String> lru = new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true) {\n    @Override\n    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<String, String> eldest) {\n        return size() > 100; // max 100 entries\n    }\n};\n	\N	HARD	["linkedhashmap", "lru-cache", "ordering"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.185952	0
04b52d45-3468-4bdd-ad94-9657ad2643d8	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What happens when you put a key into a HashMap that already exists?	The existing value is replaced with the new value, and the OLD value is returned from put(). The key's hashCode() and equals() determine whether it's the 'same' key. The key object itself is NOT replaced - only the value. If the key is new, put() returns null. putIfAbsent() only inserts if the key is absent or mapped to null, returning the existing value otherwise.	Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();\nInteger prev1 = map.put("a", 1); // returns null (new key)\nInteger prev2 = map.put("a", 2); // returns 1 (old value)\n\nmap.putIfAbsent("a", 3); // returns 2, map unchanged\nmap.putIfAbsent("b", 3); // returns null, "b"->3 inserted\n	\N	EASY	["hashmap", "put", "basics"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.188449	0
1e1359e1-ca67-4b66-b857-e07f26fe4713	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What does the synchronized keyword do and what are its forms?	synchronized provides mutual exclusion and happens-before guarantees. Three forms: 1) synchronized method - locks 'this' (instance) or Class object (static). 2) synchronized block - locks specified object. 3) Only one thread can hold a given monitor at a time. It's reentrant: a thread can re-acquire a lock it already holds. Also establishes memory visibility - changes made in a synchronized block are visible to subsequent synchronized blocks on the same monitor.	// Instance method lock (locks 'this')\npublic synchronized void increment() { count++; }\n\n// Block with explicit lock object\npublic void update() {\n    synchronized (this.lock) {\n        // critical section\n    }\n}\n\n// Static method lock (locks Class object)\npublic static synchronized void staticMethod() { }\n	\N	EASY	["synchronized", "monitor", "locking"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.193644	0
64b7276c-327c-4b9d-bf46-7388b55f56d9	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What does volatile guarantee and what doesn't it guarantee?	volatile guarantees: 1) Visibility - writes are immediately visible to all threads (no CPU cache staleness). 2) Ordering - prevents reordering of reads/writes around the volatile access (memory barrier). Does NOT guarantee: atomicity of compound operations (e.g., volatile int count; count++ is still not atomic because it's read-modify-write). Use AtomicInteger or synchronized for compound operations.	// Correct: single read/write visibility\nprivate volatile boolean running = true;\n\n// WRONG: volatile doesn't make this atomic\nprivate volatile int count = 0;\npublic void increment() {\n    count++; // read + modify + write = not atomic!\n}\n\n// Correct for compound ops:\nprivate AtomicInteger atomicCount = new AtomicInteger(0);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["volatile", "visibility", "memory-model"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.196175	0
d5a5c5d3-2a47-40c5-9367-a07828837239	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does ReentrantLock differ from synchronized?	ReentrantLock advantages: 1) tryLock() with timeout - non-blocking attempt. 2) lockInterruptibly() - can be interrupted while waiting. 3) Fairness policy (FIFO ordering of waiting threads). 4) Multiple Condition objects for fine-grained wait/notify. 5) Can be unlocked in a different scope than locked. Disadvantages: must manually unlock in finally block (error-prone), slightly more verbose. Both are reentrant.	ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock(true); // fair lock\n\npublic void doWork() {\n    lock.lock();\n    try {\n        // critical section\n    } finally {\n        lock.unlock(); // MUST be in finally\n    }\n}\n\n// Non-blocking attempt\nif (lock.tryLock(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {\n    try { /* work */ } finally { lock.unlock(); }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["reentrantlock", "lock", "synchronized-comparison"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.198276	0
172d8800-6995-4544-903b-55f4fdf4cd51	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is ExecutorService and what are the common thread pool types?	ExecutorService manages a pool of threads for async task execution. Common factories: 1) newFixedThreadPool(n) - fixed number of threads, unbounded queue. 2) newCachedThreadPool() - creates threads on demand, reuses idle threads (60s timeout). 3) newSingleThreadExecutor() - single thread, tasks execute sequentially. 4) newScheduledThreadPool(n) - for delayed/periodic tasks. Submit returns Future<T>. Always shutdown() when done to prevent resource leaks.	ExecutorService exec = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);\n\nFuture<String> future = exec.submit(() -> {\n    return computeResult();\n});\n\nString result = future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n\nexec.shutdown();\nexec.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n	\N	EASY	["executorservice", "thread-pool", "executors"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.200501	0
389fcd96-9802-48e2-8707-e2a8d025ca92	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does CompletableFuture improve over plain Future?	CompletableFuture adds: 1) Non-blocking chaining with thenApply/thenCompose/thenAccept. 2) Combining multiple futures (allOf, anyOf, thenCombine). 3) Exception handling (exceptionally, handle, whenComplete). 4) Can be completed manually (complete(), completeExceptionally()). 5) Runs on ForkJoinPool.commonPool() by default, or custom executor via *Async variants. Plain Future only offers blocking get().	CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchUser(id))\n    .thenApply(user -> enrichProfile(user))\n    .thenCompose(profile -> saveAsync(profile))\n    .exceptionally(ex -> {\n        log.error("Failed", ex);\n        return fallbackProfile();\n    })\n    .thenAccept(result -> notifyUser(result));\n\n// Combine two futures\nCompletableFuture<String> combined = future1\n    .thenCombine(future2, (a, b) -> a + b);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["completablefuture", "async", "functional"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.202572	0
629f4e13-4eb5-45df-95bb-20437cb95aea	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is ThreadLocal and when should you use it?	ThreadLocal provides thread-confined storage - each thread gets its own independent copy of the variable. Common uses: per-thread context (user session, transaction), SimpleDateFormat (not thread-safe), database connections. Pitfall: memory leaks in thread pools - threads are reused so ThreadLocal values persist. Always call remove() in a finally block or after use. InheritableThreadLocal passes values to child threads.	private static final ThreadLocal<SimpleDateFormat> dateFormat =\n    ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd"));\n\npublic String formatDate(Date date) {\n    return dateFormat.get().format(date);\n}\n\n// CRITICAL: clean up in thread pools\ntry {\n    context.set(userData);\n    processRequest();\n} finally {\n    context.remove(); // prevent memory leak\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["threadlocal", "thread-safety", "memory-leak"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.204738	0
a41680ed-e309-42cc-81e1-347307c0968d	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is CountDownLatch and how does it differ from CyclicBarrier?	CountDownLatch: one-shot synchronizer. Threads call await() to block until count reaches zero via countDown() calls. Cannot be reused. Use case: wait for N initialization tasks to complete. CyclicBarrier: reusable synchronizer. N threads call await() and ALL block until all N arrive, then all proceed together. Can be reused for repeated phases. CyclicBarrier also supports a barrier action (Runnable) executed when all parties arrive.	// CountDownLatch: main waits for workers\nCountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(3);\nfor (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    executor.submit(() -> {\n        doWork();\n        latch.countDown();\n    });\n}\nlatch.await(); // blocks until count = 0\n\n// CyclicBarrier: threads synchronize at a point\nCyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(3, () -> merge());\n// Each thread calls barrier.await() when ready\n	\N	MEDIUM	["countdownlatch", "cyclicbarrier", "synchronizers"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.206739	0
0933c3f3-8c89-4b2f-9b34-48588f329130	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does ConcurrentHashMap's compute operations help avoid race conditions?	compute(), computeIfAbsent(), computeIfPresent(), and merge() are atomic operations that lock only the relevant bucket. They eliminate check-then-act races that occur with separate get() + put() calls. computeIfAbsent() is ideal for caches: it only computes the value if key is absent, atomically. The mapping function should be short and not update other entries of the same map (risk of deadlock).	ConcurrentHashMap<String, List<String>> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n// Atomic: create list if absent, then add\nmap.computeIfAbsent("key", k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>())\n   .add("value");\n\n// Atomic increment\nmap.merge("counter", 1L, Long::sum);\n\n// WRONG: race condition with separate calls\nif (!map.containsKey("k")) { // another thread may insert here\n    map.put("k", newValue);\n}\n	\N	HARD	["concurrenthashmap", "atomic-operations", "race-condition"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.20884	0
334d259d-6deb-4bea-a866-7a97db52fc9b	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is AtomicInteger and how does CAS (Compare-And-Swap) work?	AtomicInteger provides lock-free thread-safe integer operations using CAS. CAS atomically: reads current value, compares to expected, sets new value only if current == expected, otherwise retries. Operations: incrementAndGet(), getAndAdd(), compareAndSet(). Implemented via sun.misc.Unsafe / VarHandle. Advantages over synchronized: no context switching, no deadlock possibility. Disadvantage: busy-spinning under high contention (LongAdder is better for counters).	AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);\n\ncounter.incrementAndGet(); // atomic i++\ncounter.compareAndSet(1, 2); // set to 2 only if currently 1\ncounter.updateAndGet(x -> x * 2); // atomic transform\n\n// For high-contention counters, prefer:\nLongAdder adder = new LongAdder();\nadder.increment(); // striped, less contention\nadder.sum(); // read current total\n	\N	HARD	["atomic", "cas", "lock-free"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.210894	0
0260029b-f186-42f3-b7cb-f4ac16676fc2	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is the difference between deadlock and livelock?	Deadlock: two+ threads permanently blocked, each waiting for a lock held by the other. No progress ever. Example: T1 holds lock A, waits for B; T2 holds lock B, waits for A. Livelock: threads are not blocked but make no progress because they keep responding to each other's actions. Example: two people in a corridor repeatedly stepping aside in the same direction. Starvation: a thread never gets CPU/lock due to priority or unfairness. Prevention: lock ordering (deadlock), backoff with jitter (livelock).	\N	\N	EASY	["deadlock", "livelock", "starvation", "concurrency-problems"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.213822	0
e2b4e0aa-5959-4b9f-8cc9-0882d2e7c6db	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions in Java?	Checked exceptions extend Exception (not RuntimeException) and must be declared in throws clause or caught at compile time. Unchecked exceptions extend RuntimeException and have no compile-time enforcement. Checked exceptions represent recoverable conditions (IOException, SQLException); unchecked represent programming errors (NullPointerException, IllegalArgumentException).\n	\N	\N	EASY	["checked-exceptions", "unchecked-exceptions", "exception-hierarchy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.218036	0
1fc4e3b4-1b65-4d4c-8042-ea13b6daf178	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Explain the exception class hierarchy starting from Throwable.	Throwable is the root with two direct subclasses: Error and Exception. Error represents unrecoverable JVM problems (OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError) and should not be caught. Exception splits into checked exceptions (IOException, SQLException) and RuntimeException which is the parent of all unchecked exceptions. Only Throwable and its subclasses can be thrown or caught.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["exception-hierarchy", "throwable", "error-vs-exception"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.220851	0
e4edfa67-b121-4d99-a804-ef89a29d52a5	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How does try-with-resources work and what interface must a resource implement?	Resources declared in the try parentheses must implement AutoCloseable (or its parent Closeable). They are closed automatically in reverse declaration order after the try block completes, whether normally or via exception. This eliminates manual finally-block cleanup and guarantees close is called even if the try body throws.\n	try (var conn = dataSource.getConnection();\n     var stmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n    return stmt.executeQuery();\n} // stmt closed first, then conn\n	\N	MEDIUM	["try-with-resources", "autocloseable", "resource-management"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.224517	0
03f6fef3-33c0-4e06-8206-d06e592d72c9	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What are suppressed exceptions and when do they occur?	Suppressed exceptions occur when a secondary exception is thrown during resource cleanup in try-with-resources while a primary exception is already propagating. The close() exception is attached to the primary via addSuppressed() rather than replacing it. You retrieve them with getSuppressed(). This preserves the original failure context which would be lost in traditional finally blocks where the close exception overwrites the original.\n	try {\n    Throwable primary = ex;\n    for (Throwable suppressed : primary.getSuppressed()) {\n        log.warn("Suppressed during close", suppressed);\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["suppressed-exceptions", "try-with-resources", "exception-chaining"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.228301	0
1a5eb7c2-d1b2-4c22-965f-3f95ef103f99	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What happens if both the try block and finally block throw exceptions?	The exception from the finally block propagates and the try block exception is silently lost. This is a key design flaw in pre-Java-7 resource handling. The caller only sees the finally exception with no reference to the original cause. Try-with-resources solves this via the suppressed exception mechanism, preserving both exceptions.\n	try {\n    throw new IOException("original"); // LOST\n} finally {\n    throw new IllegalStateException("from finally"); // this propagates\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["finally-block", "exception-loss", "control-flow"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.230806	0
9100343d-db77-46b0-a530-67802cb57040	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What are best practices for designing custom exception classes?	Extend Exception for recoverable conditions clients should handle, RuntimeException for programming errors. Provide constructors matching Throwable conventions: no-arg, message, message+cause, and cause-only. Make exceptions immutable and include contextual fields (error codes, failed entity IDs) as final fields. Prefer specific exception types over generic ones to enable precise catch blocks. Always chain the original cause via initCause or constructor parameter.\n	public class OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {\n    private final String orderId;\n\n    public OrderNotFoundException(String orderId, Throwable cause) {\n        super("Order not found: " + orderId, cause);\n        this.orderId = orderId;\n    }\n\n    public String getOrderId() { return orderId; }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["custom-exceptions", "exception-design", "best-practices"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.233088	0
c27936e9-756d-4cd6-905d-0c6077c68511	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the pitfalls of autoboxing between Integer and int?	Autoboxing can cause NullPointerException when unboxing a null Integer. It also causes subtle bugs with == comparison: Integer caches values -128 to 127, so == works for small values but fails for larger ones. Autoboxing in tight loops creates unnecessary object allocations and GC pressure.	Integer a = 127, b = 127;\nSystem.out.println(a == b);  // true (cached)\n\nInteger c = 128, d = 128;\nSystem.out.println(c == d);  // false (new objects)\n\nInteger x = null;\nint y = x; // NullPointerException at runtime\n	\N	MEDIUM	["autoboxing", "wrapper-types", "equality"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.237929	0
8f5be60c-a66f-4400-af71-f84460f7aa9d	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How does the String pool work and why are Strings immutable in Java?	The String pool (intern pool) is a special memory area in the heap where Java stores unique String literals. When you create a literal, JVM checks the pool first and reuses existing instances. Immutability enables safe sharing without defensive copying, allows caching of hashCode, and makes Strings inherently thread-safe. Strings created with new keyword bypass the pool unless intern() is called.	String s1 = "hello";       // pool\nString s2 = "hello";       // same reference from pool\nString s3 = new String("hello"); // heap, not pool\n\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s2);       // true\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s3);       // false\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s3.intern()); // true\n	\N	EASY	["string-pool", "immutability", "memory"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.240257	0
a9b9e309-a760-4cc4-b5cc-8d90237765f9	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is type erasure in Java generics and what problems does it cause?	Type erasure removes generic type information at compile time, replacing type parameters with their bounds (or Object if unbounded). This means you cannot use instanceof with generics, cannot create generic arrays, cannot call new T(), and cannot overload methods differing only in generic type. Bridge methods are generated to preserve polymorphism.	// At runtime, both are just List (raw type)\nList<String> strings = new ArrayList<>();\nList<Integer> ints = new ArrayList<>();\n// strings.getClass() == ints.getClass() -> true\n\n// Cannot do:\n// if (obj instanceof List<String>) {}  // compile error\n// T[] arr = new T[10];                 // compile error\n\n// These are the same erasure - won't compile together:\n// void process(List<String> list) {}\n// void process(List<Integer> list) {}\n	\N	HARD	["generics", "type-erasure", "compile-time"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.247197	0
fb99d979-d705-4168-8844-05a5ad9ffd07	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What does the static keyword mean in different contexts?	Static field: shared across all instances, belongs to the class. Static method: callable without an instance, cannot access instance members or use 'this'. Static block: runs once when the class is loaded, used for complex static initialization. Static inner class: does not hold a reference to the enclosing instance, preventing memory leaks. Static import: allows using static members without class qualification.	class Counter {\n    static int count = 0;       // shared across instances\n    static { count = loadFromDB(); } // class-load init\n\n    static int getCount() { return count; }\n\n    // Non-static inner holds reference to outer (memory leak risk)\n    class Inner {}\n    // Static nested does not\n    static class Nested {}\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["static", "class-loading", "memory"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.249235	0
50fccb7a-60a8-407b-948d-e1919e02484c	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the difference between primitives and wrapper types regarding memory, nullability, and usage in collections?	Primitives (int, long, double, etc.) live on the stack, cannot be null, and have no methods. Wrappers (Integer, Long, Double) are heap-allocated objects, can be null, and provide utility methods like parseInt(). Collections require wrappers since generics don't support primitives. A wrapper uses 16+ bytes of heap vs 4 bytes for an int. In performance-critical code, prefer primitives or specialized collections (e.g., IntStream, Eclipse Collections IntList).	\N	\N	HARD	["primitives", "wrappers", "memory-layout", "performance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.251313	0
e4bd77c2-8281-412c-8c9a-59b62c9c30a9	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	When should you prefer composition over inheritance?	Prefer composition when you need flexible behavior reuse without coupling to a parent's implementation.\nInheritance creates a rigid is-a relationship and breaks encapsulation (subclass depends on parent internals).\nComposition allows swapping strategies at runtime, avoids fragile base class problem, and keeps classes focused.\n	// Composition: delegate to a strategy\nclass OrderProcessor {\n    private final PricingStrategy pricing; // inject behavior\n    OrderProcessor(PricingStrategy pricing) { this.pricing = pricing; }\n    double calculate(Order o) { return pricing.apply(o); }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["oop", "composition", "inheritance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.256079	0
58db1522-e5fa-45f3-947b-1deeb0add6da	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do default methods in interfaces differ from abstract class methods?	Interfaces support multiple inheritance of type and behavior (a class can implement many interfaces with defaults).\nAbstract classes provide single inheritance but can hold state (instance fields) and constructors.\nDefault methods cannot access instance state and are meant for backward-compatible API evolution, not shared implementation logic.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["interfaces", "abstract-class", "java8"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.258154	0
f1c4f3ff-0f63-44b1-93e3-31bff3b2777c	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does Java resolve the diamond problem with default methods?	If a class inherits the same default method from two interfaces, the compiler forces an explicit override.\nRules: class methods win over interface defaults; more-specific interface wins (sub-interface over parent).\nIf neither rule resolves ambiguity, the class must override and can delegate via InterfaceName.super.method().\n	interface A { default void greet() { System.out.println("A"); } }\ninterface B extends A { default void greet() { System.out.println("B"); } }\ninterface C extends A { default void greet() { System.out.println("C"); } }\nclass D implements B, C {\n    public void greet() { B.super.greet(); } // must resolve explicitly\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["diamond-problem", "default-methods", "interfaces"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.261523	0
cf137333-9314-4d4e-b799-7e8d7469ab8c	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Explain runtime polymorphism and how the JVM dispatches virtual method calls.	Runtime polymorphism uses dynamic dispatch: the JVM resolves the method to invoke based on the actual object type,\nnot the declared reference type. Each object's class has a vtable (virtual method table) mapping method signatures\nto implementations. At call sites, invokevirtual looks up the receiver's class vtable to find the correct target method.\n	Animal a = new Dog(); // reference type: Animal, actual type: Dog\na.speak(); // invokevirtual dispatches to Dog.speak()\n	\N	MEDIUM	["polymorphism", "dynamic-dispatch", "jvm"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.263842	0
e7f1ca15-8748-461e-bc2e-ed5876c9ac24	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Explain the Liskov Substitution Principle with a common Java violation.	LSP states subtypes must be substitutable for their base type without altering program correctness.\nClassic violation: making Square extend Rectangle and overriding setWidth/setHeight to keep sides equal\nbreaks clients that expect independent width/height mutations. Fix: use immutable value objects or separate\nthe hierarchy into a Shape interface with area() rather than mutable dimension setters.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["solid", "lsp", "inheritance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.26601	0
d3c2b4fa-ad91-40de-b4c2-564ac9b687ad	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What are sealed classes in Java 17 and when would you use them?	Sealed classes restrict which classes may extend them using the permits clause.\nPermitted subclasses must be final, sealed, or non-sealed. This enables exhaustive pattern matching\nin switch expressions and models closed domain hierarchies (e.g., AST nodes, command types)\nwhere the set of subtypes is known at compile time.\n	public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Triangle {}\npublic record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}\npublic record Rectangle(double w, double h) implements Shape {}\npublic final class Triangle implements Shape { /*...*/ }\n\n// Exhaustive switch (no default needed)\ndouble area = switch (shape) {\n    case Circle c    -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();\n    case Rectangle r -> r.w() * r.h();\n    case Triangle t  -> computeTriangle(t);\n};\n	\N	HARD	["sealed-classes", "java17", "pattern-matching"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.268287	0
118fb6ff-e346-4870-800e-570e5db24c21	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does encapsulation interact with the Open/Closed Principle in Java design?	Encapsulation hides internal state behind well-defined interfaces, enabling OCP: classes are open for extension\n(new behavior via new implementations or decorators) but closed for modification (existing code unchanged).\nIn practice, program to interfaces, use package-private visibility for internals, and extend behavior\nthrough composition/strategy rather than modifying existing classes.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["encapsulation", "solid", "ocp"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.270568	0
8d554385-0fe1-457f-a3f2-79bb5dad5ff9	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How does lazy evaluation work in Java Streams?	Intermediate operations (map, filter, flatMap) are not executed when declared.\nThey form a pipeline that only runs when a terminal operation (collect, forEach, reduce) is called.\nThe runtime fuses operations and processes elements one-by-one, enabling short-circuiting (findFirst, limit).\n	// filter and map are NOT executed here\nStream<String> lazy = list.stream().filter(s -> s.length() > 3).map(String::toUpperCase);\n// execution starts only here\nList<String> result = lazy.collect(Collectors.toList());\n	\N	EASY	["streams", "lazy-evaluation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.278238	0
f30ade7e-bf89-4e9d-887e-82f045b32ac8	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain the difference between map() and flatMap().	map() applies a function to each element producing a 1-to-1 transformation (Stream<T> → Stream<R>).\nflatMap() applies a function that returns a Stream per element, then flattens all resulting streams\ninto a single Stream. Use flatMap when each input maps to zero or many outputs.\n	// map: one-to-one\nList<String> upper = words.stream().map(String::toUpperCase).toList();\n\n// flatMap: one-to-many (split each line into words)\nList<String> allWords = lines.stream()\n    .flatMap(line -> Arrays.stream(line.split(" ")))\n    .toList();\n	\N	MEDIUM	["streams", "flatMap", "transformation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.280399	0
a0d85f4e-0fae-457e-8591-e96526cef60c	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How does reduce() work and what is the role of the identity value?	reduce() combines stream elements into a single result using a BinaryOperator.\nThe identity is the initial accumulator value and must be a true identity for the operator\n(e.g., 0 for addition, 1 for multiplication). If no identity is provided, reduce returns Optional\nsince the stream may be empty.\n	int sum = numbers.stream().reduce(0, Integer::sum);\nOptional<Integer> max = numbers.stream().reduce(Integer::max);\n	\N	MEDIUM	["streams", "reduce", "functional"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.282831	0
84b0d8d8-0017-4fe1-a146-a5348475151e	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What are the main pitfalls of parallel streams?	Parallel streams use the common ForkJoinPool (shared across the JVM), so blocking operations starve\nother tasks. They add overhead for small datasets. Non-associative or stateful operations produce\nwrong results. Shared mutable state causes race conditions. Order-dependent logic breaks unless\nthe stream is explicitly ordered. Use parallel only for CPU-bound work on large datasets.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["streams", "parallel", "concurrency", "performance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.285151	0
114396ea-4f56-4463-ae2e-c8e12fbbc5c1	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Name three useful Collectors beyond toList() and explain when to use them.	groupingBy() — partition elements into a Map by classifier (e.g., group employees by department).\njoining() — concatenate String elements with delimiter/prefix/suffix.\ntoMap() — build a Map with key and value extractors; requires merge function for duplicate keys.\nAlso: partitioningBy (boolean split), summarizingInt (stats), collectingAndThen (post-processing).\n	Map<Department, List<Employee>> byDept =\n    employees.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment));\n\nString csv = names.stream().collect(Collectors.joining(", "));\n\nMap<Long, String> idToName = employees.stream()\n    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Employee::getId, Employee::getName));\n	\N	MEDIUM	["streams", "collectors", "grouping"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.287401	0
e5600150-eb78-45d4-8a49-623cd8a600ec	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How should Optional be used correctly in Java, and what are common anti-patterns?	Use Optional as a return type for methods that may have no result — never as a field, parameter,\nor collection element. Chain with map/flatMap/orElseGet for transformations. Anti-patterns: calling\nget() without isPresent(), using Optional.of(null), wrapping values just to unwrap immediately,\nand using Optional in serialization (it's not Serializable).\n	// Good: chained transformation\nString city = findUser(id)\n    .flatMap(User::getAddress)\n    .map(Address::getCity)\n    .orElse("Unknown");\n\n// Bad: isPresent + get (defeats the purpose)\nif (opt.isPresent()) { return opt.get(); }\n	\N	HARD	["optional", "anti-patterns", "api-design"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.29233	0
0bd0a40c-68ae-4189-a2bb-7982c2c3b935	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What does the /actuator/health endpoint return by default, and how do you show full details?	By default it returns a simple {"status":"UP"} response. To show component-level details,\nset management.endpoint.health.show-details=always (or when-authorized for authenticated users).\nEach HealthIndicator contributes its own status which is aggregated by a StatusAggregator.\n	// application.properties\nmanagement.endpoint.health.show-details=always\nmanagement.endpoint.health.show-components=always\n	\N	EASY	["spring-boot", "actuator", "health-check"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.297572	0
1e4a5d63-1a26-424d-8e5a-ea055882bfbb	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How do you create a custom HealthIndicator in Spring Boot?	Implement the HealthIndicator interface and return Health.up() or Health.down() with optional\ndetails. Register it as a Spring bean. The name prefix before "HealthIndicator" becomes the\ncomponent key in the health response.\n	@Component\npublic class CacheHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {\n    @Override\n    public Health health() {\n        if (cacheService.isReachable()) {\n            return Health.up()\n                .withDetail("hitRate", cacheService.getHitRate())\n                .build();\n        }\n        return Health.down()\n            .withDetail("error", "Cache unreachable")\n            .build();\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["spring-boot", "health-indicator", "custom-component"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.299829	0
c21543d1-d55a-477f-860e-30902090990c	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How does Spring Boot integrate with Prometheus for metrics collection?	Add micrometer-registry-prometheus dependency. Spring Boot auto-configures a\nPrometheusMeterRegistry and exposes a /actuator/prometheus endpoint in Prometheus\ntext format. Prometheus scrapes this endpoint at configured intervals. All Micrometer\nmeters (timers, counters, gauges) are automatically exported.\n	// build.gradle\nimplementation 'io.micrometer:micrometer-registry-prometheus'\n\n// application.properties\nmanagement.endpoints.web.exposure.include=prometheus,health\nmanagement.prometheus.metrics.export.enabled=true\n	\N	MEDIUM	["micrometer", "prometheus", "monitoring"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.301963	0
79cab55b-2ba2-40ef-9c5f-fe4b77c75451	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How do you register a custom metric using Micrometer in Spring Boot?	Inject MeterRegistry and use its factory methods (counter, gauge, timer). Timers measure\nlatency, counters track monotonically increasing values, gauges track current values.\nUse tags for dimensional filtering in dashboards.\n	@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final Counter orderCounter;\n    private final Timer processTimer;\n\n    public OrderService(MeterRegistry registry) {\n        this.orderCounter = registry.counter("orders.placed", "region", "eu");\n        this.processTimer = registry.timer("orders.process.duration");\n    }\n\n    public void placeOrder(Order order) {\n        processTimer.record(() -> {\n            // process order\n            orderCounter.increment();\n        });\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["micrometer", "custom-metrics", "observability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.30396	0
a0953a31-9e3d-4730-ac8a-ea12b29ddc46	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How do you secure actuator endpoints in a Spring Boot application?	Use Spring Security to restrict access. Expose only needed endpoints via\nmanagement.endpoints.web.exposure.include. Optionally run actuator on a separate\nmanagement port (management.server.port) isolated from public traffic. Apply role-based\naccess via HttpSecurity requestMatchers for the actuator base path.\n	@Bean\npublic SecurityFilterChain actuatorSecurity(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n    return http\n        .securityMatcher(EndpointRequest.toAnyEndpoint())\n        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n            .requestMatchers(EndpointRequest.to("health", "info")).permitAll()\n            .anyRequest().hasRole("ADMIN"))\n        .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults())\n        .build();\n}\n	\N	HARD	["spring-security", "actuator", "production-hardening"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.305964	0
a3582a70-102d-48ff-ba35-f17b3fbc78de	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	How do you customize the /actuator/info endpoint with build and git metadata?	Spring Boot auto-populates info from META-INF/build-info.properties (generated by\nspring-boot-maven-plugin's build-info goal) and git.properties (from git-commit-id-plugin).\nCustom properties under info.* in application.properties also appear. Enable the endpoint\nwith management.info.env.enabled=true for environment contributions.\n	// application.properties\ninfo.app.name=@project.name@\ninfo.app.version=@project.version@\nmanagement.info.env.enabled=true\nmanagement.info.git.mode=full\n	\N	EASY	["actuator", "info-endpoint", "build-metadata"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.309158	0
8895e76d-13b9-41f2-bf83-545e67222e51	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the Spring IoC container and how does it manage beans?	The IoC (Inversion of Control) container manages object creation, wiring, and lifecycle. It uses dependency injection instead of objects creating their own dependencies. The container is represented by ApplicationContext (eager initialization) or BeanFactory (lazy). It reads configuration from annotations (@Component, @Configuration), XML, or Java config, creates beans as singletons (default), resolves dependencies, and calls lifecycle callbacks (@PostConstruct, @PreDestroy).	\N	\N	EASY	["ioc", "container", "beans", "dependency-injection"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.314651	0
544d4a20-da5c-4d7b-ba66-be3d9b80fdd1	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Why is constructor injection preferred over field injection in Spring?	Constructor injection advantages: 1) Immutability - fields can be final. 2) Required dependencies are explicit - cannot create an instance without them. 3) Testability - easy to pass mocks without reflection or Spring context. 4) Prevents circular dependencies at startup (fails fast). Field injection (@Autowired on fields) hides dependencies, allows partial initialization, requires reflection for testing, and can mask design smells (too many dependencies).	// Preferred: constructor injection\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway payment;\n    private final InventoryService inventory;\n    \n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway payment, InventoryService inventory) {\n        this.payment = payment;\n        this.inventory = inventory;\n    }\n}\n\n// Avoid: field injection\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    @Autowired private PaymentGateway payment; // hidden dependency\n}\n	\N	EASY	["constructor-injection", "field-injection", "best-practices"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.317317	0
226c74b1-e6d1-4910-bacb-e23c0f36fd7e	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the difference between @Configuration and @Component?	@Configuration classes use CGLIB proxying so that @Bean method calls between beans in the same class return the SAME singleton instance (inter-bean references). @Component classes do NOT get this proxying - calling a @Bean method directly creates a NEW instance each time. @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods=false) disables proxying for performance (lite mode) but loses inter-bean reference guarantee.	@Configuration\npublic class AppConfig {\n    @Bean\n    public DataSource dataSource() { return new HikariDataSource(); }\n    \n    @Bean\n    public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate() {\n        // Calls dataSource() but returns SAME singleton due to CGLIB proxy\n        return new JdbcTemplate(dataSource());\n    }\n}\n\n@Component // No CGLIB proxy!\npublic class LiteConfig {\n    @Bean\n    public Foo foo() { return new Foo(bar()); } // bar() creates NEW instance!\n    @Bean\n    public Bar bar() { return new Bar(); }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["configuration", "component", "cglib", "proxy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.319469	0
ba4ee51b-4a09-4d90-9dbb-547155454d08	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the Spring bean lifecycle callbacks and their order?	Creation order: 1) Constructor. 2) Dependency injection (setters/@Autowired). 3) BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization. 4) @PostConstruct. 5) InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet(). 6) Custom init-method. 7) BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization. Destruction order: 1) @PreDestroy. 2) DisposableBean.destroy(). 3) Custom destroy-method. @PostConstruct/@PreDestroy are JSR-250 annotations, preferred over interfaces.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["bean-lifecycle", "postconstruct", "predestroy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.321845	0
8038afa3-21d3-44a1-8c49-dd8690b623e3	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How do Spring profiles work and how do you activate them?	Profiles allow conditional bean registration and configuration. Beans annotated with @Profile('dev') are only created when that profile is active. Activation: 1) spring.profiles.active=dev in application.properties. 2) SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE env variable. 3) Programmatically via SpringApplication.setAdditionalProfiles(). Profile-specific config files: application-{profile}.yaml override application.yaml. Multiple profiles can be active simultaneously. @Profile supports expressions: @Profile('!prod'), @Profile({'dev','test'}).	\N	\N	EASY	["profiles", "environment", "configuration"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.324385	0
a0325882-76a6-41c7-a5ed-8b641ea42c97	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is @ConditionalOnProperty and other @Conditional annotations?	@ConditionalOnProperty creates a bean only if a property has a specific value. Part of Spring Boot's auto-configuration system. Other conditionals: @ConditionalOnClass (class on classpath), @ConditionalOnMissingBean (no existing bean of type), @ConditionalOnBean (bean exists), @ConditionalOnExpression (SpEL), @ConditionalOnWebApplication. Custom conditions implement the Condition interface. These enable Boot's opinionated defaults that back off when you provide your own config.	@Configuration\n@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "feature.cache.enabled", havingValue = "true")\npublic class CacheConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @ConditionalOnMissingBean\n    public CacheManager cacheManager() {\n        return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager();\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["conditional", "auto-configuration", "property"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.327333	0
8cae4003-0d22-4d64-b197-f909ef0ed98c	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How does Spring Boot auto-configuration work?	Auto-configuration classes are listed in META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports (Boot 3.x) or spring.factories (Boot 2.x). On startup, Spring loads all listed classes but @Conditional annotations filter which actually apply. @EnableAutoConfiguration (part of @SpringBootApplication) triggers this. Auto-config backs off via @ConditionalOnMissingBean - if you define your own DataSource bean, Boot's auto-configured one won't be created. Use --debug flag to see what was applied/excluded.	\N	\N	HARD	["auto-configuration", "spring-factories", "boot-internals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.329796	0
cefb24ff-c67f-4e4c-893b-f75d19913180	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the property resolution order in Spring Boot?	Highest to lowest priority: 1) Command-line args. 2) SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON. 3) OS environment variables. 4) application-{profile}.properties (outside jar). 5) application-{profile}.properties (inside jar). 6) application.properties (outside jar). 7) application.properties (inside jar). 8) @PropertySource annotations. 9) Default properties. Profile-specific always beats non-profile. Outside jar beats inside jar. .properties and .yaml are equivalent priority.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["property-resolution", "externalized-config", "precedence"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.332001	0
8b023dde-f3db-4ac1-b91f-02401a9ae2e7	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the difference between @Value and @ConfigurationProperties?	@Value: injects individual properties, supports SpEL expressions, no type safety, no validation, no IDE auto-completion for custom props. @ConfigurationProperties: binds a prefix to a POJO, type-safe, supports @Validated with JSR-303 annotations, relaxed binding (kebab-case maps to camelCase), IDE metadata support, immutable via @ConstructorBinding. Prefer @ConfigurationProperties for groups of related properties.	// @Value - individual properties\n@Value("${server.port:8080}")\nprivate int port;\n\n// @ConfigurationProperties - structured binding\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.mail")\n@Validated\npublic record MailProperties(\n    @NotBlank String host,\n    int port,\n    @Email String from,\n    Duration timeout\n) {}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["value", "configuration-properties", "type-safety"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.334237	0
a3dd01a2-e3ee-48fd-9dc2-24fe08309096	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the key Spring Boot Actuator endpoints and their purpose?	Key endpoints: /health - application health (DB, disk, custom indicators). /info - build info, git info. /metrics - Micrometer metrics (JVM, HTTP, custom). /env - environment properties (sanitized). /beans - all Spring beans. /mappings - all @RequestMapping endpoints. /loggers - view/change log levels at runtime. /threaddump - JVM thread dump. Security: expose selectively via management.endpoints.web.exposure.include. Health details: management.endpoint.health.show-details=when-authorized.	\N	\N	EASY	["actuator", "monitoring", "health", "metrics"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.336503	0
f967806b-e58b-4ba5-98ab-be495f780955	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What does Spring Data JPA provide through repository interfaces, and what is the role of @Repository?	Spring Data JPA auto-generates CRUD implementations from interfaces extending JpaRepository or CrudRepository.\n@Repository marks the class as a persistence layer component and enables automatic translation of JPA exceptions into Spring's DataAccessException hierarchy.\nWith Spring Boot, @Repository is optional on interfaces extending Spring Data base interfaces since they are auto-detected.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["spring-data", "repositories"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.341068	0
fa5479af-1001-40c2-af0d-0219125afcf8	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How do derived query methods work in Spring Data JPA? What are their limitations?	Spring Data parses method names like findByStatusAndCreatedDateAfter() and generates JPQL at startup.\nSupports keywords like Between, Like, In, OrderBy, and property traversal via underscores.\nLimitations: complex joins are unreadable, no support for subqueries or GROUP BY, and method names become unwieldy beyond 2-3 conditions.\n	List<Order> findByStatusAndTotalGreaterThan(Status status, BigDecimal total);\nOptional<User> findFirstByEmailIgnoreCase(String email);\nList<Product> findByCategory_NameOrderByPriceDesc(String categoryName);\n	\N	EASY	["spring-data", "query-methods"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.343174	0
9e1559e7-8e3a-4427-8c6f-43bbffc56387	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does @Query differ when using JPQL vs native SQL, and when would you choose each?	@Query with JPQL operates on entity objects and is database-agnostic, validated at startup.\nNative queries use raw SQL with nativeQuery=true, useful for database-specific features like window functions or CTEs.\nJPQL for portability and type safety; native for performance-critical queries or features outside JPA spec.\n	@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.customer.id = :custId AND o.status = :status")\nList<Order> findOrders(@Param("custId") Long custId, @Param("status") Status status);\n\n@Query(value = "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL '7 days'",\n       nativeQuery = true)\nList<Order> findRecentNative();\n	\N	MEDIUM	["spring-data", "jpql", "native-query"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.345349	0
ea3102e7-7f8c-452f-b0bd-c021cf055fc9	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Explain pagination and sorting in Spring Data JPA. How do you implement a paginated endpoint?	Pass Pageable parameter to repository methods to get a Page<T> with content, total elements, and page metadata.\nSpring MVC auto-resolves Pageable from request params (page, size, sort).\nPage executes a count query; use Slice<T> if you only need hasNext without total count for better performance.\n	Page<User> findByActive(boolean active, Pageable pageable);\n\n// Controller\n@GetMapping("/users")\nPage<User> list(@PageableDefault(size = 20, sort = "name") Pageable pageable) {\n    return userRepository.findByActive(true, pageable);\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["spring-data", "pagination", "sorting"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.3477	0
94e9d419-1a3d-443d-b5be-8bedbde24dc8	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Explain @Transactional propagation levels. What happens with REQUIRES_NEW vs REQUIRED?	REQUIRED (default) joins the existing transaction or creates one if none exists.\nREQUIRES_NEW suspends the current transaction and creates a new independent one; the outer transaction does not see inner changes until inner commits.\nUse REQUIRES_NEW for audit logging or operations that must commit regardless of outer rollback.\nKey pitfall: self-invocation bypasses the proxy, so @Transactional on a method called internally from the same bean is ignored.\n	@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)\npublic void logAuditEvent(AuditEvent event) {\n    auditRepository.save(event); // commits even if caller rolls back\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["transactions", "propagation", "spring"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.350286	0
810d860d-4749-41b7-87f1-330ee8744e09	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is the N+1 problem in JPA and how do you solve it?	N+1 occurs when loading an entity triggers one query for the parent and N additional queries for each lazy-loaded association.\nSolutions: JOIN FETCH in JPQL forces eager loading in a single query; @EntityGraph declaratively specifies attributes to fetch eagerly; @BatchSize(size=N) batches lazy loads into IN clauses.\nJOIN FETCH risks cartesian products with multiple collections; use Set or @Fetch(SUBSELECT) for multiple bags.\n	@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.customer.id = :custId")\nList<Order> findWithItems(@Param("custId") Long custId);\n\n@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"items", "items.product"})\nList<Order> findByStatus(Status status);\n	\N	HARD	["n-plus-one", "performance", "jpa"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.352829	0
8ed813fe-7b24-479e-814c-2560d23b77cf	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does Spring Boot's test auto-configuration and slice isolation work internally?	Each @...Test annotation imports a specific set of auto-configuration classes via META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.*.imports files. @WebMvcTest imports WebMvcAutoConfiguration, MockMvcAutoConfiguration, and Jackson but excludes DataSource and JPA. This selective import is controlled by @ImportAutoConfiguration and @OverrideAutoConfiguration(enabled=false) which disables full auto-configuration. Custom slices can be created by combining @TypeExcludeFilters with a custom AutoConfigureX annotation.	\N	Understanding slice internals helps debug why a bean is missing in a test - check if its auto-configuration is included in that slice's imports file.	HARD	["auto-configuration", "test-slices", "spring-internals"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.393626	0
4a906048-6b84-42a8-af4a-9beba7e79bce	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does Hibernate's first-level cache work and when can it cause issues?	First-level cache (persistence context) is session-scoped, storing managed entities by ID. Repeated findById calls within the same transaction hit the cache, not the DB.\nIssues: stale reads when another process modifies data mid-transaction; memory pressure with large batch operations loading thousands of entities.\nFix stale reads with entityManager.refresh() or clear(). Fix batch memory with periodic flush() and clear() in chunks.\n	@Transactional\npublic void batchUpdate(List<Long> ids) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < ids.size(); i++) {\n        Entity e = repo.findById(ids.get(i)).orElseThrow();\n        e.setProcessed(true);\n        if (i % 50 == 0) {\n            entityManager.flush();\n            entityManager.clear(); // evict first-level cache\n        }\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["hibernate", "caching", "persistence-context"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.355882	0
bb86bf20-7f62-4ddf-a7c9-882f70875ba9	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How do you configure a SecurityFilterChain in Spring Boot 3.x and why was WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter removed?	Spring Boot 3.x uses component-based configuration where you define a SecurityFilterChain @Bean directly. WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter was removed because it forced single-inheritance and made multiple security configurations awkward. The new approach uses HttpSecurity as a builder parameter, supports multiple SecurityFilterChain beans with @Order, and aligns with Spring's general move toward functional bean definitions.	@Configuration\n@EnableWebSecurity\npublic class SecurityConfig {\n    @Bean\n    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n                .requestMatchers("/api/public/**").permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))\n            .build();\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["security-filter-chain", "configuration", "spring-boot-3"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.360077	0
a8542ed1-90bb-4dbc-a729-3198ec40ba70	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain the difference between authentication and authorization in Spring Security and where each is handled in the filter chain.	Authentication verifies identity (who are you?) and is handled by filters early in the chain like UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter or BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter. Authorization verifies permissions (what can you do?) and is handled by AuthorizationFilter at the end of the chain. Authentication produces an Authentication object stored in SecurityContextHolder; authorization reads its GrantedAuthority collection to make access decisions.	\N	\N	EASY	["authentication", "authorization", "filter-chain"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.361826	0
86273f06-d362-45c0-9a14-fb154e224917	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Describe the JWT token validation flow in a Spring Boot OAuth2 resource server.	When a request arrives with a Bearer token: 1) BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter extracts the token from the Authorization header. 2) JwtAuthenticationProvider delegates to JwtDecoder (typically NimbusJwtDecoder). 3) JwtDecoder fetches the JWK Set from the authorization server's JWKS endpoint (cached), verifies the signature, checks expiration/issuer/audience claims. 4) A JwtAuthenticationConverter maps claims to GrantedAuthority objects. 5) The resulting JwtAuthenticationToken is stored in SecurityContext.	@Bean\npublic JwtDecoder jwtDecoder() {\n    NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder\n        .withJwkSetUri("https://auth-server/.well-known/jwks.json")\n        .build();\n    decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\n        JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer("https://auth-server"),\n        new JwtClaimValidator<>("aud", aud -> aud.contains("my-api"))\n    ));\n    return decoder;\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["jwt", "oauth2-resource-server", "token-validation"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.363858	0
7593ba43-2c7a-4347-b2ac-2971869379ef	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	When should you disable CSRF protection in Spring Security and why is it safe to do so for REST APIs?	Disable CSRF for stateless REST APIs that use token-based auth (JWT/OAuth2). CSRF attacks exploit browser-stored session cookies to forge requests. If authentication relies on an Authorization header (not cookies), the browser never auto-attaches credentials to cross-origin requests, making CSRF impossible. Keep CSRF enabled for traditional server-rendered apps with session cookies or login forms.	http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // stateless JWT API\n\n// Or selectively disable for API paths only:\nhttp.csrf(csrf -> csrf\n    .ignoringRequestMatchers("/api/**")\n)\n	\N	MEDIUM	["csrf", "stateless", "rest-api"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.365953	0
4a20d852-1efb-45d8-8b9f-216ac19a867d	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How does @PreAuthorize work and what is required to enable it? Give an example using SpEL.	Method-level security with @PreAuthorize evaluates a SpEL expression before method execution. Requires @EnableMethodSecurity on a configuration class (replaces @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity). Expressions access the Authentication object, method parameters, and custom beans. Fails with AccessDeniedException (403) if expression returns false.	@EnableMethodSecurity\n@Configuration\npublic class MethodSecurityConfig {}\n\n@Service\npublic class DocumentService {\n    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or #userId == authentication.principal.id")\n    public Document getDocument(Long userId, Long docId) { ... }\n\n    @PreAuthorize("@accessChecker.canEdit(#doc, authentication)")\n    public void updateDocument(Document doc) { ... }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["method-security", "preauthorize", "spel"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.368217	0
e105fb56-1455-4d07-bdc4-6b93feb7f390	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Why does Spring Security use BCryptPasswordEncoder by default and how does it prevent rainbow table attacks?	BCrypt is an adaptive hashing function that incorporates a random salt (stored in the hash output) and a configurable cost factor. Each hash includes a unique 16-byte salt, so identical passwords produce different hashes, defeating rainbow tables. The cost factor (default 10, meaning 2^10 rounds) makes brute-force expensive. DelegatingPasswordEncoder wraps BCrypt and supports migration from older schemes by prefixing hashes with {bcrypt}, {scrypt}, etc.	@Bean\npublic PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {\n    return PasswordEncoderFactories.createDelegatingPasswordEncoder();\n    // Encodes as: {bcrypt}$2a$10$salt...hash\n}\n\n// Or explicit BCrypt with strength 12:\n@Bean\npublic PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {\n    return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12);\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["bcrypt", "password-encoder", "hashing"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.370982	0
c665be52-70fa-4f0d-b9f4-13dbd6e667da	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is the difference between @RestController and @Controller?	@RestController is a composed annotation combining @Controller and @ResponseBody.\n@Controller returns view names resolved by ViewResolver, while @RestController\nserializes return values directly to the HTTP response body via HttpMessageConverters.\n	@RestController\n@RequestMapping("/api")\npublic class UserController {\n    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")\n    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {\n        return userService.findById(id); // serialized to JSON\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["spring-mvc", "annotations"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.398066	0
68c4bf01-51d2-4d12-9d07-b7fcfb37c682	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How would you configure a custom OAuth2 resource server that validates tokens from multiple issuers with different JWKS endpoints?	Use a custom JwtDecoder that delegates to issuer-specific decoders based on the token's 'iss' claim. Parse the JWT header without verifying to extract the issuer, then route to the matching NimbusJwtDecoder. Each decoder caches its own JWK Set. Alternatively, use Spring's JwtIssuerAuthenticationManagerResolver which maps issuer URIs to AuthenticationManagers lazily, each with its own JwtDecoder and validator.	@Bean\npublic SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n    JwtIssuerAuthenticationManagerResolver resolver =\n        JwtIssuerAuthenticationManagerResolver.fromTrustedIssuers(\n            "https://auth-server-a.com",\n            "https://auth-server-b.com"\n        );\n    return http\n        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth.anyRequest().authenticated())\n        .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2\n            .authenticationManagerResolver(resolver))\n        .build();\n}\n	\N	HARD	["multi-tenant", "oauth2", "jwt-issuer-resolver"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.373305	0
320795c0-4e10-41b3-a898-6afb1080a480	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What does @SpringBootTest do and when should you avoid it?	@SpringBootTest loads the full application context including all beans, auto-configurations, and component scanning. It starts the entire Spring container, making it suitable for integration tests that need the real wiring. Avoid it for unit tests or narrow slice tests because it is slow - loading hundreds of beans when you only need a controller or repository wastes CI time and obscures test intent.	@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)\nclass OrderIntegrationTest {\n    @Autowired\n    private TestRestTemplate restTemplate;\n\n    @Test\n    void shouldCreateOrder() {\n        ResponseEntity<Order> response = restTemplate.postForEntity(\n            "/api/orders", new OrderRequest("item-1", 2), Order.class);\n        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["spring-boot-test", "integration-testing"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.377772	0
296a1018-ad71-4b9d-91b3-b9e7ed405573	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does @WebMvcTest differ from @SpringBootTest and what does it auto-configure?	@WebMvcTest is a test slice that loads only the web layer - controllers, @ControllerAdvice, filters, converters, and MockMvc. It does NOT load @Service, @Repository, or @Component beans. You must provide dependencies via @MockBean. This makes it fast and focused for testing request mapping, validation, serialization, and error handling without the full context overhead.	@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)\nclass OrderControllerTest {\n    @Autowired\n    private MockMvc mockMvc;\n\n    @MockBean\n    private OrderService orderService;\n\n    @Test\n    void shouldReturn404WhenOrderNotFound() throws Exception {\n        when(orderService.findById(99L)).thenReturn(Optional.empty());\n\n        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/99"))\n            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());\n    }\n}\n	\N	EASY	["web-mvc-test", "test-slices", "mock-mvc"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.380283	0
5893ad7d-7da8-4029-a18a-a11e2dc8265f	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is the difference between @MockBean and @Mock in Spring Boot tests?	@MockBean is a Spring Boot test annotation that creates a Mockito mock AND registers it in the application context, replacing any existing bean of that type. @Mock (Mockito) creates a plain mock with no Spring context awareness. Use @MockBean in slice tests (@WebMvcTest, @SpringBootTest) where the mock must be injected by Spring. Use @Mock in pure unit tests with no Spring context for faster execution.	\N	@MockBean also resets the context cache if the mock type differs from the original bean, which can slow down test suites if overused across different test classes.	MEDIUM	["mockbean", "mockito", "test-context"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.382391	0
313c2168-567c-4df5-bbfd-c42a43a81bf9	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does @DataJpaTest work and what makes it suitable for repository testing?	@DataJpaTest configures an embedded database, scans for @Entity classes, and configures Spring Data JPA repositories. It applies @Transactional by default so each test rolls back. It does NOT load controllers, services, or other non-JPA components. You can override the embedded DB with @AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE) to test against a real database like PostgreSQL via TestContainers.	@DataJpaTest\nclass UserRepositoryTest {\n    @Autowired\n    private TestEntityManager entityManager;\n\n    @Autowired\n    private UserRepository userRepository;\n\n    @Test\n    void shouldFindByEmail() {\n        entityManager.persistAndFlush(new User("dev@example.com", "Dev"));\n\n        Optional<User> found = userRepository.findByEmail("dev@example.com");\n        assertThat(found).isPresent()\n            .hasValueSatisfying(u -> assertThat(u.getName()).isEqualTo("Dev"));\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["data-jpa-test", "repository-testing", "test-slices"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.385745	0
acc3a033-890f-4710-bcd2-ebc86b1ea661	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do you use MockMvc to test request validation and JSON response structure?	MockMvc performs requests without starting an HTTP server, allowing assertions on status, headers, and JSON body via jsonPath. You can test @Valid bean validation by posting invalid payloads and asserting 400 responses with field error details. Combined with @WebMvcTest, it provides fast feedback on controller contract correctness.	@Test\nvoid shouldRejectInvalidOrder() throws Exception {\n    String invalidJson = """\n        {"itemId": "", "quantity": -1}\n        """;\n\n    mockMvc.perform(post("/api/orders")\n            .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)\n            .content(invalidJson))\n        .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())\n        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[0].field").value("itemId"))\n        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[1].field").value("quantity"));\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["mock-mvc", "validation", "json-path"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.388904	0
4a16441e-34ec-4a2f-94a3-adedfe2ac96e	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How do TestContainers improve integration testing over embedded databases?	TestContainers spin up real Docker containers (PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka) for tests, eliminating behavior differences between H2 and production databases. They catch issues with native SQL, database-specific features (JSON columns, window functions), and driver quirks that embedded DBs silently ignore. The @Container lifecycle ties container start/stop to test class lifecycle, and @DynamicPropertySource injects connection details into Spring context.	@SpringBootTest\n@Testcontainers\nclass OrderRepositoryIT {\n    @Container\n    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres =\n        new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15-alpine");\n\n    @DynamicPropertySource\n    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {\n        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);\n        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);\n        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);\n    }\n\n    @Autowired\n    private OrderRepository orderRepository;\n\n    @Test\n    void shouldPersistWithJsonColumn() {\n        Order order = new Order(Map.of("key", "value"));\n        Order saved = orderRepository.save(order);\n        assertThat(saved.getId()).isNotNull();\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["testcontainers", "integration-testing", "docker"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.391075	0
810273f6-f34f-4b85-8e11-73de4aac74d6	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What is an idempotency key and how would you implement it for a payment API?	An idempotency key is a client-generated unique token sent via header (Idempotency-Key: uuid) ensuring repeated requests produce the same result.\nServer stores the key with the response; on retry, returns the stored response without re-executing.\nCritical for non-idempotent operations (POST payments) to handle network retries safely.\n	@PostMapping("/payments")\npublic ResponseEntity<Payment> create(\n        @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String key,\n        @RequestBody PaymentRequest req) {\n    return idempotencyStore.findByKey(key)\n        .map(cached -> ResponseEntity.status(cached.getStatus()).body(cached.getBody()))\n        .orElseGet(() -> {\n            Payment result = paymentService.process(req);\n            idempotencyStore.save(key, result, 201, Duration.ofHours(24));\n            return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(result);\n        });\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["idempotency", "payments", "reliability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.421391	0
b13ae3d4-d88c-4acd-a163-006f480ce4a0	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Compare token bucket and sliding window rate limiting algorithms.	Token bucket: bucket holds N tokens, refills at fixed rate. Each request consumes a token; rejected if empty. Allows short bursts up to bucket capacity.\nSliding window log: tracks timestamps of each request in a window; counts entries. Precise but memory-intensive.\nSliding window counter: hybrid - splits time into sub-windows, interpolates count. Memory-efficient approximation.\nToken bucket is best for APIs allowing bursts; sliding window for strict per-second limits.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["rate-limiting", "algorithms", "scalability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.423624	0
bc8acc66-7f5b-4d04-9703-1bc196ea84b2	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	What is HATEOAS and why is it rarely implemented in practice?	HATEOAS (Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State) means responses include links to available actions/transitions.\nClient discovers API capabilities at runtime rather than hardcoding URLs.\nRarely implemented because it adds payload size, clients typically hardcode paths anyway, and tooling support is weak.\nSpring HATEOAS provides EntityModel and Link abstractions for Java APIs.\n	@GetMapping("/orders/{id}")\npublic EntityModel<Order> getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    Order order = service.findById(id);\n    return EntityModel.of(order,\n        linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).getOrder(id)).withSelfRel(),\n        linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).cancel(id)).withRel("cancel"),\n        linkTo(methodOn(PaymentController.class).pay(id)).withRel("payment"));\n}\n	\N	EASY	["hateoas", "rest", "spring"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.425814	0
6a17eba6-c451-4166-896c-2ef73e22836f	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	When would you choose GraphQL over REST? What are GraphQL's main operational challenges?	Choose GraphQL when clients need flexible queries (mobile vs web), data is highly relational, or over-fetching is costly.\nStick with REST for simple CRUD, public APIs needing HTTP caching, or file uploads.\nOperational challenges: N+1 queries (need DataLoader), no native HTTP caching (everything is POST), complex authorization per field, query complexity attacks requiring depth/cost limiting.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["graphql", "rest", "tradeoffs"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.427968	0
2e4a5c75-1c30-49d0-bf8d-8e92b103ab7c	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Compare write-through, write-back, and write-around caching strategies.	Write-through: write to cache and DB simultaneously. Ensures consistency but adds write latency. Write-back (write-behind): write to cache only, asynchronously persist to DB. Low write latency but risk of data loss. Write-around: write directly to DB, bypassing cache. Avoids cache pollution for infrequently read data but causes cache misses on recent writes.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["caching", "write-through", "write-back", "write-around"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.432871	0
04a4aacc-6df9-4c5a-bdc6-08f2efe9eee4	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Explain LRU and LFU cache eviction policies and their trade-offs.	LRU (Least Recently Used): evicts the item not accessed for the longest time. Good for temporal locality. Implemented with a HashMap + doubly-linked list for O(1) operations. LFU (Least Frequently Used): evicts the item with the lowest access count. Better for frequency-based access patterns but can retain stale popular items. LFU needs a frequency counter and is more complex to implement efficiently.	// LRU Cache using LinkedHashMap\nclass LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {\n    private final int capacity;\n    LRUCache(int capacity) {\n        super(capacity, 0.75f, true); // accessOrder=true\n        this.capacity = capacity;\n    }\n    @Override\n    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {\n        return size() > capacity;\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["lru", "lfu", "eviction", "cache-policy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.435618	0
965a361c-b11e-4b8a-a5d2-3020b08a8896	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	When would you choose Redis over Memcached and vice versa?	Choose Redis when you need: data structures (lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes), persistence (RDB/AOF), pub/sub, Lua scripting, clustering with automatic partitioning, or TTL on individual fields. Choose Memcached when you need: simple key-value caching, multi-threaded performance for high-throughput reads, lower memory overhead per key, or slab-based memory management for predictable allocation.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["redis", "memcached", "comparison", "in-memory"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.437831	0
a4e59694-5ac1-4521-b6a2-cc51a4c5bb46	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	How does CDN caching work and what are cache control headers?	CDNs cache content at edge locations close to users. Key headers: Cache-Control (max-age, s-maxage, no-cache, no-store, private, public), ETag (content hash for conditional requests), Last-Modified/If-Modified-Since (time-based validation), Vary (cache by request header variants). CDN invalidation strategies: TTL expiry, purge API, versioned URLs (cache busting).	\N	\N	EASY	["cdn", "cache-control", "http-caching", "headers"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.440345	0
ec3a613e-9cb5-4498-bcf2-ac7161f87fcb	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	What is cache invalidation and why is it considered hard?	Cache invalidation ensures cached data reflects the latest source of truth. It's hard because: (1) Timing — stale data served between update and invalidation. (2) Distributed systems — invalidating across multiple cache nodes/layers. (3) Thundering herd — many requests hit DB simultaneously after invalidation. (4) Consistency — ensuring all related cached entries are invalidated together. Strategies: TTL-based expiry, event-driven invalidation, versioned keys.	\N	Phil Karlton's quote: 'There are only two hard things in CS: cache invalidation and naming things.' In practice, prefer short TTLs with background refresh over complex invalidation logic.	HARD	["cache-invalidation", "consistency", "distributed-cache"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.442325	0
0cb9db34-7ad4-4a46-9d7d-1029d7c11ee0	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Describe a multi-layer caching architecture for a web application.	Layers (closest to user first): (1) Browser cache — static assets via Cache-Control headers. (2) CDN — edge-cached static and semi-dynamic content. (3) API Gateway/Reverse proxy — response caching for common queries. (4) Application cache — in-process cache (Caffeine/Guava) for hot data. (5) Distributed cache — Redis/Memcached for shared state across app instances. (6) Database cache — query cache, buffer pool. Each layer reduces load on the next.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["multi-layer-cache", "architecture", "cdn", "redis"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.446724	0
89b849b6-030c-4057-a205-2b4259f54558	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How does the least connections algorithm work and what problem does it solve that round robin cannot?	Least connections routes each new request to the server with the fewest active connections. It solves the problem of uneven load caused by variable request duration. Round robin can overload a server handling slow requests (large file uploads, long DB queries) while other servers sit idle. Least connections naturally adapts to heterogeneous workloads without manual weight tuning.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["load-balancing", "least-connections", "adaptive"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.453652	0
462dd586-7f55-45da-83e6-c7b2227d1f29	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What is consistent hashing and why is it preferred over modulo-based hashing for distributed caches behind a load balancer?	Consistent hashing maps both servers and keys onto a hash ring. Each key is served by the next server clockwise on the ring. When a node is added or removed, only K/N keys are remapped (K=total keys, N=nodes). Modulo hashing (key % N) remaps nearly all keys when N changes, causing a cache stampede. Virtual nodes improve uniformity by mapping each physical server to multiple ring positions.\n	// Simplified consistent hash ring in Java\nTreeMap<Integer, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();\nfor (String server : servers) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < VIRTUAL_NODES; i++) {\n        int hash = hash(server + "#" + i);\n        ring.put(hash, server);\n    }\n}\n// Route a request\nint keyHash = hash(requestKey);\nMap.Entry<Integer, String> entry = ring.ceilingEntry(keyHash);\nString target = (entry != null) ? entry.getValue() : ring.firstEntry().getValue();\n	\N	MEDIUM	["consistent-hashing", "distributed-systems", "cache"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.456141	0
14fd8409-8511-479a-92c0-e27c2d9d1317	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Compare L4 and L7 load balancing. What capabilities does L7 provide that L4 cannot?	L4 (transport layer) routes based on IP and TCP/UDP port without inspecting payload. L7 (application layer) inspects HTTP headers, URLs, cookies, and body content. L7 enables content-based routing (path-based, header-based), SSL termination, request transformation, caching, and WAF integration. L4 is faster with lower latency since it avoids parsing; use it for non-HTTP protocols or when raw throughput matters more than routing intelligence.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["L4-vs-L7", "network-layers", "routing"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.458041	0
87a4aa60-5265-4974-97bb-c1dbb0c78a61	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	What are the tradeoffs of sticky sessions (session affinity)? How would you eliminate the need for them?	Sticky sessions pin a user to one server via cookies or IP hash. Tradeoffs: uneven load distribution, reduced fault tolerance (server failure loses all pinned sessions), complicates scaling and deployments. Eliminate them by externalizing session state to a shared store (Redis, Memcached) or using stateless JWTs. This allows any server to handle any request, enabling true horizontal scaling.\n	// Spring Session with Redis - eliminates sticky sessions\n@EnableRedisHttpSession\n@Configuration\npublic class SessionConfig {\n    @Bean\n    public LettuceConnectionFactory connectionFactory() {\n        return new LettuceConnectionFactory("redis-host", 6379);\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["sticky-sessions", "stateless", "session-management"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.459941	0
685a496b-ab17-4194-8e4a-7129a1320595	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	How do health checks work in a load balancer? Distinguish between active and passive health checks.	Active health checks: the load balancer periodically sends probe requests (HTTP GET /health, TCP connect) to backends and removes unresponsive servers from the pool. Passive health checks: the load balancer monitors real traffic responses and marks a server unhealthy after N consecutive failures (e.g., 5xx errors or timeouts). Best practice is combining both: passive for fast failure detection, active for confirming recovery before re-adding to pool.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["health-checks", "fault-tolerance", "high-availability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.462387	0
f632bef0-ebfc-47a6-a150-c4d2080c4ba9	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Design a global load balancing strategy using DNS. What are the limitations and how do you mitigate them?	DNS-based load balancing returns different IP addresses per query using weighted or geo-based resolution (e.g., Route 53, Cloud DNS). Limitations: DNS TTL caching means clients hold stale IPs after failover (mitigation: low TTLs of 30-60s, but increases DNS query load), no health awareness at resolver level (mitigation: integrate health checks into DNS provider to remove unhealthy IPs), and no consideration of server load. For robust global routing, combine DNS-level geo-routing with regional L7 load balancers that handle fine-grained health-aware distribution.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["dns-load-balancing", "global-routing", "geo-distribution"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.464385	0
591a0cda-5ef0-455a-aff5-fa09cedc429f	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How does Kafka achieve parallel consumption within a topic?	Kafka partitions a topic into ordered, immutable logs. Each partition is consumed by exactly one consumer within a consumer group, enabling parallel processing. Adding partitions increases parallelism but you cannot have more active consumers than partitions in a group.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["kafka", "partitions", "consumer-groups"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.468952	0
795b952a-747b-4f66-a8bc-b81dbaebfe30	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What are the three delivery guarantees in messaging systems and how does Kafka support exactly-once?	At-most-once: fire and forget. At-least-once: retry until ack, may duplicate. Exactly-once: each message processed once. Kafka achieves exactly-once via idempotent producers (producer ID + sequence number) and transactional APIs that atomically write to multiple partitions and commit offsets.\n	Properties props = new Properties();\nprops.put("enable.idempotence", "true");\nprops.put("transactional.id", "order-processor-1");\nKafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);\nproducer.initTransactions();\nproducer.beginTransaction();\nproducer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("orders-out", key, value));\nproducer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, groupMetadata);\nproducer.commitTransaction();\n	\N	MEDIUM	["delivery-guarantees", "kafka", "exactly-once"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.472101	0
51ba759d-55ad-4fc8-ae81-d4d608f09c95	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	When would you choose RabbitMQ over Kafka?	RabbitMQ suits low-latency task distribution with complex routing (headers, topic exchanges, priority queues) and message-level acknowledgment. Kafka suits high-throughput event streaming, replay capability, and log-based ordering. Choose RabbitMQ for traditional work queues with varied routing; Kafka for event sourcing, stream processing, and durable ordered logs.\n	\N	\N	EASY	["rabbitmq", "kafka", "architecture-tradeoffs"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.474792	0
cfd9c9ee-5234-48d1-97f9-f7c88ae4476c	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	Explain the difference between pub/sub and point-to-point messaging with a Kafka example of each.	Point-to-point: one consumer processes each message (competing consumers). In Kafka, consumers in the same group split partitions so each record is delivered to one member. Pub/sub: all subscribers receive every message. In Kafka, consumers in different groups each get a full copy of the topic.\n	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["pub-sub", "point-to-point", "messaging-patterns"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.477417	0
bcb18878-ee0b-4007-a858-93791fd11883	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	What is a dead letter queue and how would you implement one in a Kafka consumer?	A DLQ captures messages that fail processing after retry exhaustion, preventing poison pills from blocking the pipeline. In Kafka, the consumer catches processing exceptions, publishes the failed record to a dedicated DLQ topic, then commits the offset on the original topic to advance past it.\n	try {\n    processRecord(record);\n} catch (Exception e) {\n    if (retryCount >= MAX_RETRIES) {\n        producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("orders.DLQ",\n            record.key(), record.value(),\n            headers.add("error", e.getMessage().getBytes())));\n    } else {\n        throw new RetriableException(e);\n    }\n}\n	\N	MEDIUM	["dead-letter-queue", "error-handling", "reliability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.479736	0
2e0b598a-6def-4b61-82d2-98d74943aecb	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How do you handle backpressure when a Kafka consumer cannot keep up with producers?	Strategies: increase partitions and consumer instances, tune max.poll.records and fetch.max.bytes to control batch size, pause/resume partition assignment programmatically, or add a buffered intermediate topic with lower retention. Monitor consumer lag via consumer group offset vs log-end offset. If lag grows unbounded, scale consumers or reduce processing cost.\n	// Pause partitions when buffer is full\nif (inMemoryBuffer.size() > THRESHOLD) {\n    consumer.pause(consumer.assignment());\n}\n// Resume when drained\nif (inMemoryBuffer.size() < LOW_WATERMARK) {\n    consumer.resume(consumer.paused());\n}\n	\N	HARD	["backpressure", "kafka", "scalability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.482023	0
91acf808-aa18-4cd6-a65b-ed53423619e7	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014	How does event sourcing work with message queues and what problem does it solve for microservices?	Event sourcing persists state changes as an immutable sequence of events rather than current state. Kafka acts as the event store; services replay the topic to rebuild state. This gives full audit trail, temporal queries, and decoupled services that derive their own read models. The challenge is managing schema evolution and ensuring event ordering per aggregate via partition keys.\n	\N	\N	HARD	["event-sourcing", "microservices", "kafka"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.484644	0
59237610-694a-4ad0-addf-2f76ae743018	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What is service discovery and how do Eureka and Consul differ?	Service discovery allows services to find each other without hardcoded URLs. Eureka (Netflix OSS) is AP-focused — favors availability during network partitions, uses client-side load balancing, and is tightly integrated with Spring Cloud. Consul (HashiCorp) is CP-focused — provides strong consistency, includes built-in health checking, KV store, and multi-datacenter support out of the box.	\N	\N	EASY	["service-discovery", "eureka", "consul"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.489623	0
b3379503-80c1-4214-9d58-d5fe8e23ab54	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Explain the circuit breaker pattern and its states.	Circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by wrapping remote calls. Three states: CLOSED (requests flow normally, failures counted), OPEN (requests fail-fast without calling downstream, after failure threshold breached), HALF-OPEN (after a wait duration, limited requests probe if service recovered). If probe succeeds, transition to CLOSED; if fails, back to OPEN.	@CircuitBreaker(name = "orderService", fallbackMethod = "fallback")\npublic Order getOrder(String id) {\n    return restTemplate.getForObject(url + "/" + id, Order.class);\n}\n\nprivate Order fallback(String id, Throwable t) {\n    return Order.cachedDefault(id);\n}\n	Resilience4j replaced Hystrix as the Spring Cloud standard. Configure failureRateThreshold, waitDurationInOpenState, and slidingWindowSize in application.yml.	MEDIUM	["circuit-breaker", "resilience4j", "fault-tolerance"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.491774	0
d96daeba-179d-407e-b2f0-c10eb8fdee94	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What are the key responsibilities of an API gateway in a microservices architecture?	API gateway is the single entry point for clients. Responsibilities: (1) Request routing to appropriate microservice, (2) Authentication/authorization (JWT validation, OAuth token introspection), (3) Rate limiting and throttling, (4) Response aggregation from multiple services, (5) Protocol translation (REST to gRPC), (6) SSL termination, (7) Request/response transformation, (8) Circuit breaking and load balancing.	\N	\N	EASY	["api-gateway", "routing", "cross-cutting-concerns"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.494237	0
84fe8705-02ca-4557-a538-d0f034d27905	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Compare the saga pattern's choreography vs orchestration approaches for distributed transactions.	Choreography: each service publishes domain events that trigger the next step. Decentralized, loosely coupled, but hard to track overall flow and debug failures. Orchestration: a central saga orchestrator directs participants by sending commands and handling replies. Easier to understand and monitor, but introduces a single point of coordination. Both require compensating transactions for rollback.	// Orchestrator-based saga (pseudo-code)\npublic class OrderSaga {\n    public void execute(OrderCommand cmd) {\n        reserveInventory(cmd);   // Step 1\n        chargePayment(cmd);      // Step 2\n        confirmOrder(cmd);       // Step 3\n    }\n    public void compensate(OrderCommand cmd, int failedStep) {\n        if (failedStep >= 2) refundPayment(cmd);\n        if (failedStep >= 1) releaseInventory(cmd);\n    }\n}\n	\N	HARD	["saga-pattern", "distributed-transactions", "choreography", "orchestration"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.49615	0
f6e1f8d6-d0cf-4cb7-99c7-dcd5d192fa6a	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	What problems does a service mesh like Istio solve?	Service mesh handles cross-cutting infrastructure concerns at the network level via sidecar proxies (Envoy). Solves: mutual TLS between services without app code changes, fine-grained traffic management (canary deployments, A/B routing), distributed tracing and observability (metrics, logs), retry/timeout/circuit-breaker policies, and access control policies. Moves networking logic out of application code into infrastructure.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["service-mesh", "istio", "sidecar-proxy"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.497979	0
b304347f-129d-4c83-acb0-3011ac80a061	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	How does bounded context from DDD guide microservice boundaries?	A bounded context defines a clear boundary within which a domain model has consistent meaning. Each microservice should align to one bounded context — owning its data, language, and rules. Shared concepts (e.g., 'Customer') may have different representations across contexts. Context mapping identifies relationships: shared kernel, customer-supplier, anticorruption layer. Wrong boundaries lead to distributed monoliths with excessive cross-service coupling.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["ddd", "bounded-context", "service-boundaries"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.499981	0
28c7b2f2-5a4e-4566-8a13-41e9f7ae7201	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	When should you choose synchronous (REST/gRPC) vs asynchronous (messaging) inter-service communication?	Synchronous (REST/gRPC): use when client needs immediate response, operations are fast, and strong consistency required (queries, real-time validation). Drawback: temporal coupling — caller blocks until response. Asynchronous (Kafka/RabbitMQ): use for fire-and-forget commands, event-driven workflows, long-running processes, and when services should be temporally decoupled. Enables better resilience and scalability but adds eventual consistency complexity.	// Async event publishing with Spring Cloud Stream\n@Bean\npublic Supplier<OrderEvent> orderCreated() {\n    return () -> new OrderEvent(orderId, "CREATED");\n}\n\n// Consumer in another service\n@Bean\npublic Consumer<OrderEvent> processOrder() {\n    return event -> inventoryService.reserve(event.getOrderId());\n}\n	\N	HARD	["sync-async", "messaging", "event-driven", "coupling"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.50246	0
ed208c96-ba03-4ff2-a85c-0a249839c715	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain database sharding and its common strategies.	Sharding partitions data across multiple database instances. Common strategies: (1) Range-based — partition by key ranges (e.g., user IDs 1-1M on shard 1). (2) Hash-based — hash the partition key to determine shard. (3) Directory-based — a lookup table maps keys to shards. Hash-based provides even distribution; range-based supports range queries efficiently.	\N	Sharding introduces complexity: cross-shard joins, rebalancing when adding shards, and maintaining referential integrity. Choose partition keys carefully to avoid hotspots.	MEDIUM	["sharding", "database", "partitioning"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.508855	0
5161442f-e416-41d8-a271-b447249e6c56	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is the CAP theorem and what trade-offs does it impose?	CAP theorem states a distributed system can guarantee at most two of three properties: Consistency (all nodes see the same data), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (system works despite network failures). Since network partitions are inevitable, the real choice is between CP (consistent but may reject requests) and AP (available but may serve stale data).	\N	Examples: CP systems — HBase, MongoDB (with majority write concern). AP systems — Cassandra, DynamoDB. The PACELC theorem extends CAP: even without partitions, there's a latency vs. consistency trade-off.	MEDIUM	["cap-theorem", "distributed-systems", "consistency", "availability"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.510896	0
42bf7d36-b7c7-443a-8589-68f09e5eed5a	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How does consistent hashing work and why is it used?	Consistent hashing maps both keys and nodes onto a circular hash ring. A key is assigned to the first node clockwise from its position on the ring. When a node is added/removed, only keys between the affected node and its predecessor need remapping — O(K/N) keys instead of O(K). Virtual nodes (multiple positions per physical node) improve load distribution.	// Simplified consistent hashing\nTreeMap<Integer, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();\n// Add node with virtual nodes\nfor (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; i++) {\n    int hash = hash(node + "#" + i);\n    ring.put(hash, node);\n}\n// Find node for key\nint keyHash = hash(key);\nMap.Entry<Integer, String> entry = ring.ceilingEntry(keyHash);\nreturn entry != null ? entry.getValue() : ring.firstEntry().getValue();\n	\N	MEDIUM	["consistent-hashing", "load-balancing", "distributed-systems"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.513224	0
65557d7f-bc08-440e-b70e-837155a30501	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What are the main data partitioning strategies and when to use each?	Key strategies: (1) Horizontal partitioning (sharding) — split rows across databases by partition key. (2) Vertical partitioning — split columns into separate tables/services (e.g., user profile vs. user activity). (3) Functional partitioning — split by business domain (e.g., orders DB, inventory DB). Use horizontal for large tables, vertical to separate hot/cold data, functional for microservice boundaries.	\N	\N	MEDIUM	["partitioning", "sharding", "database-design"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.515135	0
628e0615-ec99-463f-b922-44d1d0d8ab36	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Explain database replication strategies: master-slave vs. multi-master.	Master-slave: one primary handles writes, replicas handle reads. Simple, good read scaling, but single write bottleneck and potential data loss on master failure. Multi-master: multiple nodes accept writes, changes propagated to all. Better write availability but introduces conflict resolution complexity (last-write-wins, vector clocks, CRDTs).	\N	Replication lag in master-slave can cause read-after-write inconsistency. Solutions: read from master for recent writes, use monotonic reads, or implement causal consistency.	HARD	["replication", "master-slave", "multi-master", "consistency"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.517399	0
09da641f-9c9b-4440-8f47-b2fb2a248677	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	What is eventual consistency and how do systems handle it?	Eventual consistency guarantees that if no new updates are made, all replicas will eventually converge to the same state. Systems handle it via: (1) Read repair — fix stale data on read. (2) Anti-entropy — background processes compare and sync replicas. (3) Conflict resolution — last-write-wins, vector clocks, or application-level merge. (4) Quorum reads/writes — R+W > N ensures overlap between read and write sets.	\N	\N	HARD	["eventual-consistency", "distributed-systems", "quorum"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.519379	0
4fc4f977-85f6-4ea3-aefa-85e1f765ba06	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	How would you design a system to handle a 10x traffic spike?	Strategies: (1) Auto-scaling groups with pre-warming for predictable spikes. (2) Rate limiting and load shedding to protect core services. (3) Circuit breakers to prevent cascade failures. (4) Caching at multiple layers (CDN, application, database). (5) Async processing via message queues for non-critical work. (6) Database read replicas and connection pooling. (7) Graceful degradation — serve cached/partial responses under extreme load.	\N	\N	HARD	["scalability", "traffic-spike", "auto-scaling", "resilience"]	NEW	2.5	0	0	\N	2026-07-26 00:49:25.521688	0
