es gives you O(1) lookup AND O(1) insertion/removal by position?", "Use a HashMap for O(1) key lookup and a doubly linked list to maintain access order. Most recently used goes to the front, least recently used is at the back.", "On get: look up node in map, move it to the front of the list. On put: if key exists, update value and move to front. If new, add to front and map. If over capacity, remove the tail node from both the list and the map."]	600	2026-07-25 23:56:49.986186	\N	0	0	\N
1dff52cd-634d-485b-99ce-8589e3f12764	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Maximum Subarray (Kadane's Algorithm)	Given an integer array `nums`, find the subarray with the largest sum, and return its sum.\n\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n\n**Constraints:**\n- 1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n- -10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4\n\n**Follow up:** If you have figured out the O(n) solution, try coding another solution using the divide and conquer approach.\n	MEDIUM	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {\n        // Your solution here\n        return 0;\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "nums = [-2,1,-3,4,-1,2,1,-5,4]", "expected": "6", "description": "Subarray [4,-1,2,1] has the largest sum = 6"}, {"input": "nums = [1]", "expected": "1", "description": "Single element array"}, {"input": "nums = [5,4,-1,7,8]", "expected": "23", "description": "Entire array is the max subarray"}, {"input": "nums = [-1,-2,-3,-4]", "expected": "-1", "description": "All negative: pick the least negative"}]	["At each position, decide: start a new subarray here, or extend the previous subarray?", "Kadane's Algorithm: currentMax = max(nums[i], currentMax + nums[i]). Track the global maximum across all positions.", "If currentMax becomes negative, it's better to start fresh from the next element."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:17.700813	2026-08-09 01:52:58.688235	14	2	\N
ce040fa5-79d1-4408-a6dc-9b31d5e6af8a	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Two Sum	Given an array of integers `nums` and an integer `target`, return the indices of the two numbers that add up to `target`.\n\nYou may assume that each input would have exactly one solution, and you may not use the same element twice.\n\nYou can return the answer in any order.\n\n**Constraints:**\n- 2 <= nums.length <= 10^4\n- -10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n- Only one valid answer exists.\n	EASY	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n        // Your solution here\n        return new int[]{};\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "nums = [2,7,11,15], target = 9", "expected": "[0,1]", "description": "Basic case: 2 + 7 = 9"}, {"input": "nums = [3,2,4], target = 6", "expected": "[1,2]", "description": "Non-adjacent elements: 2 + 4 = 6"}, {"input": "nums = [3,3], target = 6", "expected": "[0,1]", "description": "Duplicate values: 3 + 3 = 6"}, {"input": "nums = [-1,-2,-3,-4,-5], target = -8", "expected": "[2,4]", "description": "Negative numbers: -3 + -5 = -8"}]	["A brute force approach checks every pair in O(n^2). Can you do better?", "Use a HashMap to store each number's index as you iterate. For each number, check if (target - num) exists in the map.", "The optimal solution runs in O(n) time and O(n) space using a single pass with a HashMap."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:17.691378	2026-08-02 01:51:31.205306	7	1	// Optimal: HashMap approach - O(n) time, O(n) space\npublic int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        int complement = target - nums[i];\n        if (map.containsKey(complement)) {\n            return new int[]{map.get(complement), i};\n        }\n        map.put(nums[i], i);\n    }\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException("No solution");\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(n)\n// Key insight: Store seen values in map, check for complement\n
44863bda-314f-4526-b8bd-0d6c5993d63b	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters	Given a string `s`, find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters.\n\n**Constraints:**\n- 0 <= s.length <= 5 * 10^4\n- s consists of English letters, digits, symbols and spaces.\n	MEDIUM	JAVA	public class Solution {\n    public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {\n        // Your solution here\n        return 0;\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "s = \\"abcabcbb\\"", "expected": "3", "description": "Longest is 'abc' with length 3"}, {"input": "s = \\"bbbbb\\"", "expected": "1", "description": "All same character: length 1"}, {"input": "s = \\"pwwkew\\"", "expected": "3", "description": "Longest is 'wke' with length 3 (not 'pwke' - must be contiguous)"}, {"input": "s = \\"\\"", "expected": "0", "description": "Empty string"}, {"input": "s = \\"dvdf\\"", "expected": "3", "description": "Longest is 'vdf' - sliding window must shrink from left"}]	["Use the sliding window technique with two pointers (left and right).", "Maintain a Set or Map of characters in the current window. When a duplicate is found, shrink the window from the left until the duplicate is removed.", "Optimization: use a HashMap<Character, Integer> storing last seen index. When duplicate found, jump left pointer to max(left, lastSeen + 1) to avoid character-by-character shrinking."]	360	2026-07-25 23:55:17.723482	2026-08-02 02:13:37.305651	7	1	\N
a62ff733-f81f-4960-8f03-40a467df4f68	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Valid Palindrome	Given a string s, determine whether it reads the same forwards and backwards after\nconverting all uppercase letters to lowercase and removing all non-alphanumeric characters.\n\nAn empty string is considered a valid palindrome.\n\nConstraints:\n- 1 <= s.length <= 2 * 10^5\n- s consists of printable ASCII characters\n	EASY	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {\n        // Your solution here\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "s = \\"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\\"", "expected": "true", "description": "Classic palindrome after removing non-alphanumeric and ignoring case"}, {"input": "s = \\"race a car\\"", "expected": "false", "description": "Not a palindrome"}, {"input": "s = \\" \\"", "expected": "true", "description": "Empty string after cleanup is a palindrome"}, {"input": "s = \\"0P\\"", "expected": "false", "description": "Mixed digit and letter, not a palindrome"}]	["Think about approaching the string from both ends simultaneously.", "Use two pointers starting at the beginning and end. Skip characters that are not letters or digits.", "Compare characters at left and right pointers (case-insensitive). Move inward. If any mismatch found, return false."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:18.196413	\N	0	0	// Two-pointer approach - O(n) time, O(1) space\npublic boolean isPalindrome(String s) {\n    int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;\n    while (left < right) {\n        while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;\n        while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(right))) right--;\n        if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(left)) != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(right))) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        left++;\n        right--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(1)\n// Key insight: Skip non-alphanumeric, compare from both ends\n
debc791b-11ae-4fb8-9ba0-3d001d366061	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock	You are given an array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a stock on the i-th day. You want to maximize profit by choosing a single day to buy and a different later day to sell.\n\nReturn the maximum profit achievable from this single transaction. If no profit is possible, return 0.\n\n**Constraints:**\n- 1 <= prices.length <= 10^5\n- 0 <= prices[i] <= 10^4\n	EASY	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        // Your solution here\n        return 0;\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]", "expected": "5", "description": "Buy at 1, sell at 6"}, {"input": "prices = [7,6,4,3,1]", "expected": "0", "description": "Prices only decrease, no profit possible"}, {"input": "prices = [2,4,1]", "expected": "2", "description": "Best profit is early buy-sell, not later"}, {"input": "prices = [1,2]", "expected": "1", "description": "Minimum profitable case"}]	["Track the minimum price seen so far as you iterate.", "At each day, calculate the profit if you sold today (current price minus minimum so far). Track the maximum of these profits.", "One pass: maintain minPrice and maxProfit. For each price, update minPrice = min(minPrice, price) and maxProfit = max(maxProfit, price - minPrice)."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:17.74348	\N	0	0	// Single pass - O(n) time, O(1) space\npublic int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n    int minPrice = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    int maxProfit = 0;\n    for (int price : prices) {\n        if (price < minPrice) {\n            minPrice = price;\n        } else {\n            maxProfit = Math.max(maxProfit, price - minPrice);\n        }\n    }\n    return maxProfit;\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(1)\n// Key insight: Track minimum price seen so far, compute profit at each step\n
9c4e4432-ecdd-4d8b-96cb-128e9c697774	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Contains Duplicate	Given an integer array `nums`, return `true` if any value appears at least twice in the array, and return `false` if every element is distinct.\n\n**Constraints:**\n- 1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n- -10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n	EASY	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n        // Your solution here\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "nums = [1,2,3,1]", "expected": "true", "description": "1 appears twice"}, {"input": "nums = [1,2,3,4]", "expected": "false", "description": "All distinct elements"}, {"input": "nums = [1,1,1,3,3,4,3,2,4,2]", "expected": "true", "description": "Multiple duplicates"}, {"input": "nums = [0]", "expected": "false", "description": "Single element: no duplicates possible"}]	["Sorting the array groups duplicates together - check adjacent elements. O(n log n) time.", "A HashSet gives O(n) time: add each element; if add() returns false, a duplicate exists.", "Space-time tradeoff: O(1) space with sort vs O(n) space with HashSet."]	180	2026-07-25 23:55:17.711807	2026-09-20 02:09:48.530551	56	4	// HashSet approach - O(n) time, O(n) space\npublic boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    for (int num : nums) {\n        if (!seen.add(num)) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(n)\n// Key insight: HashSet.add() returns false if element already exists\n
9dcdd69f-38c7-439a-a593-5d6c4ea31048	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Valid Parentheses	Given a string containing only the characters '(', ')', '{', '}', '[' and ']',\ndetermine if the input string has valid bracket pairing.\n\nA string is valid if:\n- Every opening bracket has a corresponding closing bracket of the same type.\n- Brackets are closed in the correct order (innermost first).\n- Every closing bracket has a matching opening bracket of the same type.\n\nConstraints:\n- 1 <= s.length <= 10^4\n- s consists of parentheses characters only: '(){}[]'\n	EASY	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public boolean isValid(String s) {\n        // Your solution here\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "s = \\"()\\"", "expected": "true", "description": "Simple matched pair"}, {"input": "s = \\"()[]{}\\"", "expected": "true", "description": "Multiple different bracket types all properly closed"}, {"input": "s = \\"(]\\"", "expected": "false", "description": "Mismatched bracket types"}, {"input": "s = \\"([)]\\"", "expected": "false", "description": "Incorrect nesting order"}]	["Think about what data structure naturally handles nested matching.", "Use a stack. Push opening brackets and pop when you encounter a closing one. Check if the popped bracket matches.", "For each closing bracket, if the stack is empty or the top doesn't match, return false. At the end, the stack must be empty."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:18.158442	\N	0	0	// Stack approach - O(n) time, O(n) space\npublic boolean isValid(String s) {\n    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {\n        if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {\n            stack.push(c);\n        } else {\n            if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;\n            char top = stack.pop();\n            if (c == ')' && top != '(') return false;\n            if (c == ']' && top != '[') return false;\n            if (c == '}' && top != '{') return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return stack.isEmpty();\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(n)\n// Key insight: Push opening brackets, pop and match on closing brackets\n
12c9bfad-b5e1-4d35-8f3d-2124854d8666	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013	Maximum Depth of Binary Tree	Given the root of a binary tree, return its maximum depth.\n\nThe maximum depth is defined as the number of nodes along the longest path\nfrom the root node down to the farthest leaf node.\n\nA leaf is a node with no children.\n\nConstraints:\n- The number of nodes in the tree is in the range [0, 10^4].\n- -100 <= Node.val <= 100\n	EASY	DSA	// Definition for a binary tree node:\n// class TreeNode {\n//     int val;\n//     TreeNode left;\n//     TreeNode right;\n//     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\n// }\n\npublic class Solution {\n    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {\n        // Your solution here\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "root = [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]", "expected": "3", "description": "Tree with depth 3: root -> 20 -> 15 or 7"}, {"input": "root = [1, null, 2]", "expected": "2", "description": "Right-skewed tree with depth 2"}, {"input": "root = []", "expected": "0", "description": "Empty tree has depth 0"}, {"input": "root = [1]", "expected": "1", "description": "Single node has depth 1"}]	["Think recursively: the depth of a tree is related to the depth of its subtrees.", "The depth of a node is 1 + the maximum depth of its children.", "Base case: a null node has depth 0. Recurse on left and right children, return 1 + max(left, right)."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:17.839749	\N	0	0	// Recursive DFS - O(n) time, O(h) space\npublic int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {\n    if (root == null) return 0;\n    return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(h) where h = height\n// Key insight: Depth = 1 + max(left depth, right depth)\n
7707cc2a-0cc9-460d-8015-c73cc4a802e7	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Climbing Stairs	You are climbing a staircase that has n steps to reach the top.\nAt each step, you can choose to climb either 1 step or 2 steps upward.\n\nDetermine the total number of distinct ways you can reach the top of the staircase.\n\nConstraints:\n- 1 <= n <= 45\n	EASY	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public int climbStairs(int n) {\n        // Your solution here\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "n = 2", "expected": "2", "description": "Two ways: (1+1) or (2)"}, {"input": "n = 3", "expected": "3", "description": "Three ways: (1+1+1), (1+2), (2+1)"}, {"input": "n = 5", "expected": "8", "description": "Eight distinct ways to climb 5 stairs"}, {"input": "n = 1", "expected": "1", "description": "Only one way to climb a single step"}]	["Consider how you could arrive at step n. You either came from step n-1 or step n-2.", "This forms a recurrence relation: ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2). Notice the similarity to Fibonacci numbers.", "Use bottom-up DP with two variables tracking the previous two values to achieve O(1) space."]	300	2026-07-25 23:55:17.95278	\N	0	0	// Dynamic Programming (Fibonacci) - O(n) time, O(1) space\npublic int climbStairs(int n) {\n    if (n <= 2) return n;\n    int prev2 = 1, prev1 = 2;\n    for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {\n        int current = prev1 + prev2;\n        prev2 = prev1;\n        prev1 = current;\n    }\n    return prev1;\n}\n// Time: O(n), Space: O(1)\n// Key insight: ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2), same as Fibonacci\n
a6b374a9-0714-4763-9bd5-0d8e54dafde1	a4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015	Coin Change	You are given an array of integer coin denominations and a target amount of money.\nReturn the fewest number of coins needed to make up that amount.\nIf no combination of coins can produce the target amount, return -1.\n\nYou may assume you have an infinite supply of each coin denomination.\n\nConstraints:\n- 1 <= coins.length <= 12\n- 1 <= coins[i] <= 2^31 - 1\n- 0 <= amount <= 10^4\n	MEDIUM	DSA	public class Solution {\n    public int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {\n        // Your solution here\n    }\n}\n	[{"input": "coins = [1,5,10], amount = 12", "expected": "3", "description": "10 + 1 + 1 = 12, using 3 coins"}, {"input": "coins = [2], amount = 3", "expected": "-1", "description": "Cannot make odd amount with only even coins"}, {"input": "coins = [1], amount = 0", "expected": "0", "description": "Zero amount needs zero coins"}, {"input": "coins = [1,3,4], amount = 6", "expected": "2", "description": "3 + 3 = 6, using 2 coins (not 4+1+1)"}]	["This is an unbounded knapsack variant. For each amount, try subtracting each coin and find the minimum.", "Define dp[i] as the minimum coins needed for amount i. Recurrence: dp[i] = min(dp[i - coin] + 1) for each coin where coin <= i.", "Initialize dp array with amount+1 (impossible value). dp[0] = 0. If dp[amount] > amount, return -1."]	600	2026-07-25 23:55:17.965776	\N	0	0	// Bottom-up DP - O(amount * coins.length) time, O(amount) space\npublic int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {\n    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];\n    Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);\n    dp[0] = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= amount; i++) {\n        for (int coin : coins) {\n            if (coin <= i) {\n                dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - coin] + 1);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];\n}\n// Time: O(amount * n), Space: O(amount)\n// Key insight: For each amount, try each coin and take minimum\n
