af521b76-efab-42a4-b235-b2e55a333f60	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Migrate Monolith to Microservices	You have a monolithic e-commerce application handling orders, inventory, payments, user management, and notifications. The application is experiencing scaling issues and deployment bottlenecks.\n\nDesign a migration strategy to decompose this monolith into microservices.\n\nRequirements:\n- Identify service boundaries using Domain-Driven Design (bounded contexts)\n- Design the communication patterns between services (sync vs async)\n- Handle distributed transactions (e.g., order placement involves inventory, payment, notification)\n- Plan the migration path (strangler fig pattern, incremental decomposition)\n- Design shared data decomposition strategy\n\nCurrent State:\n- Single PostgreSQL database with 50+ tables\n- Monolithic Spring Boot application (200K+ lines)\n- 20 developers, 2-week release cycles\n- Peak load: 10K orders per hour\n\nDeliverables:\n- Service boundary diagram\n- Communication pattern for each service interaction\n- Data migration strategy\n- Migration timeline and phases\n- Rollback strategy for each phase\n	- Zero downtime during migration\n- No data loss or inconsistency during transition\n- Each phase must be independently deployable and rollback-able\n- Must support running monolith and microservices in parallel during transition\n- Complete migration within 12 months\n- Team can dedicate 40% capacity to migration work\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Service Decomposition", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Bounded context identification", "Service granularity", "API contract design"]}, {"name": "Data Strategy", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Database per service approach", "Data synchronization", "Eventual consistency handling"]}, {"name": "Communication Patterns", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Sync vs async decisions", "Event-driven architecture", "Saga pattern for distributed transactions"]}, {"name": "Migration Plan", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Strangler fig application", "Phased approach", "Risk mitigation"]}, {"name": "Operational Readiness", "weight": 0.1, "criteria": ["Observability", "Service mesh", "CI/CD pipeline changes"]}]}	\N	HARD	60	2026-07-25 23:55:18.227622
021f1512-c045-450f-aa22-e86518d9abfe	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design Event-Driven Order System	Design an event-driven architecture for an order processing system.\n\nThe system must handle the complete order lifecycle:\n1. Order placed → validate inventory → reserve stock\n2. Payment processed → confirm order → update inventory\n3. Order fulfilled → ship → notify customer\n4. Handle failures at any step (compensation/rollback)\n\nRequirements:\n- Use event sourcing for the order aggregate\n- Implement the Saga pattern for distributed transactions\n- Design event schemas with versioning\n- Handle idempotency for event consumers\n- Support event replay for debugging and recovery\n\nServices Involved:\n- Order Service (owns order lifecycle)\n- Inventory Service (stock management)\n- Payment Service (payment processing)\n- Shipping Service (fulfillment)\n- Notification Service (customer communications)\n\nDeliverables:\n- Event flow diagram for happy path and failure scenarios\n- Event schema definitions\n- Saga orchestration or choreography design\n- Dead letter queue handling\n- Event store schema\n	- Process 5000 orders per minute at peak\n- End-to-end order processing < 30 seconds (happy path)\n- No lost events (at-least-once delivery)\n- Event replay must not cause duplicate side effects\n- Support schema evolution without breaking consumers\n- Compensating transactions must complete within 60 seconds\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Event Design", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Event schema quality", "Versioning strategy", "Event granularity"]}, {"name": "Saga Implementation", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Orchestration vs choreography choice", "Compensation logic", "Failure handling"]}, {"name": "Reliability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Idempotency", "Dead letter handling", "Event ordering guarantees"]}, {"name": "Operational", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Event replay capability", "Monitoring", "Debugging support"]}]}	\N	MEDIUM	45	2026-07-25 23:55:18.238092
7c0e215d-e2ab-4cd3-a321-a0ec1c81beed	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design Plugin Architecture	Design a plugin architecture for a document processing application that allows third-party developers to extend functionality.\n\nThe application processes documents through a pipeline: ingest → parse → transform → validate → export.\n\nRequirements:\n- Plugins can hook into any pipeline stage\n- Plugins are loaded dynamically at runtime (no application restart)\n- Plugin isolation (a failing plugin doesn't crash the host)\n- Plugin lifecycle management (install, enable, disable, uninstall)\n- Plugin configuration and dependency management\n- Plugin marketplace/registry for discovery\n\nPlugin Capabilities:\n- Add new document format parsers (e.g., custom XML schema)\n- Add transformation steps (e.g., PII redaction, translation)\n- Add validation rules (e.g., compliance checks)\n- Add export targets (e.g., custom cloud storage)\n- Add UI extensions (settings panels, preview renderers)\n\nDeliverables:\n- Plugin API/SPI design\n- Plugin loading and isolation mechanism\n- Extension point definitions\n- Security model (sandboxing, permissions)\n- Plugin communication protocol\n	- Plugin load time < 500ms\n- Plugin failure must not affect host or other plugins\n- Support 50+ simultaneously loaded plugins\n- Plugin API must be backward compatible across major versions\n- Memory overhead per plugin < 50MB\n- Hot-reload without processing pipeline interruption\n	{"categories": [{"name": "API Design", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Extension point clarity", "Backward compatibility", "Ease of plugin development"]}, {"name": "Isolation & Security", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Failure isolation", "Sandboxing approach", "Permission model"]}, {"name": "Lifecycle Management", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Dynamic loading", "Hot-reload", "Dependency resolution"]}, {"name": "Architecture", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Pipeline integration", "Event/hook system", "Plugin communication"]}]}	\N	MEDIUM	30	2026-07-25 23:55:18.24262
2f2b4c61-ed5f-4673-a723-81c1166f6b5d	a6000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Apply CQRS to Banking System	Design a banking system using CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) pattern.\n\nThe system handles:\n- Account management (open, close, freeze accounts)\n- Transactions (deposits, withdrawals, transfers)\n- Balance inquiries and statement generation\n- Fraud detection and alerts\n- Regulatory reporting\n\nRequirements:\n- Separate command (write) and query (read) models\n- Event sourcing for the command side (complete audit trail)\n- Optimized read models for different query patterns\n- Strong consistency for balance updates\n- Eventually consistent read models acceptable for reporting\n- Support for temporal queries ("what was the balance at time T?")\n\nRead Model Projections Needed:\n- Current balance (real-time, strongly consistent)\n- Transaction history (paginated, filterable)\n- Monthly statements (pre-computed)\n- Fraud detection aggregates (sliding window)\n- Regulatory reports (daily/monthly snapshots)\n\nDeliverables:\n- Command and event definitions\n- Read model schemas for each projection\n- Consistency boundary design\n- Event store implementation approach\n- Projection rebuild strategy\n	- Balance queries must reflect all committed transactions (strong consistency)\n- Transaction processing: 50K transactions per second\n- Statement generation: < 5 seconds for any account\n- Event store must retain all events indefinitely (regulatory requirement)\n- Read model rebuild from events must complete within 4 hours for 1 year of data\n- Zero tolerance for balance inconsistency (financial accuracy)\n	{"categories": [{"name": "CQRS Design", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Command/query separation clarity", "Model optimization", "Consistency boundaries"]}, {"name": "Event Sourcing", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Event design", "Aggregate boundaries", "Snapshot strategy"]}, {"name": "Read Model Projections", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Projection design per use case", "Rebuild strategy", "Staleness handling"]}, {"name": "Consistency & Correctness", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Strong consistency for balances", "Eventual consistency trade-offs", "Idempotency"]}]}	\N	HARD	45	2026-07-25 23:55:18.24596
105eea3a-fcc1-4823-9f82-bdb8ef4b8988	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a URL Shortener	Design a URL shortening service similar to bit.ly or TinyURL.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Given a long URL, generate a unique short URL\n- When users access the short URL, redirect to the original long URL\n- Users can optionally specify a custom short link\n- Links expire after a configurable time-to-live (TTL)\n- Analytics: track click count, geographic distribution, referrer\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- High availability (99.99% uptime)\n- Low latency redirects (< 10ms p99)\n- Short URLs should be as short as possible\n- System should be horizontally scalable\n\nConsider:\n- How to generate unique short codes (hash vs counter-based)\n- Storage strategy for billions of URLs\n- Caching layer for popular URLs\n- How to handle custom aliases and collisions\n- Rate limiting to prevent abuse\n	- Handle 100M new URLs per month (write-heavy initially, read-heavy over time)\n- 10:1 read-to-write ratio (1B redirects per month)\n- Redirect latency < 10ms at p99\n- URL length: 7-8 characters using [a-zA-Z0-9]\n- Data retention: 5 years default, configurable per URL\n- Storage estimate: ~500 bytes per URL record, ~500GB over 5 years\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Scalability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Horizontal scaling strategy", "Database sharding approach", "Read replica usage"]}, {"name": "Data Model", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Short code generation algorithm", "Collision handling", "Schema design"]}, {"name": "Caching", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Cache layer design", "Cache invalidation on expiry", "Hot URL optimization"]}, {"name": "Availability", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Redundancy", "Failover strategy", "Geographic distribution"]}, {"name": "Trade-offs", "weight": 0.15, "criteria": ["CAP theorem awareness", "Consistency vs availability choice", "Cost considerations"]}]}	\N	MEDIUM	45	2026-07-25 23:55:18.255
6a5bc108-7f90-4cf8-b9df-221ab548b327	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Weather Reporting System	Design a system that collects weather data from distributed sensors and serves real-time and historical weather information to users.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Ingest data from thousands of weather stations (temperature, humidity, wind, precipitation)\n- Serve current weather for a given location (lat/lng or city)\n- Provide historical weather data with time-range queries\n- Generate weather alerts when thresholds are exceeded\n- Aggregate data at different granularities (hourly, daily, monthly)\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Ingest 100K data points per second from sensors\n- Query response under 200ms for current weather\n- Data retention: raw data 1 year, aggregated data 10 years\n- Sensor data loss tolerance: under 0.1%\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Time-series data storage and compression\n- Geospatial indexing for location queries\n- Data aggregation and rollup strategies\n- Alerting pipeline design\n	- 50,000 weather stations reporting every 60 seconds\n- ~50K writes/second sustained\n- Query patterns: point-in-time current, range historical, geo-radius\n- Raw data: 200 bytes per reading\n- Storage: ~3TB/year raw; ~100GB/year aggregated\n	{"alerting": 15, "data_model": 25, "aggregation": 15, "query_design": 20, "ingestion_pipeline": 25}	- Ingestion: Sensors → MQTT/Kafka → Stream processor → Time-series DB\n- Storage: TimescaleDB or InfluxDB for time-series; geospatial index for location queries\n- Current weather: Materialized view of latest reading per station; cached in Redis by geo-cell\n- Historical: Partition by time (monthly); pre-aggregate hourly/daily rollups\n- Geo-query: Map user location to nearest station(s) using geohash or R-tree index\n- Alerts: Stream processor evaluates threshold rules in real-time; push to notification service\n- Retention: Raw data TTL 1 year; aggregated data retained indefinitely with compression\n- API: GET /weather/current?lat=&lng=; GET /weather/history?station=&start=&end=&granularity=\n	EASY	20	2026-07-25 23:56:50.213006
862e527c-5566-4fe5-8eb4-1a6202a07fbd	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Rate Limiter	Design a distributed rate limiting service that can be used across multiple microservices.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Limit requests per user/IP/API key within a time window\n- Support multiple rate limiting algorithms (fixed window, sliding window, token bucket)\n- Configurable limits per endpoint, user tier, or API key\n- Return appropriate headers (X-RateLimit-Remaining, X-RateLimit-Reset)\n- Support both hard limits (reject) and soft limits (throttle/queue)\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- Sub-millisecond decision latency\n- Distributed: work across multiple application instances\n- Accurate counting even under high concurrency\n- Graceful degradation if rate limiter is unavailable\n\nConsider:\n- Algorithm trade-offs (token bucket vs sliding window log vs sliding window counter)\n- Distributed synchronization (Redis-based vs local + sync)\n- Race conditions in counter updates\n- How to handle rate limiter failures (fail-open vs fail-closed)\n	- Decision latency < 1ms at p99\n- Support 1M+ requests per second across all services\n- Accuracy: no more than 1% over-admission under normal conditions\n- Configuration changes propagate within 5 seconds\n- Memory budget: < 100 bytes per rate limit rule per user\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Algorithm Design", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Algorithm selection with justification", "Accuracy analysis", "Edge case handling"]}, {"name": "Distributed Coordination", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Cross-instance synchronization", "Race condition handling", "Consistency model"]}, {"name": "Performance", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Latency optimization", "Memory efficiency", "Atomic operations"]}, {"name": "Resilience", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Failure mode handling", "Graceful degradation", "Monitoring/alerting"]}]}	\N	MEDIUM	30	2026-07-25 23:55:18.258353
d795ef40-8b02-411d-bf1f-8ffc0a136643	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Real-Time Chat System	Design a real-time messaging system similar to WhatsApp or Slack.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- One-on-one messaging and group chats (up to 1000 members)\n- Message delivery guarantees (sent, delivered, read receipts)\n- Online/offline presence indicators\n- Message history and search\n- File/image sharing\n- Push notifications for offline users\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- Real-time delivery (< 100ms for online users)\n- Message ordering guarantees within a conversation\n- Offline message queuing and delivery on reconnect\n- End-to-end encryption support\n- Scale to 500M daily active users\n\nConsider:\n- Connection management (WebSocket vs long polling vs SSE)\n- Message routing and fan-out for group chats\n- Storage strategy for messages (hot vs cold storage)\n- Presence system design at scale\n- How to handle network partitions and reconnections\n	- 500M daily active users, 50M concurrent connections\n- Message delivery < 100ms for online recipients\n- 40B messages per day\n- Message storage: indefinite for 1:1, configurable for groups\n- Max group size: 1000 members\n- File sharing: up to 100MB per file\n- 99.99% message delivery guarantee\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Real-Time Architecture", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Connection protocol choice", "Message routing", "Fan-out strategy for groups"]}, {"name": "Reliability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Delivery guarantees", "Ordering guarantees", "Offline handling"]}, {"name": "Scalability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Connection server scaling", "Message storage partitioning", "Presence at scale"]}, {"name": "Data Model", "weight": 0.15, "criteria": ["Message schema", "Conversation indexing", "Search infrastructure"]}, {"name": "Security", "weight": 0.1, "criteria": ["E2E encryption approach", "Authentication", "Data privacy"]}]}	\N	HARD	60	2026-07-25 23:55:18.261817
e18e1a40-bc5a-44ae-a9ba-0fbc32025c48	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a News Feed System	Design a social media news feed system similar to Facebook's or Twitter's timeline.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Users see posts from people they follow, ranked by relevance\n- Support text, image, video, and link posts\n- Real-time updates when new posts are published\n- Like, comment, and share interactions\n- Personalized ranking (not just chronological)\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- Feed generation < 200ms\n- Support users following thousands of accounts\n- Handle celebrity accounts (millions of followers)\n- Eventually consistent (new posts appear within seconds)\n- Scale to 1B users\n\nConsider:\n- Push vs pull model (fan-out on write vs fan-out on read)\n- Hybrid approach for celebrity accounts\n- Feed ranking and ML integration points\n- Caching strategy for feed pages\n- How to handle feed updates in real-time\n	- 1B total users, 500M DAU\n- Average user follows 200 accounts\n- Celebrity accounts: up to 100M followers\n- Feed generation latency < 200ms at p95\n- New post visible to followers within 5 seconds\n- Feed page size: 20 posts, support infinite scroll\n- 10M new posts per hour\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Feed Generation", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Push vs pull trade-off", "Hybrid approach for celebrities", "Feed assembly pipeline"]}, {"name": "Ranking", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Ranking signals", "ML integration", "Freshness vs relevance balance"]}, {"name": "Scalability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Fan-out strategy", "Storage partitioning", "Cache design"]}, {"name": "Real-Time Updates", "weight": 0.15, "criteria": ["New post propagation", "Feed invalidation", "Long-polling/SSE/WebSocket"]}, {"name": "Data Model", "weight": 0.1, "criteria": ["Post storage", "Social graph storage", "Feed cache structure"]}]}	\N	HARD	60	2026-07-25 23:55:18.264563
c98e2221-22c3-43a2-9818-d6299e47c9a2	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Distributed Key-Value Store	Design a distributed key-value store similar to DynamoDB or Cassandra.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Basic operations: put(key, value), get(key), delete(key)\n- Configurable consistency levels (ONE, QUORUM, ALL)\n- Automatic data partitioning across nodes\n- Support for TTL on keys\n- Range queries on keys (if using ordered partitioning)\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- High availability (AP system with tunable consistency)\n- Horizontal scalability (add nodes without downtime)\n- Automatic failure detection and recovery\n- Low latency reads and writes (< 10ms at p99)\n\nConsider:\n- Partitioning strategy (consistent hashing with virtual nodes)\n- Replication and consistency protocols\n- Conflict resolution (vector clocks, last-write-wins)\n- Failure detection (gossip protocol, phi accrual)\n- Read repair and anti-entropy mechanisms\n	- Store petabytes of data across thousands of nodes\n- Read/write latency < 10ms at p99 for quorum operations\n- Handle 1M operations per second per node\n- Survive up to 2 simultaneous node failures per replica group\n- Rebalancing on node add/remove within minutes\n- Value size: up to 1MB\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Partitioning", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Consistent hashing implementation", "Virtual nodes", "Rebalancing strategy"]}, {"name": "Replication & Consistency", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Replication factor", "Quorum protocol", "Conflict resolution"]}, {"name": "Failure Handling", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Failure detection", "Hinted handoff", "Read repair", "Anti-entropy"]}, {"name": "Storage Engine", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["LSM tree or B-tree choice", "Compaction strategy", "Memory management"]}]}	\N	MEDIUM	45	2026-07-25 23:55:18.267108
def5d0d0-16e4-4d6e-a29e-20a58ca861c0	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Notification System	Design a scalable notification system that supports multiple channels.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Support push notifications (iOS/Android), SMS, email, and in-app notifications\n- Template-based notification content with personalization\n- User preference management (opt-in/out per channel and category)\n- Notification scheduling (immediate and delayed)\n- Delivery tracking and analytics\n- Rate limiting per user to prevent notification fatigue\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- Process 10M notifications per hour\n- Delivery within 30 seconds for real-time notifications\n- At-least-once delivery guarantee\n- Graceful handling of third-party provider failures\n- Support for A/B testing notification content\n\nConsider:\n- Priority queues for urgent vs batch notifications\n- Provider abstraction for multi-channel delivery\n- Retry strategy with exponential backoff\n- Deduplication to prevent duplicate sends\n- How to handle provider rate limits and quotas\n	- 10M notifications per hour peak, 100M per day\n- Real-time notifications delivered within 30 seconds\n- Batch notifications processed within 1 hour window\n- 99.9% delivery rate (excluding user opt-outs)\n- Support 100+ notification templates\n- User base: 50M users with varying preferences\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Architecture", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Component separation", "Queue design", "Provider abstraction"]}, {"name": "Reliability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["At-least-once delivery", "Retry strategy", "Deduplication"]}, {"name": "Scalability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Horizontal scaling", "Priority handling", "Batch processing"]}, {"name": "User Experience", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Preference management", "Rate limiting", "Channel selection logic"]}]}	\N	MEDIUM	45	2026-07-25 23:55:18.269587
91efbf8d-c49b-47f8-86c3-39326f365bd7	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Search Autocomplete System	Design a search autocomplete (typeahead) system similar to Google's search suggestions.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Return top 5-10 suggestions as user types each character\n- Suggestions ranked by popularity, recency, and personalization\n- Support for trending queries (boost recent popular searches)\n- Multi-language support\n- Spell correction / fuzzy matching\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- Response time < 50ms (users expect instant feedback)\n- Handle 100K queries per second\n- Update suggestions based on new search data (near real-time)\n- Graceful degradation under load\n\nConsider:\n- Data structure for prefix matching (Trie vs inverted index)\n- How to rank and score suggestions\n- Caching strategy for common prefixes\n- How to update the suggestion index without downtime\n- Personalization without sacrificing latency\n	- Response latency < 50ms at p99\n- 100K autocomplete requests per second\n- Suggestion corpus: 5B unique queries\n- Update frequency: new trending queries reflected within 15 minutes\n- Storage: prefix tree for 5B queries (~100GB compressed)\n- Personalization: last 1000 searches per user\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Data Structure", "weight": 0.3, "criteria": ["Trie design or alternative", "Memory optimization", "Prefix matching efficiency"]}, {"name": "Ranking", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Scoring algorithm", "Trending detection", "Personalization approach"]}, {"name": "Performance", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Latency optimization", "Caching strategy", "Request fan-out"]}, {"name": "Data Pipeline", "weight": 0.2, "criteria": ["Index update mechanism", "Near real-time processing", "Offline vs online components"]}]}	\N	HARD	45	2026-07-25 23:55:18.271979
b2fad307-2e0b-434c-a56a-964c706e439e	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Distributed Cache	Design a distributed caching system similar to Redis Cluster or Memcached.\n\nFunctional Requirements:\n- Key-value storage with TTL support\n- Support for data structures (strings, lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes)\n- Atomic operations (increment, compare-and-swap)\n- Pub/sub messaging\n- Cluster management (add/remove nodes, rebalancing)\n- Persistence options (snapshot and append-only log)\n\nNon-Functional Requirements:\n- Sub-millisecond read/write latency\n- Linear scalability with node count\n- Automatic failover with minimal data loss\n- Memory-efficient storage\n- Support for 10M+ operations per second per node\n\nConsider:\n- Data partitioning across nodes (hash slots)\n- Replication for high availability\n- Client-side vs server-side routing\n- Memory management and eviction policies\n- Persistence vs pure in-memory trade-offs\n- How to handle hot keys\n	- Sub-millisecond latency (< 500μs at p99)\n- 10M operations per second per node\n- Cluster size: up to 1000 nodes\n- Memory per node: up to 256GB\n- Failover time: < 5 seconds\n- Data loss on failover: < 1 second of writes\n- Support keys up to 512 bytes, values up to 512MB\n	{"categories": [{"name": "Architecture", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Cluster topology", "Routing strategy", "Node communication protocol"]}, {"name": "Data Management", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Partitioning scheme", "Replication protocol", "Consistency guarantees"]}, {"name": "Performance", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Memory management", "Eviction policies", "Hot key handling", "IO model"]}, {"name": "Reliability", "weight": 0.25, "criteria": ["Failover mechanism", "Persistence strategy", "Split-brain handling"]}]}	\N	HARD	60	2026-07-25 23:55:18.274683
8215a336-c887-4488-9eea-73af1b2c1e71	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Design a URL Shortening Service	Design a URL shortening service similar to TinyURL or bit.ly that converts long URLs into short, shareable links.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Given a long URL, generate a unique shortened URL\n- Redirect users from the short URL to the original URL\n- Allow users to set custom short aliases\n- Track basic analytics (click count per link)\n- Support link expiration with configurable TTL\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Low-latency redirects (sub-50ms)\n- High availability for read path\n- Short URLs should be compact (6-8 characters)\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Short code generation strategy (hash-based vs counter-based)\n- Storage choice for URL mappings\n- Caching frequently accessed URLs\n- Handling collisions in short code generation\n	- 10M new URLs per month\n- 100:1 read-to-write ratio\n- 7-character codes using [a-zA-Z0-9] (3.5 trillion combinations)\n- Average URL record size: 500 bytes\n- 99.9% availability target\n	{"api_design": 25, "data_model": 25, "trade_offs": 10, "scalability": 20, "encoding_strategy": 20}	- API: POST /shorten {longUrl, customAlias?, ttl?} → shortUrl; GET /{code} → 301 redirect\n- Encoding: Base62 on auto-increment ID or MD5 hash truncated to 7 chars\n- Storage: Key-value store (DynamoDB/Redis) for fast lookups by short code\n- Cache: Redis/Memcached layer for hot URLs (LRU eviction)\n- Collision handling: Append random suffix and retry on hash collision\n- Expiration: TTL field checked on read; background cleanup job for expired entries\n- Analytics: Async write to separate analytics store on each redirect\n- Read path: Client → LB → Cache → DB → 301 redirect\n	EASY	25	2026-07-25 23:56:50.16104
6542ff4a-1c52-4b15-a99e-290fd1e43371	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Design Pastebin	Design a text-sharing service like Pastebin where users can store and share plain text or code snippets via a unique URL.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Users can create a paste with text content and get a unique URL\n- Pastes can be public or unlisted (accessible only via direct link)\n- Support syntax highlighting for common languages\n- Pastes expire after a configurable duration\n- Users can view raw text or formatted version\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Fast paste retrieval (sub-100ms)\n- Support large pastes (up to 10MB)\n- High read availability\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Object storage vs database for paste content\n- URL generation and uniqueness\n- Content delivery and caching\n- Metadata vs content separation\n	- 5M new pastes per day\n- Average paste size: 10KB, max: 10MB\n- 5:1 read-to-write ratio\n- Retention: 30 days default, permanent for registered users\n- 99.9% read availability\n	{"api_design": 20, "data_model": 25, "trade_offs": 10, "scalability": 20, "storage_design": 25}	- API: POST /pastes {content, language?, expiry?} → pasteUrl; GET /pastes/{id} → content\n- Storage: Metadata in relational DB; content in object store (S3) for large pastes\n- URL generation: Base62-encoded unique ID (6-8 chars)\n- Caching: CDN for popular pastes; application cache for recent pastes\n- Expiration: TTL stored in metadata; lazy deletion on access + periodic cleanup\n- Separation: Metadata (title, language, created_at, expiry) in DB; raw content in blob store\n- Read path: CDN → App Server → Cache → Object Store\n	EASY	20	2026-07-25 23:56:50.17204
4d4e075c-595c-4ca6-9b8b-f453482ca938	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Design a Parking Lot System	Design a system to manage a multi-floor parking lot with different vehicle types and real-time availability tracking.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Track available spots per floor and vehicle type (motorcycle, car, bus)\n- Assign nearest available spot when a vehicle enters\n- Calculate parking fee based on duration and vehicle type\n- Display real-time availability on entrance displays\n- Support monthly pass holders with reserved spots\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Real-time spot availability updates\n- Handle concurrent entry/exit at multiple gates\n- System remains functional if network to central server drops\n- Sub-second spot assignment\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Concurrency control for spot assignment\n- Data model for floors, spots, and vehicles\n- Pricing strategy implementation\n- Real-time availability broadcasting\n	- 5 floors, 500 spots per floor (2500 total)\n- Peak: 200 vehicles entering/exiting per hour\n- 4 entry gates, 4 exit gates operating concurrently\n- Fee calculation: per-hour rates varying by vehicle type\n- Display refresh: under 2 seconds\n	{"api_design": 20, "data_model": 30, "edge_cases": 10, "real_time_updates": 15, "concurrency_handling": 25}	- Data model: Floor → Spots (type, status, vehicle_id); Vehicle (plate, type, entry_time)\n- Spot assignment: Find nearest available spot of matching type using floor-priority queue\n- Concurrency: Optimistic locking on spot status (CAS operation) to prevent double-assignment\n- Pricing: Strategy pattern — HourlyRate, DailyMax, MonthlyPass with vehicle-type multipliers\n- Real-time: Publish spot changes to WebSocket/SSE for display boards\n- Entry flow: Scan plate → assign spot → raise gate → update availability\n- Exit flow: Scan plate → calculate fee → process payment → release spot → raise gate\n- Offline mode: Gates operate with local cache; sync when connectivity restores\n	EASY	25	2026-07-25 23:56:50.178503
b134aaa3-5390-4a65-9bed-c4ec8dc758af	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Design a Conference Room Booking System	Design a system for booking conference rooms in an office building, handling scheduling conflicts and resource management.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Users can search available rooms by time slot, capacity, and amenities\n- Book a room for a specific time range with attendee list\n- Cancel or modify existing bookings\n- Recurring meeting support (daily, weekly)\n- Conflict detection — prevent double-booking\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Strong consistency for booking operations (no double-books)\n- Fast availability queries (sub-200ms)\n- Support 1000 concurrent users checking availability\n- Calendar integration capability\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Conflict detection and prevention strategy\n- Recurring event storage and expansion\n- Search and filtering for available rooms\n- Notification system for booking changes\n	- 50 conference rooms across 3 buildings\n- 5000 employees, peak 500 concurrent booking requests\n- Booking granularity: 15-minute slots\n- Look-ahead window: 30 days\n- Average 200 bookings per day\n	{"api_design": 20, "data_model": 25, "recurring_events": 15, "query_performance": 15, "conflict_resolution": 25}	- Data model: Room (capacity, amenities, building); Booking (room_id, start, end, organizer, attendees, recurrence_rule)\n- Conflict detection: Check overlapping time ranges with SELECT ... WHERE room_id=? AND start < ? AND end > ?\n- Concurrency: Serializable isolation or advisory locks per room during booking\n- Recurring events: Store recurrence rule (RFC 5545); expand instances on query\n- Search: Index on (building, capacity, amenities) for filtered availability queries\n- API: GET /rooms/available?start=&end=&capacity=; POST /bookings; DELETE /bookings/{id}\n- Notifications: Event-driven — publish booking changes to notify attendees via email/Slack\n	EASY	25	2026-07-25 23:56:50.182963
62c7b404-bbc8-4e79-9498-b560474fb8ed	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016	Design an Online Presence Indicator	Design a system that tracks and displays user online/offline status in real-time, similar to the green dot in Slack or WhatsApp.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Show real-time online/offline/away status for each user\n- Detect user going offline (explicit logout or timeout)\n- Allow users to set custom status (Do Not Disturb, Away)\n- Friends/contacts can subscribe to status changes\n- Show "last seen" timestamp for offline users\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Status updates propagated within 5 seconds\n- Handle millions of concurrent connected users\n- Minimize server resource usage for idle connections\n- Graceful degradation under load\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Heartbeat mechanism for presence detection\n- Efficient fan-out of status changes to subscribers\n- Connection management at scale\n- Storage for presence state\n	- 50M concurrent users\n- Heartbeat interval: 30 seconds\n- Status change propagation: under 5 seconds\n- Average user has 200 contacts to notify\n- 99.9% accuracy for online/offline detection\n	{"data_model": 20, "trade_offs": 10, "scalability": 30, "fan_out_strategy": 15, "real_time_design": 25}	- Heartbeat: Client sends ping every 30s; server marks offline after 2 missed heartbeats\n- Connection: WebSocket for active users; long-polling fallback\n- Storage: Redis with TTL keys — user:{id}:presence with 60s TTL, refreshed on heartbeat\n- Fan-out: Publish status change to pub/sub channel; subscribers receive via WebSocket\n- Last seen: Update timestamp on each heartbeat; persist to DB on disconnect\n- Optimization: Batch status queries for contact list (MGET in Redis)\n- Custom status: Separate field in user presence record; manual override disables auto-detection\n- Scaling: Partition users across presence servers by user_id hash\n	EASY	20	2026-07-25 23:56:50.19347
e0131eb7-4a54-420e-8857-7b3c98bd9a4d	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Video View Counter	Design a scalable system that accurately counts video views for a platform like YouTube, handling millions of concurrent viewers.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Increment view count when a user watches a video for at least 30 seconds\n- Display approximate real-time view count on video page\n- Prevent duplicate counting from same user within a session\n- Provide exact counts for analytics dashboard (eventual consistency OK)\n- Support trending/popular video rankings based on view velocity\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Handle 1M+ concurrent viewers on viral videos\n- View count display can be slightly stale (30s delay acceptable)\n- No view should be permanently lost\n- Horizontal scalability for write path\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Write-heavy workload optimization\n- Approximate vs exact counting trade-offs\n- Deduplication strategies\n- Batch aggregation patterns\n	- 500M video views per day\n- Peak: 100K views/second on a single viral video\n- Deduplication window: 1 hour per user per video\n- Display staleness tolerance: 30 seconds\n- Count accuracy: 99.9% within 1 hour\n	{"data_model": 15, "trade_offs": 10, "scalability": 30, "deduplication": 20, "consistency_model": 25}	- Write path: View events → Kafka topic → Consumer aggregates in batches\n- Deduplication: Bloom filter or Redis HyperLogLog per video per hour window\n- Approximate count: Redis INCR for real-time display (fast, slightly lossy)\n- Exact count: Batch job aggregates from event log every few minutes\n- Hot videos: Dedicated counter shards — shard by video_id, merge on read\n- Display: Read from Redis approximate counter; refresh every 30s\n- Anti-fraud: Rate limit per IP; ignore views under 30s watch time\n- Storage: Event log in Kafka (retention 7d); aggregated counts in Cassandra\n	EASY	20	2026-07-25 23:56:50.200715
eadab786-e73b-44b5-979f-fb60b59b87e9	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Vending Machine System	Design the backend system for a network of smart vending machines that handles inventory, payments, and remote monitoring.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Process item selection and payment (card/mobile)\n- Track real-time inventory per machine per slot\n- Alert operators when stock is low or machine malfunctions\n- Support dynamic pricing (time-of-day, demand-based)\n- Remote configuration of product assignments to slots\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Machine must function during network outages (offline mode)\n- Payment processing under 3 seconds\n- Inventory sync within 5 minutes of reconnection\n- Support 10K machines reporting simultaneously\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Offline-first architecture\n- State synchronization after network partition\n- Payment processing flow\n- Monitoring and alerting pipeline\n	- 10,000 machines, 40 slots each\n- 50 transactions per machine per day average\n- Network connectivity: 95% uptime per machine\n- Inventory sync frequency: every 5 minutes when online\n- Payment timeout: 3 seconds max\n	{"api_design": 20, "data_model": 25, "monitoring": 10, "payment_flow": 20, "offline_handling": 25}	- Architecture: Machine (edge) ↔ Cloud Backend ↔ Operator Dashboard\n- Offline mode: Local state machine handles transactions; queues events for sync\n- Sync: On reconnect, push queued transactions and pull config/pricing updates\n- Payment: Machine → Payment Gateway → Backend confirms → Dispense item\n- Inventory: Decrement locally on dispense; batch sync to backend periodically\n- Alerts: Machine publishes health metrics; backend applies threshold rules\n- Data model: Machine (location, status); Slot (product_id, quantity, price); Transaction log\n- Conflict resolution: Last-write-wins for config; additive merge for transaction logs\n	EASY	20	2026-07-25 23:56:50.20459
b8767b8d-0e15-4595-817d-43e8d2f17f52	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Nested Comments System	Design a threaded comment system like Reddit where comments can be nested to arbitrary depth with voting and collapsing.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Users can post comments and reply to existing comments (unlimited nesting)\n- Upvote/downvote comments with score calculation\n- Sort comments by best, new, or controversial\n- Collapse/expand comment threads\n- Load nested replies lazily (show top 3, "load more" for rest)\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Fast initial page load (top-level + 2 levels deep)\n- Efficient tree traversal for deeply nested threads\n- Handle posts with 10K+ comments\n- Real-time vote count updates\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Tree data structure storage in relational/document DB\n- Efficient subtree retrieval queries\n- Sorting algorithms for comment ranking\n- Pagination within nested structures\n	- Posts can have up to 50K comments\n- Maximum nesting depth: 20 levels\n- Initial load: top 10 root comments with 2 levels of replies\n- Vote updates visible within 5 seconds\n- Page load time under 500ms for initial comments\n	{"api_design": 20, "data_model": 30, "pagination": 10, "query_efficiency": 25, "sorting_strategy": 15}	- Storage: Adjacency list (comment_id, parent_id, post_id, depth, path) in PostgreSQL\n- Materialized path: Store path as "1.5.12.34" for efficient subtree queries\n- Initial load: WHERE post_id=? AND depth<=2 ORDER BY score DESC LIMIT 10 per level\n- Lazy loading: GET /comments/{id}/replies?offset=3&limit=10\n- Voting: Redis for real-time scores; periodic flush to DB\n- Sorting: Wilson score interval for "best"; timestamp for "new"; |upvotes-downvotes| for controversial\n- API: POST /posts/{id}/comments; POST /comments/{id}/replies; PUT /comments/{id}/vote\n- Optimization: Denormalize reply_count and score on comment record\n	EASY	25	2026-07-25 23:56:50.207379
14f78aac-67c9-4e0b-982c-b0812d9d86b1	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Tagging Service	Design a generic tagging service that allows any entity (posts, products, users) to be tagged with labels for categorization and search.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Add/remove tags on any entity type\n- Search entities by one or more tags (AND/OR logic)\n- Autocomplete tag names as user types\n- Show tag popularity (usage count)\n- Support tag hierarchies (e.g., "programming/java")\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Tag search returns results in under 100ms\n- Support billions of entity-tag associations\n- Autocomplete responds in under 50ms\n- Consistent tag counts (eventually consistent OK)\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Many-to-many relationship modeling at scale\n- Full-text and prefix search for autocomplete\n- Inverted index for tag-based entity lookup\n- Tag normalization and deduplication\n	- 100M entities, average 5 tags each (500M associations)\n- 1M unique tags\n- Tag search: 10K queries per second\n- Autocomplete: 50K requests per second\n- Tag count accuracy: within 1% (eventual consistency)\n	{"api_design": 20, "data_model": 30, "scalability": 15, "autocomplete": 10, "search_design": 25}	- Data model: Tags (id, name, parent_id, count); EntityTags (entity_type, entity_id, tag_id)\n- Search: Inverted index — tag_id → list of entity_ids; intersect for AND, union for OR\n- Autocomplete: Trie or prefix index in Redis (ZRANGEBYLEX); precomputed top suggestions\n- Normalization: Lowercase, trim, deduplicate on insert\n- Hierarchy: Materialized path ("programming/java") or closure table\n- Counts: Increment/decrement on tag/untag; periodic recount job for drift correction\n- API: POST /entities/{type}/{id}/tags; GET /search?tags=a,b&op=AND; GET /tags/suggest?prefix=pro\n- Scaling: Partition entity-tag associations by entity_type; cache hot tags\n	EASY	20	2026-07-25 23:56:50.210163
