eduplication: Hash(user_id + template_id + params) checked in Redis with TTL window\n- Rate limiting: Per-user sliding window counter; defer low-priority if limit approaching\n- Retry: Exponential backoff (1s, 4s, 16s); dead-letter queue after max retries\n- Tracking: Log delivery status per notification; aggregate for analytics dashboard\n	MEDIUM	45	2026-07-25 23:56:50.247767
c7eb64c4-1c54-40e9-8218-10d3a77e5c4c	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Design a Task Scheduler	Design a distributed task scheduling system that executes jobs at specified times or intervals, similar to cron but scalable and fault-tolerant.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Schedule one-time tasks at a future timestamp\n- Schedule recurring tasks with cron expressions\n- Cancel or modify scheduled tasks\n- Retry failed tasks with configurable policy\n- Task execution history and status tracking\n- Support task dependencies (run B after A completes)\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Execute tasks within 1 second of scheduled time\n- Handle 1M scheduled tasks with 10K executing per minute\n- No duplicate execution (exactly-once semantics)\n- Survive node failures without losing scheduled tasks\n- Horizontal scaling of execution capacity\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Distributed scheduling without single point of failure\n- Exactly-once execution guarantees\n- Time-based partitioning and polling strategies\n- Worker pool management and task routing\n	- 1M active scheduled tasks\n- 10K task executions per minute at peak\n- Timing accuracy: within 1 second of scheduled time\n- Task payload: up to 10KB\n- Execution timeout: configurable, default 5 minutes\n- Retention: 30 days of execution history\n	{"api_design": 15, "scalability": 20, "exactly_once": 25, "fault_tolerance": 15, "scheduling_design": 25}	- Storage: Tasks in DB with (id, schedule, next_run_at, status, payload, retry_policy)\n- Polling: Workers poll for tasks WHERE next_run_at <= now() AND status = PENDING; claim via CAS\n- Exactly-once: Distributed lock per task (Redis SETNX with TTL); release on completion or timeout\n- Recurring: After execution, compute next_run_at from cron expression; update record\n- Partitioning: Shard tasks by next_run_at time bucket; assign buckets to scheduler nodes\n- Failure handling: Heartbeat from worker; if missed, mark task as failed; apply retry policy\n- Dependencies: DAG of task IDs; parent marks children as READY on completion\n- Scaling: Add worker nodes; rebalance time-bucket assignments via consistent hashing\n- API: POST /tasks {schedule, payload, retry}; DELETE /tasks/{id}; GET /tasks/{id}/history\n	MEDIUM	40	2026-07-25 23:56:50.252403
3dcbda9b-353f-49ba-9dd7-f3bc86096398	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design an E-commerce Service	Design the backend for an e-commerce platform handling product catalog, shopping cart, checkout, and order management at scale.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Product catalog with search, filtering, and categories\n- Shopping cart with add/remove/update quantities\n- Checkout flow with inventory reservation and payment processing\n- Order tracking with status updates\n- Flash sales with limited inventory (handle thundering herd)\n- Seller dashboard for inventory and order management\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Product search under 100ms\n- Cart operations under 50ms\n- Handle 100K concurrent checkouts during flash sales\n- Strong consistency for inventory (no overselling)\n- 99.99% availability for browse and cart\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Inventory management and reservation patterns\n- Handling flash sales and high-concurrency checkout\n- Payment processing and idempotency\n- Event-driven order state machine\n- Search and catalog service separation\n	- 50M products, 10M DAU\n- Flash sales: 100K concurrent checkout attempts for 1000 items\n- Cart: 200M active carts, average 5 items each\n- Orders: 2M per day, peak 50K/minute during sales\n- Payment: 99.99% success rate required\n- Search: 50K queries/second\n	{"data_model": 20, "scalability": 25, "payment_flow": 15, "flash_sale_handling": 15, "inventory_management": 25}	- Catalog: Elasticsearch for search; PostgreSQL for source of truth; CDC to sync\n- Cart: Redis hash per user (fast reads/writes); persist to DB on checkout\n- Inventory: Pessimistic lock on checkout — decrement with WHERE qty >= requested\n- Flash sale: Queue-based — accept into waiting queue; process sequentially; reject when sold out\n- Payment: Idempotency key per order; two-phase — reserve → confirm after fulfillment\n- Order state machine: CREATED → PAID → PROCESSING → SHIPPED → DELIVERED (event-driven)\n- Separation: Catalog Service, Cart Service, Order Service, Payment Service, Inventory Service\n- Flash sale optimization: Pre-warm inventory count in Redis; atomic decrement; async DB sync\n- API: GET /products/search; POST /cart/items; POST /checkout; GET /orders/{id}\n	MEDIUM	55	2026-07-25 23:56:50.258038
2da0a1a1-6684-4f9d-be56-d6c7e906e0a4	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design an Online Payment Service	Design a payment processing platform like Stripe that handles transactions between merchants and customers with multiple payment methods.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Process payments (credit card, bank transfer, digital wallets)\n- Support refunds (full and partial)\n- Merchant onboarding with KYC verification\n- Transaction ledger with double-entry bookkeeping\n- Webhook notifications for payment events\n- Multi-currency support with exchange rates\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Payment processing under 2 seconds end-to-end\n- 99.999% availability for payment API\n- Strong consistency for financial transactions (no double-charge)\n- PCI-DSS compliance for card data handling\n- Idempotent API to handle network retries safely\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Idempotency and exactly-once payment processing\n- Double-entry ledger design\n- Distributed transaction handling\n- Security and PCI compliance architecture\n- Reconciliation and settlement processes\n	- 10M transactions per day, peak 5K TPS\n- Transaction value: $0.50 to $50,000\n- Availability: 99.999% (5 minutes downtime/year)\n- Latency: payment confirmation under 2 seconds\n- Idempotency window: 24 hours\n- Reconciliation: daily settlement with 100% accuracy\n	{"security": 20, "api_design": 15, "scalability": 15, "ledger_design": 25, "consistency_guarantees": 25}	- Idempotency: Client provides idempotency_key; server deduplicates within 24h window\n- Ledger: Double-entry — every transaction creates debit + credit entries; sum always zero\n- Payment flow: Create intent → Authorize (hold funds) → Capture (settle) — two-phase\n- Refund: Reverse entries in ledger; initiate refund with payment processor\n- State machine: CREATED → AUTHORIZED → CAPTURED → SETTLED (or FAILED/REFUNDED)\n- Security: Tokenize card data immediately; store tokens not PANs; vault service for sensitive data\n- Multi-currency: Lock exchange rate at authorization time; settle in merchant's currency\n- Webhooks: Reliable delivery with retry; signing for authenticity; idempotent event IDs\n- Reconciliation: Nightly batch compares internal ledger with processor settlement reports\n	MEDIUM	50	2026-07-25 23:56:50.262588
3b547c93-4ab5-4801-abed-4a8203ebac49	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Distributed Counter	Design a distributed counting system that can handle millions of increments per second while providing accurate reads, used for likes, views, and inventory counts.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Increment/decrement a named counter by any amount\n- Read current counter value (exact or approximate)\n- Support millions of independent counters\n- Batch increment (update multiple counters atomically)\n- Counter reset and deletion\n- Historical counter values (time-series of changes)\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Write throughput: 1M increments/second per hot counter\n- Read latency under 10ms\n- No lost increments (durability guarantee)\n- Tunable consistency (strong for inventory, eventual for likes)\n- Horizontal scaling without coordination bottleneck\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Sharded counter pattern for write throughput\n- Consistency vs availability trade-offs for counters\n- Aggregation strategies (read-time merge vs periodic rollup)\n- Conflict-free replicated data types (CRDTs)\n	- 100M active counters\n- Hot counters: 1M writes/second (viral content)\n- Read latency: under 10ms for approximate, under 100ms for exact\n- Durability: zero lost increments after acknowledgment\n- Storage: 100 bytes per counter shard\n	{"trade_offs": 10, "scalability": 20, "read_aggregation": 15, "consistency_model": 25, "sharding_strategy": 30}	- Sharded counters: Split hot counter into N shards; writers pick random shard; readers sum all shards\n- Write path: Hash(counter_id + random) → shard; atomic increment on shard (Redis INCRBY)\n- Read path (approximate): Cache aggregated value with short TTL (5s); serve from cache\n- Read path (exact): Sum all shards on demand; more expensive but accurate\n- Durability: Write-ahead log before acknowledging; async flush to persistent store\n- Adaptive sharding: Monitor write rate; split shards when hot; merge when cold\n- CRDT approach: G-Counter (grow-only) per node; merge by taking max per node\n- Batch: Multi-key atomic increment via Redis pipeline or DB transaction\n- History: Append delta events to time-series store; rollup hourly/daily\n	MEDIUM	40	2026-07-25 23:56:50.26586
2ca79670-09a6-41fe-89fa-601263c4ddcd	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Distributed Unique ID Generator	Design a system that generates globally unique, roughly time-ordered IDs at high throughput across multiple data centers without coordination.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Generate unique 64-bit IDs (fits in a long/bigint)\n- IDs are roughly sortable by generation time\n- Support multiple data centers generating IDs independently\n- No single point of failure or coordination required\n- Expose as a service with batch ID generation support\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Generate 10K+ IDs per second per node\n- Zero collisions guaranteed (not probabilistic)\n- IDs should not leak internal infrastructure details\n- Sub-millisecond generation latency\n- Clock skew tolerance across nodes\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Snowflake ID structure and bit allocation\n- Clock synchronization and drift handling\n- Multi-datacenter coordination-free generation\n- Comparison with UUID, database sequences, and other approaches\n	- 64-bit ID space\n- 5 data centers, 100 nodes per data center\n- Generation rate: 10K IDs/ms per node (10M/second total)\n- Clock skew tolerance: up to 5ms between nodes\n- Zero collision guarantee over system lifetime\n- Rough time ordering (within same millisecond, order not guaranteed)\n	{"trade_offs": 10, "scalability": 20, "id_structure": 30, "clock_handling": 15, "uniqueness_guarantee": 25}	- Structure (Snowflake-style): [1 bit unused][41 bits timestamp][5 bits datacenter][5 bits machine][12 bits sequence]\n- Timestamp: Milliseconds since custom epoch; gives ~69 years of IDs\n- Sequence: 12 bits = 4096 IDs per millisecond per machine before waiting\n- Uniqueness: Combination of (timestamp + datacenter + machine + sequence) is globally unique\n- Clock skew: If clock moves backward, reject requests until caught up; or use logical clock\n- Allocation: Datacenter ID from config; machine ID from ZooKeeper lease or static assignment\n- Batch: Pre-generate batch of IDs; return array in single call for bulk inserts\n- Comparison: vs UUID (128-bit, not sortable); vs DB sequence (coordination required, SPOF)\n- API: GET /ids?count=100 → [id1, id2, ...]; single node, no network call needed for generation\n	MEDIUM	40	2026-07-25 23:56:50.268991
4ad46c83-c2d9-42d4-bb8a-8841ea57797f	a3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Design a Food Delivery Service	Design the backend for a food delivery platform like DoorDash or Uber Eats, handling restaurant discovery, ordering, real-time delivery tracking, and driver dispatch.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- Browse nearby restaurants with menus and ratings\n- Place orders with customization options\n- Real-time order status tracking (placed, preparing, picked up, en route, delivered)\n- Driver matching and dispatch optimization\n- ETA estimation for delivery\n- Rating system for restaurants and drivers\n\n### Non-Functional\n- Restaurant search under 200ms\n- Order placement under 1 second\n- Real-time location updates every 5 seconds\n- Handle dinner rush (10x normal traffic)\n- 99.9% order completion rate\n\n## Topics to Cover\n- Geospatial indexing for restaurant and driver discovery\n- Real-time location tracking and ETA computation\n- Driver dispatch and matching algorithms\n- Order state machine and event-driven architecture\n- Handling peak load and surge pricing\n	- 500K restaurants, 2M drivers, 50M users\n- Peak orders: 50K per minute during dinner rush\n- Driver location updates: 2M updates every 5 seconds\n- Delivery radius: 10km from restaurant\n- ETA accuracy: within 5 minutes of actual\n- Average delivery time: 30 minutes\n	{"scalability": 20, "order_management": 15, "geospatial_design": 25, "dispatch_algorithm": 25, "real_time_tracking": 15}	- Restaurant discovery: Geospatial index (PostGIS/Redis GEO) — find restaurants within radius of user\n- Driver matching: Find available drivers near restaurant; score by distance, rating, acceptance rate\n- Dispatch: Assign order to best driver; timeout after 30s → reassign to next best\n- Location tracking: Drivers push GPS every 5s → Kafka → update in Redis GEO; push to customer via WebSocket\n- ETA: Graph-based routing (OSRM) + ML model trained on historical delivery times\n- Order state: Event-sourced — PLACED → CONFIRMED → PREPARING → READY → PICKED_UP → DELIVERED\n- Peak handling: Queue orders; dynamic pricing to attract more drivers; pre-position drivers in hot zones\n- Services: Restaurant Service, Order Service, Driver Service, Location Service, Pricing Service\n- API: GET /restaurants?lat=&lng=&radius=; POST /orders; GET /orders/{id}/track; POST /drivers/{id}/location\n	MEDIUM	55	2026-07-25 23:56:50.272113
