COPY public.guide (id, topic_id, title, content_markdown, sort_order, estimated_minutes, created_at) FROM stdin;
ad2f35cd-da81-4dd6-9dbb-a8211d9208cc	a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012	Java Collections Framework Overview	# Java Collections Framework Overview\n\nThe Java Collections Framework provides a unified architecture for storing and manipulating groups of objects. Understanding the hierarchy and choosing the right implementation is critical for writing performant code.\n\n## Interface Hierarchy\n\n```\nIterable\n └── Collection\n      ├── List (ordered, allows duplicates)\n      ├── Set (no duplicates)\n      │    └── SortedSet → NavigableSet\n      └── Queue (FIFO or priority ordering)\n           └── Deque (double-ended)\n\nMap (key-value pairs, separate hierarchy)\n └── SortedMap → NavigableMap\n```\n\n## When to Use What\n\n### List Implementations\n\n| Implementation | Use When | Avoid When |\n|---------------|----------|------------|\n| **ArrayList** | Random access by index, iteration, appending | Frequent insert/remove in the middle |\n| **LinkedList** | Frequent add/remove at head/tail (as Deque) | Random access by index |\n| **CopyOnWriteArrayList** | Read-heavy, rarely modified, concurrent access | Frequent writes (copies entire array) |\n\n**Default choice:** `ArrayList` in ~95% of cases. Its cache-friendly contiguous memory layout outperforms LinkedList even for middle insertions up to several thousand elements.\n\n### Set Implementations\n\n| Implementation | Use When | Ordering |\n|---------------|----------|----------|\n| **HashSet** | Fast contains/add/remove, no ordering needed | None |\n| **LinkedHashSet** | Need insertion-order iteration | Insertion order |\n| **TreeSet** | Need sorted iteration or range queries | Natural/Comparator order |\n| **EnumSet** | Set of enum constants | Enum declaration order |\n\n### Map Implementations\n\n| Implementation | Use When | Ordering |\n|---------------|----------|----------|\n| **HashMap** | General purpose key-value storage | None |\n| **LinkedHashMap** | Iteration in insertion/access order, LRU caches | Insertion or access order |\n| **TreeMap** | Sorted keys, range queries (subMap, headMap) | Natural/Comparator order |\n| **EnumMap** | Enum keys (extremely fast, array-backed) | Enum declaration order |\n| **ConcurrentHashMap** | Thread-safe map with high concurrency | Single-threaded (slight overhead) |\n\n### Queue/Deque Implementations\n\n| Implementation | Use When |\n|---------------|----------|\n| **ArrayDeque** | Stack (LIFO) or Queue (FIFO), faster than Stack/LinkedList |\n| **PriorityQueue** | Elements processed by priority (min-heap) |\n| **LinkedBlockingQueue** | Producer-consumer with blocking |\n| **ConcurrentLinkedQueue** | Non-blocking concurrent queue |\n\n## Performance Characteristics\n\n### Time Complexity Summary\n\n| Operation | ArrayList | LinkedList | HashSet | TreeSet |\n|-----------|-----------|------------|---------|---------|\n| get(index) | O(1) | O(n) | N/A | N/A |\n| add(end) | O(1)* | O(1) | O(1)* | O(log n) |\n| add(middle) | O(n) | O(1)** | N/A | N/A |\n| contains | O(n) | O(n) | O(1)* | O(log n) |\n| remove | O(n) | O(1)** | O(1)* | O(log n) |\n\n*amortized, **if you already have a reference to the node\n\n### HashMap Key Considerations\n\n- Always override both `hashCode()` and `equals()` for custom key objects\n- Immutable keys are safest (changing a key's hash after insertion makes it unfindable)\n- Load factor 0.75 balances space and time (resize at 75% capacity)\n- Initial capacity should be set if the number of elements is known to avoid repeated resizing\n\n## Choosing the Right Collection: Decision Flowchart\n\n1. **Need key-value pairs?** → Use a Map implementation\n2. **Need unique elements?** → Use a Set implementation\n3. **Need ordered sequence?** → Use a List implementation\n4. **Need FIFO/LIFO/priority?** → Use a Queue/Deque\n\nThen within each category:\n- **Need thread safety?** → Use concurrent variant (ConcurrentHashMap, CopyOnWriteArrayList)\n- **Need sorting?** → Use Tree variant (TreeMap, TreeSet)\n- **Need insertion order?** → Use Linked variant (LinkedHashMap, LinkedHashSet)\n- **Otherwise** → Use the Hash/Array variant (HashMap, HashSet, ArrayList)\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- **Modifying a collection during iteration** throws ConcurrentModificationException. Use `Iterator.remove()` or collect items to remove separately.\n- **Using mutable objects as Map keys** can cause lost entries if the hash changes after insertion.\n- **Autoboxing overhead** with primitive-heavy code: consider specialized libraries (Eclipse Collections, HPPC) for `int`/`long` collections.\n- **Collections.unmodifiableList()** is a view, not a copy. Modifications to the original list are visible through the wrapper.\n	1	12	2026-07-25 00:08:06.725725
8970469d-4438-45a6-ba1d-c3bf9cc27d95	a2000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011	Dependency Injection in Spring Boot	# Dependency Injection in Spring Boot\n\nDependency Injection (DI) is the core mechanism Spring uses to manage object creation and wiring. Instead of objects creating their own dependencies, the Spring IoC container injects them.\n\n## Constructor Injection (Recommended)\n\nConstructor injection is the preferred approach in modern Spring applications:\n\n```java\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;\n    private final InventoryService inventoryService;\n\n    // Spring auto-injects dependencies via constructor\n    // @Autowired is optional for single-constructor classes (Spring 4.3+)\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway paymentGateway, InventoryService inventoryService) {\n        this.paymentGateway = paymentGateway;\n        this.inventoryService = inventoryService;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Dependencies can be `final` (immutable after construction)\n- Object is never in a partially-initialized state\n- Easy to test: just pass mocks via constructor, no Spring context needed\n- Makes excessive dependencies obvious (too many constructor params = class does too much)\n- Prevents circular dependencies at startup (fails fast with clear error)\n\n## Field Injection (Antipattern)\n\n```java\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    @Autowired\n    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway; // hidden dependency\n}\n```\n\n**Why it's an antipattern:**\n- Hides dependencies from the class API\n- Cannot make fields `final`\n- Requires reflection or a full Spring context for testing\n- Allows the class to be instantiated in an invalid state\n- Circular dependencies are detected late (or not at all)\n\nThe Spring team officially recommends constructor injection as of Spring Framework 4.x.\n\n## @Qualifier for Disambiguation\n\nWhen multiple beans of the same type exist, use `@Qualifier` to specify which one:\n\n```java\n@Configuration\npublic class DataSourceConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @Qualifier("primary")\n    public DataSource primaryDataSource() {\n        return DataSourceBuilder.create().url("jdbc:postgresql://primary:5432/db").build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    @Qualifier("replica")\n    public DataSource replicaDataSource() {\n        return DataSourceBuilder.create().url("jdbc:postgresql://replica:5432/db").build();\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class ReportService {\n    public ReportService(@Qualifier("replica") DataSource dataSource) {\n        // uses the replica for read-heavy reports\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## @Primary as Default\n\n`@Primary` marks a bean as the default choice when no `@Qualifier` is specified:\n\n```java\n@Bean\n@Primary\npublic ObjectMapper defaultMapper() {\n    return new ObjectMapper();\n}\n\n@Bean\n@Qualifier("yaml")\npublic ObjectMapper yamlMapper() {\n    return new ObjectMapper(new YAMLFactory());\n}\n```\n\nAny injection point requesting `ObjectMapper` without a qualifier gets the `@Primary` bean. Explicit `@Qualifier("yaml")` overrides the primary selection.\n\n## Circular Dependencies\n\nA circular dependency occurs when Bean A depends on Bean B, which depends on Bean A. With constructor injection, Spring detects this immediately at startup:\n\n```\nBeanCurrentlyInCreationException: Error creating bean 'serviceA':\nRequested bean is currently in creation: Is there an unresolvable circular reference?\n```\n\n**Solutions:**\n1. **Redesign** (best): Extract shared logic into a third service\n2. **@Lazy on one dependency**: Injects a proxy that resolves the real bean on first use\n3. **Event-driven**: Use `ApplicationEventPublisher` to decouple\n4. **@PostConstruct setter**: Inject one dependency after construction (avoid if possible)\n\n```java\n// @Lazy breaks the cycle by injecting a proxy\n@Service\npublic class ServiceA {\n    public ServiceA(@Lazy ServiceB serviceB) {\n        this.serviceB = serviceB;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Best Practices Summary\n\n| Practice | Recommendation |\n|----------|---------------|\n| Injection style | Constructor injection always |\n| Field visibility | `private final` for all dependencies |\n| @Autowired annotation | Omit on single-constructor classes |\n| Multiple implementations | Use @Qualifier or @Primary |\n| Circular dependencies | Redesign to eliminate them |\n| Optional dependencies | Use `Optional<T>` parameter or `@Nullable` |\n	1	15	2026-07-25 00:08:06.771439
\.
