y to find the maximum sum of a subarray. It keeps track of the maximum sum seen so far and the current sum of the subarray.\n\n### Follow-up: Divide and Conquer Approach\nHere is a suggested implementation of the divide and conquer approach:\n\n```java\npublic class MaxSubarraySum {\n    public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {\n        return maxSubArray(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);\n    }\n\n    private int maxSubArray(int[] nums, int left, int right) {\n        if (left == right) {\n            return nums[left];\n        }\n\n        int mid = (left + right) / 2;\n        int leftMax = maxSubArray(nums, left, mid);\n        int rightMax = maxSubArray(nums, mid + 1, right);\n        int crossMax = maxCrossingSum(nums, left, mid, right);\n\n        return Math.max(Math.max(leftMax, rightMax), crossMax);\n    }\n\n    private int maxCrossingSum(int[] nums, int left, int mid, int right) {\n        int sum = 0;\n        int maxSoFar = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n\n        for (int i = mid; i >= left; i--) {\n            sum += nums[i];\n            maxSoFar = Math.max(maxSoFar, sum);\n        }\n\n        sum = 0;\n        for (int i = mid + 1; i <= right; i++) {\n            sum += nums[i];\n            maxSoFar = Math.max(maxSoFar, sum);\n        }\n\n        return maxSoFar;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis implementation uses a divide and conquer approach to find the maximum sum of a subarray. It recursively divides the input array into two halves and finds the maximum sum of each half, as well as the maximum sum of the crossing subarray. It then returns the maximum of these three values.	\N	2026-07-26 01:52:30.892704
